PVD("india_ndp_2012-2017",{"docs":["","IND NDP 2012 17"],"s":{"1":[[1,"53","2","Financial Inclusion Expansion through Cooperatives and Banking Correspondents","They should be given increased prominence during Twelfth Plan because potential benefits and cost of inaction are both very high.",""],[1,"92","2","Microfinance Regulatory Framework","The Microfinance Regulation Act which has been introduced in Parliament will establish a regulatory framework which would allay suspicions and allow the industry to develop unhindered with due regula- tory oversight.",""],[1,"92","2","SHG and Collective Guarantee Credit Schemes","This is where SHGs and similar collective guarantee credit schemes have a big role to play.",""],[1,"92","2","Microfinance Support for Small Credit","Microfinance institutions are another vehicle.",""],[1,"92","2","Mezzanine Financial Agencies for Small Credit","We do need other kinds of mezzanine financial agencies-SHG, microfinance, coopera- tive-which permit the banks an easier way to fund the capital needs of small creditors.",""],[1,"92","3","Aadhaar-Based Financial Access Reform","The Aadhar number must become a passport for ordinary people to be able to use the savings and payments facilities of our banking system, or for that matter, all other regulated savings products-mutual funds, insurance and so on.",""]],"2":[[1,"6","4","Cabinet Committee on Investment","A Cabinet Committee on Investment has been established to deal with situations where clearances are unduly delayed and this has succeeded in removing many bottlenecks.",""],[1,"14","4","Ease of Doing Business Reform","Industrial growth can be greatly helped by policies aimed at improving the ease of doing business, and also, policies that would encourage a flow of both risk capital and debt into small and medium industries.",""],[1,"35","4","Tax Issue Resolution for Investment Climate Improvement","This calls for urgent action to tackle implementation constraints in infrastructure which are holding up large projects, combined with action to deal with tax related issues which have created uncertainty in the investment climate.",""],[1,"38","2","Business Regulatory Environment Improvement","This can be done by improving the business regulatory environment, strengthening the governance capacity of States, investing more in infrastructure rather than subsidies, and by using Science and Technology (S&T) to drive innovation.",""],[1,"64","2","Economic Reform Continuation for Private Sector Efficiency","the main instrument available for improved efficiency of resource use is to continue economic reforms, which increase competitive pressure in the system and give producers the flexibility and freedom they need to upgrade technology and expand capacity.",""],[1,"65","3","Transparent Allocation of Scarce Natural Resources Mechanism","The process of extending transparent policies and mechanisms to allocation of scarce natural resoruces to private com- panies for commercial purposes has also been initi- ated.",""],[1,"66","3","Business Regulatory Environment Reform Initiatives","The Plan proposes some initiatives to tune up India's business regulatory environment.",""],[1,"98","10","Procedural Simplification and Human Interface Reduction","Endeavour to reduce transaction cost through procedural simplification and reduction of human interface.",""]],"3":[[1,"291","8","Industrial Research and Innovation Promotion Measures","The thrust of the Department of Scientific & Industrial Research (DSIR) is to promote industrial research, technology development and transfer to enable India to emerge as a global industrial research and innovation hub.",""],[1,"291","8","Enabling Environment for New Innovations","Emphasis is on attracting industrial research in the country through industry and institution-centric motivational measures and incentives, creating an enabling environment for development of new innovations to channelise benefits to the people.",""],[1,"292","2","Science and Engineering Leadership Initiative","The Twelfth Five Year Plan of CSIR focuses on achieving science and engineering leadership; developing innovative technological solutions; practising open innovation initiatives; developing and nurturing human resource in trans-disciplinary areas; facilitating science-based entrepreneurship; and enabling socio-economic transformation through appropriate S&T intervention.",""],[1,"292","2","Innovative Technology Solutions Development","The Twelfth Five Year Plan of CSIR focuses on achieving science and engineering leadership; developing innovative technological solutions; practising open innovation initiatives; developing and nurturing human resource in trans-disciplinary areas; facilitating science-based entrepreneurship; and enabling socio-economic transformation through appropriate S&T intervention.",""],[1,"292","2","Open Innovation Initiative","The Twelfth Five Year Plan of CSIR focuses on achieving science and engineering leadership; developing innovative technological solutions; practising open innovation initiatives; developing and nurturing human resource in trans-disciplinary areas; facilitating science-based entrepreneurship; and enabling socio-economic transformation through appropriate S&T intervention.",""],[1,"292","2","Science-Based Entrepreneurship Promotion","The Twelfth Five Year Plan of CSIR focuses on achieving science and engineering leadership; developing innovative technological solutions; practising open innovation initiatives; developing and nur- turing human resource in trans-disciplinary areas; facilitating science-based entrepreneurship; and enabling socio-economic transformation through appropriate S&T intervention.",""],[1,"292","4","PRISM Support for Innovative Proposals from MSMEs","Promoting Innovations in Individuals, Start-ups and MSMEs (PRISM)-wherein innovative proposals of MSMEs shall be supported;",""],[1,"292","4","Support for CSIR Cluster Innovation Centres","CSIR- Cluster Innovation Centres (CICs) promoted by National Innovation Council shall be supported for providing innovative solutions;",""],[1,"292","4","Expansion of TePP Outreach Centres Network","existing network of TePP Outreach Centres shall be expanded;",""],[1,"292","5","Technopreneur Promotion Programme Support","supported and support shall be extended to approved Technopreneur Promotion Programme (TePP) projects, spilling over from the Eleventh Five Year Plan;",""],[1,"292","5","Patent Acquisition and Collaborative Research Support Scheme","Scheme on Patent Acquisition and Collaborative Research and Technology Development (PACE)- wherein support shall be provided to Indian industries to acquire Intellectual Property at early stage from overseas or within the country and add value to the acquired IP;",""],[1,"292","5","Industry-University Collaborative Research PPPs","focus shall be on PPPs to create enabling environment for collaborative research between Industry and Universities/Public Funded Research Institutions;",""],[1,"292","5","Industrial R&D and Common Research Facilities Support","Building Industrial R&D and Common Research Facilities (BIRD)-wherein R&D in Industry shall be encouraged and supported; and support shall be provided for creation of Common Research Facilities for Small and Micro Industries;",""],[1,"292","5","Access to Knowledge for Technology Development and Dissemination","Access to Knowledge for Technology Development and Dissemination (A2K+)-wherein science-, technology- and innovation-related international journals from major publishers shall be made accessible to 1,500 in-house R&D units of industry and 600 Scientific & Industrial Research Organisations (SIROs) and techno-entrepreneurs, besides conducting studies/conferences on industrial status in the country;",""],[1,"292","5","Technology Development and Demonstration Programme Support","support shall be extended to Technology Development and Demonstration Programme (TDDP) projects, spilling over from the Eleventh Five Year Plan.",""],[1,"292","6","National Competency Development Programme","The important activities envisaged during the Twelfth Five Year Plan period would include Consultancy Promotion, Services, Research and Analysis, National Programme for Competency Development in strategic focus areas, Technology Delivery Transfer and Commercialisation, National Knowledge Depository, Training and Development, Export promotion and International Collaborations.",""],[1,"292","6","Technology Transfer and Commercialisation Initiative","The important activities envisaged during the Twelfth Five Year Plan period would include Consultancy Promotion, Services, Research and Analysis, National Programme for Competency Development in strategic focus areas, Technology Delivery Transfer and Commercialisation, National Knowledge Depository, Training and Development, Export promotion and International Collaborations.",""],[1,"292","6","National Knowledge Depository","The important activities envisaged during the Twelfth Five Year Plan period would include Consultancy Promotion, Services, Research and Analysis, National Programme for Competency Development in strategic focus areas, Technology Delivery Transfer and Commercialisation, National Knowledge Depository, Training and Development, Export promotion and International Collaborations.",""],[1,"292","6","Training and Development Programme","The important activities envisaged during the Twelfth Five Year Plan period would include Consultancy Promotion, Services, Research and Analysis, National Programme for Competency Development in strategic focus areas, Technology Delivery Transfer and Commercialisation, National Knowledge Depository, Training and Development, Export promotion and International Collaborations.",""],[1,"292","6","Export Promotion Initiative","The important activities envisaged during the Twelfth Five Year Plan period would include Consultancy Promotion, Services, Research and Analysis, National Programme for Competency Development in strategic focus areas, Technology Delivery Transfer and Commercialisation, National Knowledge Depository, Training and Development, Export promotion and International Collaborations.",""],[1,"295","2","Programme for Inspiring Inventors and Innovators","The focus of NRDC during the Twelfth Five Year Plan period will be on launching (i) Programme for Inspiring Inventors and Innovators (PIII) and (ii) Programme for Development of Technologies for Commercialisation.",""],[1,"295","2","Programme for Development of Technologies for Commercialisation","The focus of NRDC during the Twelfth Five Year Plan period will be on launching (i) Programme for Inspiring Inventors and Innovators (PIII) and (ii) Programme for Development of Technologies for Commercialisation.",""],[1,"295","8","Scale-up and Validation of Leads Initiative","CSIR has proposed to upgrade an activity for scale-up and validation of leads towards product/process development into an independent initiative.",""],[1,"296","5","Innovation Complexes and Translational Research Strengthening","Innovation Complexes: CSIR during the Mid Term Appraisal of the Eleventh Five Year Plan had resolved to bolster its translational research",""],[1,"296","6","Establishment of Innovation Complexes","capability through establishment of Innovation Complexes at identified locations across the coun- try.",""],[1,"296","8","Open Innovation Platform Expansion","Open Innovation has been identified as a major platform during the Twelfth Plan.",""],[1,"297","8","Intellectual Property Portfolio Consolidation","During the Twelfth Plan period, the efforts to consolidate this IP portfolio further would be continued.",""],[1,"297","14","Early-Stage Knowledge and Intellectual Property Acquisition for Portfolio Building","Acquisition of early-stage relevant knowledge/IP for portfolio building.",""],[1,"308","16","Centre-State Technology Partnership Programme","Centre-State Technology partnerships-At least five viable partnerships through programme support, PPP for R&D.",""],[1,"308","16","National Challenge Area Mega PPP","One mega PPP for national challenge area, five PPPs for large-scale challenge, 25 PPPs for proof of concepts for technology solutions.",""],[1,"308","17","Technology Development and Transfer Programme","Technology Development and Transfer, IDP, IS-STAC through the ongoing programmes, 200 technolo- gies demonstrations, 150 proof of concepts, and 25 cooperative investments with other socio-economic Ministries have been targetted.",""],[1,"309","1","Nano Science and Technology Mission Start-up Company Creation","Under Nano S&T Mission-25 start-up companies under PPP models would be created.",""],[1,"309","2","PPP for R&D Challenge Areas","PPP for R&D-One mega PPP for national challenge areas and five PPPs for large-scale challenge and also 25 PPPs for proof of concepts for technology solutions.",""],[1,"309","3","Science for Equity, Employment and Development Projects","Hundred projects for Science for Equity, Employment and Development and 50 Model Demonstration Projects.",""],[1,"309","8","Grand Challenge Programmes in Health Care and Agriculture","Launching of eight Grand Challenge programmes in health care and agriculture on a mission mode around national priorities in development sectors through bottom-up approach and discovery-led innovation, interdepartmental participation and separate government and management structure.",""],[1,"309","9","Bio-Cluster Establishment and Commissioning Programme","Establishment and commissioning of three ongoing bio-clusters at Faridabad, Mohali and Bengaluru and two new bio-clusters with clusters boards to govern, and establish incubators, common technology plat- forms, contract labs for SMEs, genetically modified products (GMP) facilities, research hospital and so on.",""],[1,"309","11","Biotechnology Regulatory System Strengthening","Strengthening of regulatory system for biotechnology through establishment of BRAI, under the act of Parliament and establishment/strengthening of 10 regulatory testing laboratories with good laboratory practice (GLP) standard.",""],[1,"309","13","BIRAC Establishment and Operationalisation","Establishment and operationalisation of BIRAC",""],[1,"309","13","Public-Private Partnership Schemes for Biotechnology Innovation","launching of two to three new PPP schemes such as ignition grants, start-up grants, shared technology incubators and bio-parks.",""],[1,"310","8","Cluster Innovation Centre Establishment and MSME Innovation Support","Establishment of 40 CICs; support to 1,200 plus innovative proposals from MSME Clusters; acquisition of around 20 Globally Patented Technologies by Indian Industries and value addition.",""],[1,"310","13","Spin-off Company Establishment Initiative","Setting up 15 spin-off companies.",""],[1,"310","21","NMITLI New Project and Product Development Initiative","Under NMITLI, the target is to launch five to seven new projects per year; launch some unique products such as Micro PCR (a platform technology for diagnostic applications), dental implants benefiting Indian masses, next generation clutch plates and so on.",""],[1,"310","24","Grand Challenge-Driven Innovation Projects","Launching of three Grand Challenge-driven projects with global participation.",""],[1,"310","24","Participatory Technology Development and Transfer","Develop at least five tech- nologies in participatory mode and transfer the same to stakeholders.",""],[1,"310","24","CSIR Centres for Collaborative Research","Set up at least five CSIR Centres for Collaborative Research with academia, R&D institutions and industry.",""],[1,"313","3","National Innovation Ecosystem Collaboration Strategy","Therefore, the national strategies for innovation need to focus on various types of institutions in the ecosystem and aim for more effective collaboration amongst them.",""],[1,"313","4","Innovation Ecosystem Strengthening Policy Package","Government has a critical role to play in strengthening the innovation ecosystem.",""],[1,"313","4","Knowledge Infrastructure Strengthening","It must provide the enabling policy interventions, strengthen knowledge infrastructure, improve inter-institutional collaborations, provide a mechanism for funding business innovations at all levels especially small and medium scale enterprises (SMEs) and provide vision through a national-level road map for innovations.",""],[1,"313","4","Inter-Institutional Collaboration Enhancement","It must provide the enabling policy interventions, strengthen knowledge infrastructure, improve inter-institutional collaborations, provide a mechanism for funding business innovations at all levels especially small and medium scale enterprises (SMEs) and provide vision through a national-level road map for innovations.",""],[1,"313","4","Business Innovation Funding Mechanism for SMEs","It must provide the enabling policy interventions, strengthen knowledge infrastructure, improve inter-institutional collaborations, provide a mechanism for funding business innovations at all levels especially small and medium scale enterprises (SMEs) and provide vision through a national-level road map for innovations.",""],[1,"313","6","National Innovation Ecosystem and Inclusive Innovation Initiatives","NInC's initiatives are also aimed at fostering an innovation eco- system across domains and sectors to strengthen entrepreneurship and growth, and to facilitate the birth of new ideas.",""],[1,"314","2","Industry Innovation Cluster Initiative","Recognising this need, NInC has envisioned the Industry Innovation Cluster initiative.",""],[1,"314","3","Cluster Innovation Centre Initiative","The focal point of this initiative would be the creation of a Cluster Innovation Centre (CIC).",""],[1,"314","9","National Innovation Promotion Service","Creation of a National Innovation Promotion Service to replace/add to National Service Scheme in colleges to use college students to identify local innovations.","Ministry of Youth Affairs and Sports | Ministry of Human Resource Development (HRD)"],[1,"315","6","Innovation Ecosystem Information Sharing Platform","Therefore, the strengthening of the innovation ecosystem requires a platform for information sharing and dissemination.",""],[1,"315","6","India Innovation Portal Development","the NInC has developed the India Innovation Portal to enable easy access to these as well as to become a wider information repository on innovation and a platform for collaboration as well.","NInC"],[1,"316","1","State Innovation Councils","NInC is facilitating the setting up of State Innovation Councils in each State.",""],[1,"316","2","Sectoral Innovation Councils","NInC is also encouraging the setting up of Sectoral Innovation Councils aligned to Union Government Ministries to promote innovation eco- systems across sectors and domains.","Union Government Ministries"],[1,"316","3","Innovation Awards and Challenge Programme","To induce a culture of innovation in the coun- try, there is a need to offer encouragement through awards and challenges which mobilise people to engage and respond creatively and bring focus on neglected societal challenges.",""],[1,"316","3","Inclusive Innovation Challenge Setting Initiative","The NInC is also seeking to set chal- lenges for the Indian imagination to incentivize the citizens to come up with solutions, especially those that relate to inclusive innovation.",""],[1,"316","4","International Collaboration Platforms for Innovation and Research","NInC is also focused on facilitating and leveraging platforms for international collaboration for driving innovation and multidisciplinary research.",""],[1,"316","8","Grand Challenge Awards for Innovation","NInC is also working on several other ideas such as announcing 10 Grand Challenge Awards to leverage public imagination for innovative solutions in critical areas.",""],[1,"316","8","Knowledge City Development in Kerala","It is also looking at promotion of projects that create an innovation dividend like the setting up a Knowledge City in Kerala.",""],[1,"316","8","Innovation and Entrepreneurship Draft Policy Development","Further, NInC is also working towards creating draft policies on innovation and entrepreneurship",""],[1,"317","1","Institutionalize Innovation Thinking in Policy-Making","to institutionalise innovation thinking into policy- making for providing the requisite stimulus from the Central Government.",""],[1,"317","2","Innovation Ecosystem Development and Enterprise Form Innovation","The Planning Commission is examining changes that would facilitate the multiplication of more such enterprises.",""],[1,"317","3","Intellectual Property Rights Management Strengthening","India must improve its management of IPR.","Department of Industrial Policy and Promotion (DIPP)"],[1,"317","5","Intellectual Property Rights Management and Reform","Therefore, as India aims to become amongst the global leaders in innovation, it will also have to be amongst the leaders in efficient manage- ment of IPR and innovations in IPR concepts and policies.",""],[1,"318","6","Innovation Agenda Mainstreaming in Cabinet Proposals","the Cabinet Secretariat issued orders to have the agenda of innovation embedded in all proposals to the Cabinet where action on innovation is reported specifically in each proposal to the Cabinet.","Office of the Prime Minister | Cabinet Secretariat"]],"4":[[1,"66","4","Physical Infrastructure Improvement Strategy","strategies for improving infrastructure are a core of the Plan",""],[1,"66","5","Manufacturing Sector Technology Upgrading Strategy","Strategies are required to induce more depth and value-addition in India's manufactur- ing sector that are not 'protectionist' and that leverage FDI and are compatible with an open global trade regime.",""],[1,"98","5","Domestic Manufacturing Support for Export Inputs","Encourage domestic manufacturing for inputs to export industry and reduce the dependence on imports.",""],[1,"98","6","Export Technology Upgradation Initiative","Promote technological up-gradation of exports to retain a competitive edge in global markets.",""]],"5":[[1,"26","23","Capacity Building for Industrial Pollution Management Project","Capacity Building for Industrial Pollution Management project",""],[1,"98","9","Incentives for Green Goods Manufacturing","Provide incentives for manufacturing of green goods recognising the imperative of building capacities for environmental sustainability.",""],[1,"148","9","Green Technology Development and Adoption Support","The primary objective of developing green technology is to replace the obsolete and inefficient systems with more energy-efficient and clean technologies.",""],[1,"148","9","MSME Green Technology Financing Support","Funds need to be made available to assist the industry adopt green technologies within their own premises, and also for building common environmental protection infrastructure within the industrial clusters.",""],[1,"148","9","Industrial Cluster Environmental Protection Infrastructure","Funds need to be made available to assist the industry adopt green technologies within their own premises, and also for building common environmental protection infrastructure within the industrial clusters.",""],[1,"148","10","Green Technology Investment Vehicle","This fund can be used to establish a focused investment vehicle for companies investing in green technology, and environmentally supportive businesses such as renewable energy, green transport, and water and waste management among others.",""],[1,"150","2","Environmentally Sound Technology Diffusion and Transfer","It is also important to facilitate diffusion and transfer of environmentally sound technologies and management techniques, which are a key element of any sustainable development strategy.",""],[1,"150","5","Eco-Industrial Park Green Design and Cleaner Production Program","Components of this approach include green design of the park infrastructure (new or retrofitted); cleaner",""],[1,"150","6","Conversion of Special Economic Zones into Eco-Industrial Hubs","we should consider converting our Special Economic Zones (SEZ) and townships along the Mumbai-Delhi Industrial Corridor into Eco- industrial hubs as outlined above.",""],[1,"158","11","Iron and Steel Industry Policy Framework Development","Ministry of Steel and Department of Industrial Policy and Promotion need to work together and evolve a suitable policy framework so that progress along the above dimensions is incentivised to improve the efficiency of iron and steel industry in our country.","Ministry of Steel | Department of Industrial Policy and Promotion"],[1,"159","12","Cement Industry Policy Framework Development","Department of Industrial Policy and Promotion needs to evolve a suitable policy framework to incen- tivise full realisation of the potential offered by the above measures in the cement industry.","Department of Industrial Policy and Promotion"]],"6":[[1,"12","1","Manufacturing Growth and MSME Expansion Strategy","The Twelfth Plan therefore seeks to achieve a faster growth in manufacturing, with particular emphasis on the medium, small and micro enterprises, which provide the best scope for absorbing labour currently employed in low productivity occupations.",""],[1,"14","4","SME Risk Capital and Debt Financing Policy","Industrial growth can be greatly helped by policies aimed at improving the ease of doing business, and also, policies that would encourage a flow of both risk capital and debt into small and medium industries.",""],[1,"39","1","Entrepreneurship and MSME Growth Support","The objective must be to stimulate new entrepreneurship while enabling existing MSMEs, including in agriculture, to invest more and grow faster.",""],[1,"39","1","MSME Infrastructure and Affordable Credit Access","For this, we need to meet their needs for infrastructure and for easier, cheaper and faster access to capital.",""],[1,"39","3","Centre Support for SME-Led Growth Framework","The Centre's contributions would lie mainly in creating the appropriate macroeconomic framework, financial sector policies and national level infrastructure.",""],[1,"53","5","Venture Capital and Angel Investment Promotion","In a knowledge economy, we need to do much more to encourage the growth of venture capital funds and angel investors.",""],[1,"92","5","Venture Capital and Private Equity Finance Expansion","To increase access to equity-especially for small businesses- venture capital, private equity finance and similar",""],[1,"92","6","Regulatory Encouragement for Equity Providers to Small Businesses","Regulatory encouragement to the providers of equity to small business is therefore essential.",""]],"7":[[1,"16","1","Implementation Capacity Building Fund Allocation","The Twelfth Plan, therefore, provides for a portion of the funds in many programmes to be used to build implementation capacity.",""],[1,"16","1","Rajiv Gandhi Panchayat Shashaktikaran Abhiyan","A new Centrally Sponsored Scheme for strengthening the capacity of the Panchayats, the Rajiv Gandhi Panchayat Shashaktikaran Abhiyan has also been introduced.",""],[1,"36","5","Capacity Building and Governance Reform for Public Sector Accountability","it is necessary to focus on capacity building and governance reforms, including system change that will increase accountability in the public sec- tor.",""],[1,"38","2","State Governance Capacity Strengthening","This can be done by improving the business regulatory environment, strengthening the governance capacity of States, investing more in infrastructure rather than subsidies, and by using Science and Technology (S&T) to drive innovation.",""],[1,"40","7","State Planning and Implementation Capacity Strengthening","This will require strengthening of States' own capacities to plan, to implement and to bring greater synergies within their own administration and with the Central Government.",""],[1,"48","3","Institutional Capacity Development for Governance Bodies","The Twelfth Plan also needs to focus on developing the capabilities of our institutions to perform the increasingly complex and demand- ing tasks expected of them.",""],[1,"321","4","Rajiv Gandhi Panchayat Sashaktikaran Abhiyan","the increase from the second year of the Twelfth Plan, the first year being devoted to strengthening the capacities of the States and the Ministry to absorb these additional funds will be deployed for the Rajiv Gandhi Panchayat Sashaktikaran Abhiyan meant for strengthening human resource and systems capacities of PRIs.","Ministry of Panchayati Raj"],[1,"326","1","Local-Level Capacity Building","The Twelfth Plan must address capacity building at the local level as a key instrument for improving outcomes.",""],[1,"326","2","Pooled Capacity-Building Fund for Local Bodies","A pooled fund across programmes should be created from which resources could be drawn for capacity building.",""],[1,"326","3","Professionalisation of Capacity-Building Institutions","They need to be thoroughly professionalised",""],[1,"326","3","Civil Society Partnership for Capacity Building","They need to be thoroughly professionalised and also need to develop powerful partnerships with carefully selected civil society organisations which have a commendable track-record in this sphere.",""],[1,"326","3","Public-Private Partnership Upgrading of Government Institutes","A number of institutes of the government, which have otherwise limited staff can be upgraded by entering into public-private partnership (PPP) and thus, strengthen the excellence of the faculty as well as, the quality of training imparted.",""],[1,"326","4","Fast-Track Hiring Policy for External Talent","recruitment from the private sector and hiring of external consultants through a fast track process needs to be enabled by an appropriate hiring policy.",""],[1,"326","5","Capacity Building Provisions for ULBs and PRIs","the Twelfth Plan would make adequate provisions in each Plan programme that would serve the requirements of capacity building at the cutting-edge level of implementation.",""],[1,"354","12","Panchayat and Municipality Governance Capacity Building","strengthen, to this end Panchayat- and Municipality-level governance with more appropriate capacity building, to facilitate participatory planning, decision-making, implementation and monitoring, to reflect local felt needs;",""],[1,"354","13","Professional Support for Local Body Planning and Monitoring","provide professional support to local bodies for planning, implementation and monitoring their plans;",""],[1,"354","14","Panchayat Capacity and Service Delivery Improvement","improve the performance and delivery of critical functions assigned to Panchayats, and counter possible efficiency and equity losses on account of inadequate local capacity.",""],[1,"362","9","PRI Capacity Building","capacity building of PRIs, in terms of both human resources capacities and systems of implementation;",""],[1,"364","1","Rajiv Gandhi Panchayat Sashaktikaran Abhiyan","The RGPSA seeks to enhance capacities and effectiveness of Gram Panchayats and the Gram Sabhas by strengthening the institutional structure for knowledge creation and capacity building and by providing them necessary human resource and infrastructure support.",""],[1,"364","1","Performance-Linked Funding for Panchayati Raj","RGPSA will provide performance-linked funds from 2014-15 onwards.",""],[1,"364","1","State Performance-Linked Scheme Funds","Twenty per cent scheme funds will be tied to State performance on identified deliverables in the State Plan.",""]],"8":[[1,"334","5","Disaster Risk Management Mainstreaming and Preparedness Strategy","The Plan prescribed a strategy of disaster management which included three important components: first, policy guidelines on preparation of developmental plans across sectors for integrating disaster management into developmental plans and a specific scheme; second, a multi-pronged strategy for the total risk management and third, recognition of a need for plan expenditure on disaster management and preventive measures in addition to calamity relief fund.",""],[1,"334","5","National Disaster Management Authority Establishment","In pursuance of this strategy, the National Disaster Management Authority was proposed under National Disaster Management Act 2005.",""],[1,"334","7","Early Warning Systems in Hazard-Prone Areas","First, set- ting up of early warning systems in all hazards prone areas of the country would need a special focus.",""],[1,"334","7","Disaster Risk Reduction for Multiple Hazards","It is proposed that disaster risk reduction in respect of earthquake, flood, drought, Tsunami, cyclones, for- est fire, chemical nuclear and biological disasters be set up.",""],[1,"334","7","Disaster Communication Systems","Effective communication systems have to be set up in all the levels to ensure timely and accurate dissemination of warning signals to vulnerable communities.",""],[1,"334","7","Disaster Risk Technology Application Initiative","Necessary innovative technologies and their application would need to be accordingly taken up in the plan.",""],[1,"334","8","Disaster Risk Reduction Mainstreaming in Development Schemes","Second, mainstreaming disaster risk reduction in all major schemes would need to be an important area of focus.",""],[1,"335","1","Mainstream Disaster Risk Reduction in Major Schemes","Disaster risk reduction will need to be thus incorporated in all major schemes, specifically the flagship schemes, for reducing the vulnerability in the hazards prone areas of the country.",""],[1,"335","1","Earthquake-Resilient School Building Safety","For example, safety of the school buildings, especially in earthquake prone areas has to be ensured.",""],[1,"335","2","Community Disaster Awareness and Capacity Building","It is important that community awareness and capacity building within the community and government is undertaken at all the three levels: National, State and Districts including villages.",""]],"9":[[1,"57","4","New Water Resources Legislative Framework","It is also necessary to consider whether a new legislative framework is necessary to help manage our water resources better.",""],[1,"57","5","National Framework Law for Water Governance","Centre, the States and the local governance institu- tions needs to be developed.",""],[1,"57","6","Groundwater Protection and Regulation Model Legislation","New model legislation is needed for protection, conservation, management and regulation of groundwater.",""],[1,"179","11","New Water Legal and Institutional Framework","A new legal and institutional framework for water based on broader consensus among the States.",""],[1,"191","2","Strengthening Groundwater Governance Coordination and CGWB Mandate","Effective management of groundwater requires changes in the nature of coordination among the government ministries related to groundwater ... These agencies must be required to assess the impact of their decisions on groundwater and report to CGWB, on issues concerning groundwater.",""],[1,"191","3","Groundwater Impact Assessment Protocol Development","CGWB may develop protocols for conducting assessment of impact of major (industrial/urban/hydrological) interventions on groundwater and strengthen its own internal capacities to widen its scope of work.",""],[1,"191","7","Energy-Efficient Agricultural Power Subsidy Delivery Investments","Major investments will be required in this direction in the Twelfth Plan.",""],[1,"191","8","Sustainable Groundwater Irrigation Development","During the Twelfth Plan sustainable groundwater irrigation development will be promoted in 11 Eastern Sates including the seven North-Eastern States,23 in order to more fully realise the potential of this region to contribute to the needs of national food security, even while ensuring that this intensification of groundwater use does not lead to the same deterioration in water table and water quality that has been experienced in other parts of India.",""],[1,"208","2","National Water Commission for Irrigation Compliance Monitoring","During the Twelfth Plan, there is also a proposal to set up a National Water Commission (NWC) to monitor compliance with conditionali- ties of investment and environment clearances given to irrigation projects.","Union Ministry of Water Resources"],[1,"208","3","Water Conflict Resolution Regulatory Framework","We need to evolve an institutional framework backed by a legal regime that facilitates setting up of regulatory bodies that would enable resolution of water conflicts.",""],[1,"208","3","Transparent Water Entitlements and Pricing Process","This demands a transparent and participatory process of determination of entitlements and prices.",""],[1,"208","3","State Water Regulatory Authority Establishment","Again to ensure sustainability and meet environmental needs, a regulatory authority is a must in each State.",""],[1,"209","1","State Water Regulatory Governance Framework","The bill proposes a separation of the authority to make 'political' or 'normative' decisions and the authority to make 'technical' or 'predominantly non-normative' decisions.",""],[1,"209","2","SIWEA Regulatory and Enforcement Body","The SIWEA will be a multi-disciplinary body of independent professionals from civil engineering, ecology/environmental science, economics, accounts and auditing, sociology/political science and geology/hydrogeology.",""],[1,"209","3","Stakeholder Committee for APPRC Review and Approval","The SC will deliberate on the drafts of the APPRC prepared by SIWEA and provide considered comments and suggestions, which will be incorporated by SIWEA and re-presented to SC for final approval.",""],[1,"209","7","Integrated Project and Programme Execution","Execution of Projects and Programmes, including techno-economic, socio-cultural, environmental and ecological aspects of project and programme design, including adherence to the integrated state water plan;",""],[1,"209","11","Private Sector Participation in Water Provisioning","Private Sector Participation, including criteria determining the details of tariff recovery in case of private water provisioning, norms for project- level purchases related to equipment, establish- ment, and other aspects of project management, specification of sectoral responsibilities to be handed over to private parties and so on;",""],[1,"209","12","Integrated State Water Plan Preparation","Preparation of Integrated State Water Plan (ISWP);",""],[1,"210","2","Modular Water Sector Governance Bill","The Bill also builds in enough flexibility in its design to take care of differences across States through a modular structure, from which modules based on the state-specific situation, requirements, priorities of water sector governance, and other factors could be selected by the state government while preparing and enacting their final draft of the Bill.",""],[1,"210","3","New Legal Framework for Groundwater Management","this requires a new legal framework to support efforts in this direction.",""],[1,"211","13","Groundwater Use Regulation and Allocation Control","Regulate and control iniquitous groundwater use and distribution, based on priority of allocation",""],[1,"212","2","Groundwater Extraction Regulation","Regulate the over-extraction of groundwater in order to ensure the sustainability of groundwater resources, equity of their use and distribution, and to ensure fulfilment of ecosystem needs;",""],[1,"212","3","Community-Based Groundwater Management Mechanisms","Promote and protect community-based, participatory mechanisms of groundwater management that are adapted to specific locations;",""],[1,"212","4","Groundwater Contamination Prevention and Mitigation","Prevent and mitigate contamination of ground- water resources;",""],[1,"212","5","Conservation, Recharge, and Management Practices","Promote and protect good conservation, augmen- tation (recharge) and management practices;",""],[1,"212","6","Groundwater Protection and Land Use Control","Protect areas of land that are crucial for the sustainable management of groundwater resources and ensure that high groundwater consuming activities are not located in areas unable to support them.",""],[1,"212","13","Maharashtra Groundwater Development and Management Act","Proposed Maharashtra Groundwater (Develop- ment and Management) Act, 2009.",""],[1,"213","5","National Water Law","the need for a national water law becomes imperative.",""],[1,"213","10","National Water Law Enactment","A national water law is, therefore, necessary to make the tenets of such a consensual statement justiciable.",""],[1,"214","3","Framework Law Justiciability and Judicial Review Mechanism","the laws passed and the executive actions taken by the Central and State Governments and the devolved functions exercised by PRIs will have to conform to the general principles and priorities laid down in the framework law, and that deviations can be challenged in a court of law.",""],[1,"214","4","Water Law Incorporation of Supreme Court Principles","The law will incorporate all major legal pronouncements by the Supreme Court with reference to water such as the Public Trust Doctrine and the recognition of the fundamental right to water as also the principle of subsidiarity, as explicated in the 73rd and 74th Constitutional amendments, the prevention and precautionary principles, most recently statutorily recognised in the National Green Tribunal Act, 2010 and the transparency principles of The Right to Information Act, 2005.",""],[1,"214","7","National Water Framework Law Drafting and Finalisation","In July 2012, the Ministry of Water Resources constituted a Committee under the Chairmanship of Dr. Y.K. Alagh, former Member, Planning Commission to draft a National Water Framework Law.","Ministry of Water Resources | Union Minister of Water Resources"],[1,"224","5","Groundwater Use Regulation Model Bill","The Model Bill is built around the need to regulate unreasonable use of sources of groundwater that threaten the aquifer to ensure that the resource (aquifer) itself is protected and can provide a sustainable basis for meeting the basic needs of every person for decades to come.",""],[1,"258","5","Comprehensive Water Resources Management Package","(i) development of a comprehensive water database and assessment of the impact of climate change on water resources, (ii) promotion of citizen and state actions for water conservation, augmentation and preservation, (iii) focused attention on vulnerable areas including over-exploited areas, (iv) increas- ing water use efficiency by 20 per cent, and (v) pro- motion of basin level integrated water resources management.",""],[1,"258","6","Hydrological Mapping and Water Resources Framework","During the Twelfth Plan, it should be our effort to create a framework which enables the mapping of hydrological units with the hierarchy of river basin at the highest level to catchment at the intermedi- ate and watershed at the lowest level.",""],[1,"258","6","Water Scenario Formulation and Adaptation Assessment","Formulation of such framework including development of pres- ent and future scenarios can be undertaken by line Department(s) in consultation with the academic and research organisations working in this area.",""],[1,"258","6","Integrated Water Utilisation Assessment","Assessment of water utilisation for various purposes linked to sources is a basic necessity for working out the adaptation measures.",""],[1,"258","6","National Water Mission Revamp","During the Twelfth Plan the National Water Mission would be revamped to ensure an integrated management of ground and surface water resources.",""],[1,"261","5","Water Mission for Conservation and Equitable Distribution","The Water Mission aims at conservation of water, minimising wastage and ensuring more equi- table distribution both across and within states.",""],[1,"261","5","National Program for Sustainable Water Resources Management","A new national program will be launched for sustainable management of water resources in the country.",""],[1,"262","5","Merge Water Mission into the New National Water Mission","The Water Mission here needs to be merged with the new National Water Mission that is being formulated for the Twelfth Five Year Plan.",""],[1,"263","19","Hydrological Unit Mapping and Water Use Management Research","Framework for mapping hydrological units, assessment of water utilisation to address inter-sectoral competition, research to support policy improvements in water use management and to improve understanding of linkages within the ecosystem.",""]],"10":[[1,"47","7","Integrated Water Supply and Sanitation Planning","it is, therefore, necessary to adopt a habitation approach to sanitation and to institutionalise the integration of water supply with sanitation in each habitation.",""],[1,"47","7","Integrated Urban Water, Toilet, and Sewerage Planning","Urban development must give top priority to planning for water, toilets and sewerage as an integrated whole taking into account the likely expansion of the urban population.",""],[1,"179","5","Integrated Urban Water Supply and Sewage Systems","All urban water supply projects to necessarily integrate sewage systems within them.",""],[1,"195","12","Water and Waste Management Investment in Human Settlements","The Twelfth Plan will focus on the need to invest in water and waste management in human settlements based on a strategy that is both affordable and sustainable.",""],[1,"197","4","Affordable and Scalable Sewage Network Investment","it is equally and even more critical that we invest in building affordable and scalable sewage networks, which requires a fresh look at the current technology for sewage and its treatment.",""],[1,"198","4","Community Sanitation and Toilet Management Initiative","mobilising women in the slums in self-help groups (SHGs) and launching an awareness campaign on sanitation through training and building/renovation of community toilets and child-friendly toilets in the slums, which would be managed by the women of the community on a pay-and- use basis",""],[1,"200","3","Affordable Water Supply for All","Plan to supply water at affordable costs to all",""],[1,"200","6","Sewage and Water Supply Investment Priority","Invest on sewage first and water supply next",""],[1,"200","7","Sewage System Cost Reduction","Reduce costs on sewage systems so that invest- ment can reach all",""],[1,"200","15","Mandatory Sewage Component for Water Schemes","No Water Scheme Will be Sanctioned without a Sewage Component",""],[1,"200","16","Sewage Infrastructure Expansion and Planning","Investment in sewage must match the investment in water supply.",""],[1,"201","2","Regional Drinking Water Supply and Wastewater Treatment Planning","Thus, a regional planning approach for provision of drinking water supply and wastewater treatment and disposal is necessary to meet needs of both rural and urban areas and avoid duplication of schemes.",""],[1,"239","11","National Ganga River Basin Authority","A National Ganga River Basin Authority (NGRBA) has been set up to ensure effective abatement of pollution and conservation of the river Ganga by adopting a holistic approach with the river basin as the unit of planning.",""],[1,"244","4","River Basin Conservation Legal Framework and Management Strategy","Ensure ecological flows in all rivers by regulating abstractions so as to allow conservation of riverine ecosystems through developing a legal framework and management strategy for conservation of river basins.",""],[1,"244","5","Treated Sewage Recycling and Reuse in Urban Projects","Promotion of recycling and reuse of treated sewage in urban projects such as sanitation, landscaping, central air conditioning and so on.",""],[1,"251","3","Completion of Ongoing River and Lake Conservation Works","Concerted efforts would be made to complete the ongoing work of the Eleventh Plan under National River Conservation Plan (NRCP)/National Ganga River Basin Authority (NGRBA)/National Lake Conservation Plan (NLCP).",""],[1,"251","4","Sewage Treatment Capacity Enhancement Funding","additional fund for enhancement of sewage treat- ment capacity need to be made available either under the JNNURM/UIDSSMT and/or under NRCP.",""],[1,"251","4","Urban Local Body Capacity Strengthening for Sewage Treatment","Technical and financial capacity of ULBs will also have to be suitably augmented for meeting both the capital and Operations & Maintenance (O&M) requirements of Sewage Treatment Plants (STPs).",""],[1,"251","4","State Sewerage Infrastructure Financing and Maintenance Allocation","States are also required to earmark allocations and mobilise necessary resources for funding sewerage infrastructure and their maintenance.",""],[1,"251","5","Integrated National Wetland and Lake Conservation Scheme (NPCA)","Merger of National Wetland Conservation Programme (NWCP) and NLCP schemes into one integrated scheme entitled NPCA recommended by Expenditure Finance Committee is proposed with effect from Twelfth Plan period.","National River Conservation Directorate | MoEF"],[1,"251","6","Ganga River Basin Treatment Strategy","The NGRBA has proposed a river basin treatment strategy which includes augmentation and sustenance of ecological flow of the river and its tributaries.",""],[1,"251","8","National Environmental Monitoring Programme","There is a need to set up a unified National Environmental Monitoring Programme NEMP",""],[1,"262","5","Separate Monitoring of Sewage Treatment as a Climate Adaptation Policy Thrust","critical actions like treatment of all sewage being released into water bodies, which have a bearing on our adaptive capacity to climate change, should be monitored as a separate policy thrust area under the Prime Minister's Council on Climate Change.",""]],"11":[[1,"14","4","Water Efficiency Regulation and Pricing Policy","The Plan emphasises the need to design policies regard- ing the efficient use of water through a combination of regulation and appropriate pricing.",""],[1,"14","4","Public Investment for Water Conservation","These policies will have to be supported by well designed public investment to encourage effective conservation of water.",""],[1,"57","3","Aquifer Mapping Programme","An aquifer mapping programme that would enable more informed participatory management and better alignment of cropping patterns to water availability across the country will need to be the starting point of our efforts.",""],[1,"57","3","Massive Groundwater Recharge Programme","This must be combined with a massive groundwater recharge programme based on integrating a reformulated MGNREGS with programmes on watershed development and restoration of water bodies.",""]],"12":[[1,"179","3","Watershed Restoration and Groundwater Recharge Programme","A massive programme for watershed restoration and groundwater recharge must be launched by transforming Mahatma Gandhi National Rural Employment Guarantee Act (MGNREGA) into our largest watershed programme,3 giving renewed energy to the reformed Integrated Watershed Management Programme launched in the Eleventh Five Year Plan and launching a completely revamped programme on Repair, Renovation and Restoration (RRR) of Water Bodies.",""],[1,"192","7","Completion of Ongoing DDP, DPAP and IWDP Projects","a major part of the Eleventh Plan was occupied in completion of a large number of ongoing projects under DDP, DPAP and IWDP",""],[1,"193","4","Revision of Common Guidelines for Watershed Development Projects","The Ministry of Rural Development constituted a Committee under the Chairmanship of Dr. Mihir Shah, Member Planning Commission to revisit the Common Guidelines for Watershed Development Projects to provide necessary flexibility within the Guidelines and to ensure momentum to IWMP, even while strengthening its innovative features.","Ministry of Rural Development"],[1,"193","5","Integrated Watershed Management Programme Five-Year Implementation","In order to provide greater momentum to the programme and avoid thin spreading of resources, it has been decided to make the IWMP a five-year programme.",""],[1,"193","6","Professional Human Resources Deployment Allocation","Hence a special allocation of 10 per cent of the total project cost has been proposed for deployment of professional human resources.",""],[1,"193","7","National Capacity Building Strategy","A new national strategy for capacity building has been unveiled, since this was a key requirement of the programme that needed much greater direction and momentum.",""],[1,"193","8","Institution Building Support Provision","Hence, a special provision has been made for this activity and guidelines issued to facilitate the same.",""],[1,"193","9","Civil Society Participation Guidelines for the Watershed Programme","The new Guidelines seek to provide further scope and facilitation for civil society participation in the programme.",""],[1,"193","10","Ridge-to-Valley Watershed Programme","A watershed programme must follow the ridge-to-valley principle since the ridge is the catchment of streams and water bodies in the lower reaches.",""],[1,"193","12","Expeditious Fund Release Procedures","To overcome avoidable delays in the progress of work, a new set of expeditious fund release procedures are out- lined in these Guidelines.",""],[1,"193","13","Central Level Nodal Agency Establishment","A strong professionally managed CLNA is now being set up with major facilitating responsibilities elaborated in these Guidelines.",""],[1,"193","13","Redefinition of the NRAA Role","The role of the NRAA is also being suitably redefined in line with the recommendations of the Working Group for the Twelfth Five Year Plan",""],[1,"194","2","Convergence Framework for IWMP with Allied Programmes","a new framework is proposed for convergence of IWMP with allied programmes such as MGNREGA, NRLM, RKVY and so on.",""],[1,"194","3","Forest Land Work Facilitation Guidelines","These Guidelines proffer a frame- work within which this work can be facilitated.",""],[1,"194","6","Water Bodies Repair, Renovation and Restoration Scheme Overhaul","the Twelfth Plan proposes a major overhaul of this scheme based on the lessons learnt so far as also drawing upon exemplary work done in some parts of the county by civil society organisations such as the DHAN Foundation in Tamil Nadu.",""],[1,"194","9","Command Area Development for Water Bodies","developing the command areas that are served by these water bodies",""],[1,"194","10","RRR Scheme Integrated Watershed and Command Area Development","To realise its full potential, the RRR scheme must combine work that is generally done in separate silos of watershed treatment and command area development.",""],[1,"194","10","RRR Scheme Institution-Building and Stakeholder Participation","It needs also to absorb the central lesson of both these schemes, that it is not merely engineering but institution-building that must equally take centre-stage.",""],[1,"194","11","Convergence of RRR Projects with the IWMP","The objective must be to converge all RRR projects with the IWMP in such a way that the treatment of the catchment of the water bodies to be restored occurs paripassu with, if not prior to, the repair and renovation of the water body itself.",""],[1,"194","12","Gram Panchayat Water Users' Association Strengthening","Water Users' Association (WUA) at the Gram Panchayat Level, which would plan and participate in the implementation of renovation, pisciculture, tree planting and command area development works, as also maintenance and management, including water distribution and conflict-resolution across uses and users.",""],[1,"194","13","Cascade Association Water Infrastructure Maintenance","The CAs will be responsible for renovation, cleaning and excavation of feeder channels and repairs to diversion weirs/regulators on feeder channels.",""],[1,"194","13","Cascade Water Sharing Conflict Resolution Mechanism","They will also help resolve conflicts across WUAs within the cascade on water sharing and maintenance responsibilities.",""],[1,"195","1","Block-Level WUA Federation Support Mechanism","WUA Federations at the Block level, which will help mobilise funds for rehabilitation of water bodies from various sources, organise train- ing programmes for WUAs, monitor O&M of rehabilitated systems as also the performance of WUAs and CAs.",""],[1,"195","2","Repair, Renovation and Restoration Scheme for Water Bodies","During the Twelfth Plan, RRR will cover all water bodies with ayacuts of 20 ha to 2000 ha.",""],[1,"195","10","RRR Scheme Steering Committee","A Steering Committee under the aegis of the Planning Commission and the Ministry of Water Resources, headed by an experienced and renowned professional in the field, will be responsible for preparing the detailed Operational Guidelines and sanctioning projects under the RRR scheme.","Planning Commission | Ministry of Water Resources"]],"13":[[1,"50","3","Power Distribution Sector Financial Strengthening","Equally important is the need to address the financial weakness of the distribution segment of the power sector.",""],[1,"61","6","Power Distribution Company Debt Restructuring Plan","A debt restructuring plan, in which State Governments take over a large part of the burden of paying back the debt has been approved by the Cabinet and must be implemented by all the affected Sates.",""],[1,"61","6","Bank Loan Restructuring for Power Sector Debt","The commercial banks will have to bear part of the burden by restructuring the loans, and the Reserve Bank of India (RBI) may have to allow some regulatory forbearance relieving the banks of treating the restructured loans as non-performing assets (NPA) and making suitable provisions for them.",""],[1,"61","6","Discom Operational Viability Restoration Measures","As envisaged in the package, these steps must be combined with credible steps on the part of the State Governments and the discoms to ensure restoration of the operational viability of the discoms in future.",""],[1,"61","6","Open Access Implementation in the Power Sector","An early implementation of open access would help create an environment that would promote efficiency and competitiveness.",""],[1,"69","18","Universal Village Electrification and Loss Reduction Programme","Provide electricity to all villages and reduce AT&C losses to 20 per cent by the end of Twelfth Five Year Plan.",""],[1,"129","4","Private Sector Participation in Electricity Transmission and Distribution","To create Transmission Super Highways, the government has allowed private sector participation in the transmission sector.",""],[1,"129","4","Model Framework for Private Participation in Electricity Distribution","a model framework is being developed by the Planning Commission.",""]],"14":[[1,"149","2","Perform, Achieve and Trade Scheme","The Perform, Achieve and Trade (PAT) scheme is an example of a certificate based trading scheme promoting energy efficiency.",""],[1,"149","2","Renewable Energy Certificate Mechanism","Similarly, Renewable Energy Certificate (REC) mechanism is a market-based instrument introduced to promote renewable energy, and facilitate renew- able purchase obligations, which legally mandate a percentage of electricity to be procured by distribu- tion companies from renewable energy sources.",""],[1,"159","13","Perform-Achieve-Trade Energy Efficiency Mechanism","Perform-Achieve-Trade (PAT) is a market- based mechanism under the National Mission for Enhanced Energy Efficiency (NMEEE), under the Prime Minister's National Action Plan for Climate Change (NAPCC).","Ministry of Power (MoP)"],[1,"160","5","Energy Savings Certificate Issuance Process","The process to issue energy savings certificates (ESCerts) to those DCs who achieve SEC lower than the specified value.",""],[1,"160","8","PAT Cycle Monitoring, Verification, and Energy Saving Certificate Trading","The Monitoring and Verification (M&V) is carried out from the second year onwards. After the completion of M&V, energy saving certifi- cates will be issued and trading will be permitted.",""],[1,"161","5","National Mission on Enhanced Energy Efficiency","The existing National Mission on Enhanced Energy Efficiency (NMEEE) has been designed to deal with energy efficiency and emission",""],[1,"161","8","Energy Efficiency Risk Insurance and Financing Fund","What is needed is a mechanism to insure risk and assure finance on reasonable terms.",""],[1,"161","9","Combined Energy Efficiency Package","it would be useful to envisage a combined Energy Efficiency Package-consisting of the PAT scheme and an Energy Conservation Fund, to be imple- mented by a unified Central Government agency, namely the Bureau of Energy Efficiency (BEE).","Bureau of Energy Efficiency (BEE)"],[1,"162","2","Carbon Emissions Trading Scheme","It sets a steadily declining ceiling on carbon emissions, and by creating a market that rewards companies for slashing CO2 (corporations that reduce emissions below their allotment can sell them on the open market), it uses the free enterprise system to achieve emissions reduction.",""],[1,"162","6","Market Monitoring Agency","It needs a market monitoring agency to examine issues such as rent seeking, cornering the market and so on.",""],[1,"162","9","Economy-wide Carbon Tax","Carbon tax covers the entire economy, including automobiles, households and other units impossible to reach in a cap-and- trade.",""],[1,"162","26","Central Energy Conservation Fund Financing Mechanism","the energy efficiency fee will be deposited in the Central Energy Conservation Fund managed by the BEE (Section 20 of the Energy Conservation Act).",""],[1,"173","2","Emitter Pays Carbon Pricing Mechanism","Possible mechanism for implementing this could be a system of compulsory carbon credits purchased by emitting entities, equal to their emissions over and above the permissible limit, or a carbon tax regime with proceeds going to the carbon service providers, including the State Forest Departments, in proportion to the quantum of carbon service provided.",""],[1,"239","3","National Mission on Enhanced Energy Efficiency","A National Mission on Enhanced Energy Efficiency (NMEEE) has been launched under National Action Plan on Climate Change (NAPCC) by the Ministry of Power in order to achieve fuel sav- ings of 23 MTOE (against 24 MTOE consumed in nine sectors); avoid capacity addition of over 19,000 MW; and reduce 98.55 MTs of Carbon Dioxide (CO2) equivalent annually over a five-year period.","Ministry of Power"],[1,"239","3","Low-Carbon Inclusive Growth Strategy Drafting","An Expert Group constituted by the Planning Commission is in the process of drafting a low carbon inclusive growth strategy for India for the Twelfth Five Year Plan.","Planning Commission"],[1,"260","7","Lower-Carbon Industrial Transition Strategy","This will need to be addressed through a well thought out lower carbon strategy for inclusive growth.",""],[1,"261","2","Energy Efficiency Mission","The Energy Efficiency Mission seeks to upscale efforts to create a market for energy efficiency.",""],[1,"261","3","Sustainable Habitat Mission","The Sustainable Habitat Mission attempts to promote energy efficiency in buildings, management of solid waste, and modal shift to public transport including transport options based on bio-diesel and hydrogen.",""],[1,"262","8","Green Building Codes and Urban Habitat Planning Initiative","the Habitat Mission under NAPCC should focus on critical areas like evolution, adoption and implementation of green building codes, urban habitat planning and development, so on.",""],[1,"262","8","PM's Council Monitoring of Solid Waste Management","this subject, being of immense importance, should be monitored as a policy thrust area through the PM's Council;",""],[1,"262","10","Energy Conservation Fund for Industrial Efficiency","To facilitate efficiency improving technology interventions in the industry at large, an Energy Conservation Fund needs to set up under the aegis of the Bureau of Energy Efficiency.",""],[1,"262","10","Advanced Coal Technologies Promotion","Similarly, Advanced Coal Technologies, Dedicated Freight Corridors and Improved Urban Public Transport are the critical policy thrust areas that can go a long way in saving the scarce fossil fuels for the country, and therefore, need a focused attention at the highest level.",""],[1,"262","10","Dedicated Freight Corridors Development","Similarly, Advanced Coal Technologies, Dedicated Freight Corridors and Improved Urban Public Transport are the critical policy thrust areas that can go a long way in saving the scarce fossil fuels for the country, and therefore, need a focused attention at the highest level.",""],[1,"262","10","Improved Urban Public Transport","Similarly, Advanced Coal Technologies, Dedicated Freight Corridors and Improved Urban Public Transport are the critical policy thrust areas that can go a long way in saving the scarce fossil fuels for the country, and therefore, need a focused attention at the highest level.",""],[1,"265","2","Renewable and Clean Energy Deployment Support","Low carbon strategies will particularly require enhanced deployment of renewable and clean energy technologies, and capital finance for improvements in technology.",""],[1,"265","2","Climate Policy Regulatory and Market Mechanism Framework","Some of these objectives may be met through regulatory interventions and use of market mechanisms, in which case the required budgetary support may be small, but indirect and unquantified costs for economy may be large.",""],[1,"265","2","Sectoral Climate Mitigation Policy Financing","In other cases, adequate financial outlays will be needed to implement policies and measures that can achieve specific mitigation outcomes in the individual sectors.",""],[1,"265","13","Budgetary Support for Low-Carbon Infrastructure and Transport","Some prominent examples are budgetary support for super-critical thermal power plants, for dedicated freight corridor, for urban public transport, so on.",""],[1,"267","6","Perform, Achieve & Trade Scheme","Innovative domestic markets mechanisms are being evolved under the Perform, Achieve & Trade (PAT) Scheme that is being implemented by the Bureau of Energy Efficiency for designated indus- tries under the provision of Energy Conservation Act 2010.",""],[1,"267","6","Partial Risk Guarantee Fund for Energy Efficiency Support","Efforts are being made to support this scheme by creating a Partial Risk Guarantee Fund with help from the Global Environmental Facility.",""],[1,"267","6","Financial Support and Capacity Building for Small and Medium Industry","for which new forms of financial support and capacity building are needed.",""],[1,"267","7","Renewable Energy Certificates Regulatory Mechanism","Renewable Energy Certificates is another attempt at creating domestic markets through regulatory interventions at the state level.",""]],"15":[[1,"302","5","Nuclear Science and Engineering R&D Programme","The DAE has been pursuing R&D in nuclear science and engineering and also in advanced mathematics.","DAE"],[1,"302","5","Indigenous Nuclear Power Programme Support","R&D by the R&D units of DAE provide valuable support to expand the indigenous Indian nuclear power programme","DAE"],[1,"302","5","Non-power Nuclear Technology Applications Development","R&D by the R&D units of DAE provide valuable support to expand ... and also to develop non-power applications of nuclear technology for use in industry, agriculture, health care and research.","DAE"],[1,"302","6","Department of Atomic Energy R&D Programme Framework","Categorisation of the DAE's R&D activities into seven major programmes MP1 to MP7 followed during the previous two Plan periods will be maintained in the Twelfth Plan.",""],[1,"302","6","Nuclear Power and Radiation Technology Research Programme","Major programmes MP1-MP3 address R&D support to the three-stage Indian nuclear power programmes; MP4 addresses the development of advanced technologies such as accelerators, lasers and so on, and radiation technologies and their applications; MP5 incorpo- rates the basic research in all the relevant branches of science; MP6 facilitates strengthening the research- education linkages and MP7 aims to development of the infrastructure for all the R&D activities.",""],[1,"303","1","Nuclear Plant Ageing Management and Safety Upgrade Programme","The main emphasis of the DAE in the Twelfth plan includes ageing management and safety upgrades of all nuclear plants in operation, and incorporating enhanced safety features in the upcoming plants.","DAE"],[1,"303","1","Metallic Fuel Deployment for Fast Reactor Development","Another thrust area, metal- lic fuel deployment with its associated fuel cycle in the fast reactor, is the key to reducing doubling time, thus accelerating the pace of nuclear power deployment.",""],[1,"303","4","Nuclear Power Plant Safety Research and Life-Extension Programme","Programme under MP1-MP3 include experimental verification of safety-related issues, ageing and degradation studies, life-extension assessment and investigation of new safety concepts for incorporation in nuclear power plants to address extreme external events.",""],[1,"303","4","Uranium Exploration Technology Development Programme","Thrust would be given to the development of new techniques for further exploration of uranium with a view to augmenting installed nuclear power capacity.",""],[1,"303","6","Fast Reactor Programme Infrastructure and Fuel Cycle Facilities","Other activities for the fast reactor programme include production of mixed oxide fuel pins for PFBR at Advanced Fuel Fabrication facility; alloy characterisation facility for fast reactor fuels, pyro-chemical reprocessing and sodium fire facilities, fuel cell and argon glove box for sodium chemistry studies and ultra filtration units for separation of strontium, cesium, lanthanides and actinides from simulated wastes.",""],[1,"303","6","Robotic Inspection Devices for Reactor Maintenance","Robotic device for in-service inspection and indigenous spider-robot for steam generator tube inspection have also been developed.",""],[1,"305","1","Indigenous Light Water Reactor Research and Development Programme","For Light Water Reactor (LWR) programme, R&D to develop, design and verify indigenous LWR con- cepts and development of equipment is planned.",""],[1,"305","1","Prototype Fast Breeder Reactor Civil Construction","The civil construction of the PFBR is in an advanced stage and is expected to be completed by 2012-13.",""],[1,"305","1","MOX-Fuelled Fast Reactor Development Programme","Two 500 MWe MOX-fuelled fast reactors are planned to be set up.",""],[1,"305","1","Metal-Fuelled Fast Reactor Design and Construction Plan","a 120 MWe metal-fuelled fast reactor will be designed at IGCAR in the Twelfth Plan, with construction proposed in the Thirteenth Plan.",""],[1,"305","2","Thorium-Fuelled Advanced Heavy Water Reactor Demonstration Programme","A major programme to experimentally demonstrate the available margins to extreme internal and external events will be carried forward in the Twelfth Plan period to further add to validation of these advanced safety features, many of which are generic in nature.",""],[1,"305","3","Compact High Temperature Reactor Technology Development","planning for a CHTR is an important step towards the development of advanced reactor technologies required for hydro- gen generation.",""],[1,"311","11","Apsara Reactor Upgradation with Indigenous Fuel","Apsara Reactor upgradation with indigenously developed fuel.",""],[1,"311","12","AHWR Test Facility Construction and Commissioning","Construction and commissioning of AHWR Thermal Hydraulics Test Facility (ATTF) and AHWR Fuelling Machine Test Facility (FMTF).",""],[1,"311","13","Low-Energy Linear Proton Accelerator Development and Commissioning","Technology development and commissioning of a low energy (20 MeV) linear proton accelerator (LEHIPA) as a part of front end of ADS driver.",""],[1,"311","15","Experimental Solar Test Facility","Setting up an experimental Solar Test Facility (SOTEF).",""],[1,"311","16","Electron and Ion Accelerator Technology Development","Technology development for Electron and Ion Accelerators.",""],[1,"311","17","Fast Reactor Fuel Reprocessing Facility Expansion","Augmentation of facilities for Fast Reactor Fuel Reprocessing.",""]],"16":[[1,"51","8","Major Port Capacity Expansion through PPP","An aggressive expansion of port capacity in the major ports based on PPP is essential in the Twelfth Plan.",""],[1,"51","8","New Public-Private Partnership Ports","In addition, two entirely new PPP ports are proposed by the Central Government; one in West Bengal and the other in Andhra Pradesh.",""],[1,"80","7","Port Project Clearance and Decision Facilitation","It is vital that we smoothen out the path ahead for the port sector.",""],[1,"129","2","Private Participation in Port Development and Operations","The government has encouraged private sector participation in port development and operations.",""]],"17":[[1,"164","8","Rail Freight Modal Shift Incentive Policy","In order to improve the efficiency of freight movement, it is necessary to devise policy instruments to incentivize modal shift to the more efficient modes of freight transport, namely the railways.",""],[1,"164","15","Dedicated Freight Corridor Project","GoI initiated the DFC project by setting up a special purpose vehicle called Dedicated Freight Corridor Corporation of India (DFCCIL) in 2006.",""],[1,"165","4","Transport Policy Committee Freight Efficiency Study","The Transport Policy Committee needs to further investigate these bottlenecks and suggest solutions to overcome them.",""]],"18":[[1,"226","6","Tenancy Legalization and Land Leasing Reform","There is, therefore, a strong case for legalising tenancy and allowing leasing-in and leasing-out land with adequate safeguards to protect the interests of small and marginal farmers.",""],[1,"226","7","Tenancy Law Reform","Reforming tenancy laws would allow all sections to appropriately participate in the lease market depending upon their resource endowment.",""],[1,"227","6","Collective Land Distribution for D&W Farmers","All such available land should be distributed to groups of D&W farmers rather than to individual families.",""],[1,"227","8","Homestead Land Ownership Rights Programme","Kerala has had a longstanding programme of giving ownership rights on land on which a homestead stands, in its land reform programme.",""],[1,"227","8","Homestead Plot Allocation for Landless Families","The West Bengal and Orissa governments have also allotted homestead plots to landless families.",""],[1,"228","2","Land Leasing Legal and Institutional Reform","This will need both legal changes and institutional innovation.",""],[1,"228","3","Tenancy Legalisation and Regulation Reform","Tenancy should be legalised and regulated to provide security to the tenant while also protecting the landowner's rights.",""],[1,"228","3","Group-Based Land Leasing Support","A group approach to leasing in and use of the land should be built into the system, as also financial and institutional support for such cul- tivation.",""],[1,"228","6","Public Land Bank at Panchayat Level","the Twelfth Plan Working Group on Disadvantaged Farmers, including Women proposes the creation of a Public Land Bank (PLB) at the panchayat level.",""],[1,"228","7","Concessional Land Leasing for Disadvantaged Farmers","The PLB would lease out the land under its command to specially designated categories of disadvantaged farmers, such as marginal farmers, women, dalits, and tribals, whether leasing as individuals or in groups.",""],[1,"228","7","Institutional Finance and Support for Leaseholders","Institutional finance and other support could also be provided.",""],[1,"228","8","Private Land Bank Incentive Scheme","There can be several incentives for farmers to deposit their land in the PLB: (i) a minimum rent from the PLB even for fallow land; (ii) an additional 'topping up' rent for land that gets leased out; (iii) development of the land in terms of soil conservation and so on, via MGNREGA or other means.",""],[1,"228","8","Guaranteed Lease and Consolidated Plot Provision","The PLB should provide a guaranteed lease and, where possible, a consolidated plot of reasonable size.",""],[1,"229","1","Pilot Seed Capital Support for PLB","The PLB should be provided initial seed capital from the central and the state governments in a ratio of say, 80:20, or even 100 per cent by the Centre in the pilot stage for three years.",""],[1,"229","2","Group Farming Expansion Programme","The most comprehensive solution would be group farming with individual land ownership.",""],[1,"229","3","Women’s Group Farming and Joint Leasing Support","In March 2010, an additional step was taken under which SHGs undertaking group farming can be registered as Joint Liability Groups (JLGs)-a National Bank for Agriculture and Rural Development (NABARD) scheme-and given financial and technical support.",""],[1,"229","3","Land Preparation and Reclamation Support for Group Farming","The state government also provides support for land preparation and reclamation (linking it with MGNREGS in some districts).",""],[1,"229","4","Women-Led Dry Land Agriculture Group Farming Programme","In 2005, United Nations Development Programme (UNDP) involvement ended but the programme continued under APMSS.",""],[1,"229","6","Pilot Replication of Kudumbshree and APMSS Models","The Kudumbshree and APMSS models could be tried on a pilot basis in other States, adapted to local contexts.",""],[1,"229","6","Replication of Group Enterprise Models in Agriculture","The group enterprise model should also be replicated for other agricultural sectors, such as fisheries (for example, group pisciculture), poul- try or livestock management.",""],[1,"229","6","Integration of Group Farming with MGNREGS","Group farming could also be integrated with MGNREGS for improving agricultural land.",""],[1,"229","6","Promotion of Group Farming with MGNREGS","Such efforts to integrate group farming with MGNREGS need to be encour- aged to leverage such schemes better for improving land resources for agriculture.",""],[1,"229","7","Five-Year Funding for Group Enterprise Formation and Mentoring","Funding for five years could be provided to all organisations willing to help form and mentor groups until they become self-sustaining.",""]],"19":[[1,"11","4","Agricultural GDP Growth Acceleration Target","Hence the Twelfth Plan target of accelerating GDP growth is accompanied by a specific target to accelerate growth of agricultural GDP.",""],[1,"14","4","Agricultural Diversification and Private Sector Marketing Policy","This includes policies aimed at supporting the diversification of agriculture, and greater involvement of the private sector in marketing agricultural produce.",""],[1,"65","5","Agricultural Productivity Support and Rain-Fed Farming Investment Package","Action is needed on several fronts including provision of basic support services such as technology and irrigation infrastructure, access to credit, good and reliable seeds and improved post-harvest technology.",""],[1,"65","5","MGNREGA On-Farm Productivity Convergence","Properly designed and converged, MGNREGA can contribute to creating positive synergy with agricultural growth.",""],[1,"65","6","Crop Procurement Parity Reform","Third, to ensure that there is enough parity between procurement operations for crops such as oilseeds and pulses as for rice and wheat, so that we can avoid situations like at present",""]],"20":[[1,"274","2","Long-term Agricultural R&D Grant Support","The Department of Biotechnology (DBT) pro- poses to support 10 agricultural universities through long-term R&D grants for promoting R&D on agri- culture for public and social good.","Department of Biotechnology (DBT)"],[1,"274","2","Interdepartmental Agricultural Research Synergy Framework","Synergy and con- necting competencies of institutions under Indian Council of Agricultural Research (ICAR) with the research programmes supported by the six science departments form the selected approach for R&D on agriculture.","Indian Council of Agricultural Research (ICAR)"],[1,"274","2","Integrated Agricultural Technology Deployment Support","As an example, synthesising R&D outputs from agro-metrological services of Ministry of Earth Sciences (MoES), advisory services of State remote sensing centres and State-based Spatial Data Infrastructure initiatives of Department of Space (DOS), National Spatial Data Infrastructure and National Geographic Information Systems (NGIS), fertiliser and other agrochemical technology solu- tions from CSIR, food processing technologies from both CSIR and Department of Atomic Energy (DAE), translation research in molecular breeding emanating from the efforts of DBT and technology deployment support to States for implementation of technologies and services by Department of Science & Technology (DST) would form a strong impact.","Ministry of Earth Sciences (MoES) | Department of Space (DOS) | Department of Science & Technology (DST) | Department of Biotechnology (DBT) | CSIR | Department of Atomic Energy (DAE)"],[1,"274","2","Priority Agricultural Technology Development Agenda","Secondary agriculture, climate-resilient agriculture, water-saving agriculture, technologies for reducing food wastages as well as indigenous manufacture of fertilisers, precision agriculture for water-starved agro-climatic zones and international S&T coop- eration for enhancing water and land productivity would form the priority areas of the six departments.",""],[1,"284","7","Regulatory Approval Pipeline for Public Sector GM Crops","Public sector-developed GM crops such as insect-resistant chickpea, rice, brinjal; drought-tolerant groundnut, sunflower and mustard with hybrid vigour are in regulatory pipeline.",""],[1,"284","7","Accelerated Molecular Breeding Programme","Accelerated molecular breeding programmes in rice, wheat, corn and mustard have been launched, and protein-rich maize is already commercialised.",""]],"21":[[1,"55","2","Soil Fertility Management and Organic Fertilisation Support","The best practices of soil fertility management need to be adopted, which include generation of biomass for bulk addition of organic matter in the soil to maintain proper soil health, in situ degeneration of biomass through sole cropping/inter-cropping/bund cropping of green manure crops, recycling of farm and household waste through use of intensive nutrient recycling methods such as composting, production of bio- fertilisers at regional and local levels, adoption of bio-dynamic farming methods and crop rotations to enrich the soil.",""],[1,"65","5","Integrated Rain-Fed Area Natural Resource Management Approach","Rain-fed farming requires a natural resource management perspective with a farming systems approach focusing on producing diverse products that mutually reinforce each other and stabilise the system.",""],[1,"65","5","Rain-Fed Farming Resilience and Soil Productivity Investments","They require knowledge and institutional investments to improve soil moisture management, enhance soil productivity, revitalise common pool resources, provide appropriate seed and low external input systems as also farm mechanisation, along with diverse livelihood options such as livestock and fisheries.",""],[1,"65","6","Rice Productivity and Groundwater Stress Reduction Measures","First, to increase rice pro- ductivity in Eastern India and at same time relieve North-West India from the stress on groundwater caused by this water-intensive crop.",""],[1,"65","6","Balanced Nutrient Use Improvement Measures","Second, to focus on growing imbalances in nutrient use that can affect productivity seriously.",""],[1,"258","2","Climate-Resilient Agricultural Adaptation Measures","Amongst the key actions for adapting Indian agriculture to climate change are improved land management practices, development of resource conserving technologies, development of crop varieties that can withstand climate-stress, effective risk management through early warning, credit-insurance support to farmers and nutritional strategies for managing heat stress in dairy animals.",""],[1,"258","2","Methane Reduction and Sustainable Emissions Management Measures","Complementary actions in terms of identification of cost-effective opportunities for reducing methane generation, emissions in ruminants by modification of diet, and in rice paddies by water and nutrient management will help make adaptation measures sustainable.",""],[1,"258","2","Agricultural Policy Support for Land Use and Water Efficiency","New policies should support the new land use arrangements, enhance investment in water harvesting, promote small-farm mechanisation and efficient water use technologies.",""],[1,"258","2","Financial Incentives for Sustainable Land Management","A package of financial incentives for improved land management, including resource conservation (water, carbon, energy) and balanced fertiliser use may facilitate quicker adoption of these measures.",""],[1,"261","4","Sustainable Agriculture Mission","The Sustainable Agriculture Mission aims at making Indian agriculture more resilient to climate change through development of new varieties of climate-stress resistant crops, new credit and insur- ance mechanisms, and improving productivity of rain-fed agriculture.",""]],"22":[[1,"205","2","Small Multi-purpose Farm Pond Development","Part of the waterlogged area could be used for construction of small multi-purpose farm ponds.",""],[1,"205","2","Rainwater Conservation in Upper Catchments","In situ rainwater conservation in the upper catchment and intensification of the use of groundwater through shallow tubewells are possible interventions to mitigate the problem.",""],[1,"205","2","Shallow Tubewell Groundwater Intensification","In situ rainwater conservation in the upper catchment and intensification of the use of groundwater through shallow tubewells are possible interventions to mitigate the problem.",""]],"24":[[1,"232","11","Participatory Urban Renewal Pilot in Mumbai C Ward","Another ongoing experiment is the improvement of the C ward in Mumbai that showcases the promises of participatory processes in urban renewal.",""],[1,"232","11","Scaling of Land Readjustment Experiments","There is need for scaling such experiments.",""],[1,"232","15","Urban Land-Use Regulation Reform","Adopting mixed land-use and subsequently modifying regulations governing land-use, and removing deficiencies in the urban land market need to be given high priority.",""],[1,"232","15","JNNURM Urban Reforms Completion","Completion of reforms mandated by JNNURM must be given priority.",""],[1,"232","16","Land-Use Conversion and Building Bye-Law Simplification Reform","Simplification of procedures for conversion of land-use and change in building bye laws have been mandated under JNNURM.",""],[1,"233","1","Phase-II of the Rajiv Awas Yojana","Phase-II of the Rajiv Awas Yojana (RAY) is to be launched during the Twelfth Plan.",""],[1,"233","1","Slum Dwellers Property Rights Enactment","RAY mandates giving 'property rights' to slum dwellers by suitable enactment within a year of the project being sanctioned.",""],[1,"233","1","Affordable Housing Reservation Requirements","during this period it also mandates enactment of legislations to earmark 10-15 per cent of land or 20-25 per cent dwelling units for housing projects for economically weaker sections/LIG category and earmarking of at least 25 per cent of municipal budget for urban poor.",""],[1,"233","1","State Timelines for Rent Control Act Modification","It also requires the participating states to draw spe- cific timelines for legislations like modification of the Rent Control Act.",""]],"25":[[1,"57","7","Industrial Water Pollution Control and Water Efficiency Measures","Parallel efforts are needed to contain pollution of surface water and contamination of groundwater, which is reaching serious proportions.",""],[1,"58","2","Integrated Urban Sewage Treatment Requirement","The Twelfth Plan must ensure that no water scheme in urban India will be sanctioned without an integrated sewage treatment component, which ensures that city waste does not pollute our fresh water sources.",""]],"26":[[1,"6","3","Foreign Investment Liberalization and Attraction Measures","Caps on the permitted level of FDI in a number of sectors have been relaxed, and a broader review of policies is being undertaken with a view to facilitate an easier flow of foreign investment.",""],[1,"61","2","Investor Confidence Restoration Measures","The Finance Ministry has appointed two expert committees to look into these issues and it is hoped that the recommendations of these committees will provide a reasonable basis for reviving investor confidence on these issues.","Finance Ministry"],[1,"63","8","Foreign Direct Investment Liberalization in Retail and Other Sectors","The steps taken to liberalise FDI, especially in areas where there is evident investor interest such as for example, FDI in retail, would help by sending the right signals.",""],[1,"64","4","Gradual Financial Sector Liberalisation","we should continue with our strategy of gradual liberalisation in the financial sector.",""],[1,"97","1","Calibrated Capital Account Opening Policy","This basic policy of calibrated opening up of the capital account should continue.",""],[1,"97","2","Calibrated Capital Account Liberalisation Road Map","There is a case for comprehensively reviewing the present policy and laying out a clearer road map of calibrated liberalisation over the Twelfth Plan period and beyond.",""]],"27":[[1,"14","1","Rigorous and Independent Programme Evaluation System","This calls for rigorous and independent evaluations of the effectiveness of our programmes in achieving the desired outcomes and an analysis of why they fall short.",""],[1,"15","5","Strengthening the Role of Evaluation in Planning","The role of evaluation needs to be greatly strengthened in our planning process.",""],[1,"15","5","Independent Evaluation Office","The Planning Commission has created a new mechanism with an Independent Evaluation Office headed by a Director General at the level of Member of the Planning Commission.","Planning Commission"],[1,"16","3","Mid-Term Review of Policy Change Implementation","The mid term review of the Twelfth Plan should focus specifically on the success achieved in this dimension.",""],[1,"16","3","Assessment of Centre and State Policy Restructuring Implementation","An assessment should be made of success achieved by the Centre, in implementing the policy restructuring envisaged with a parallel exercise for each State.",""],[1,"102","6","Poverty Measurement Reform Committee","it has been decided to (i) de-link the benefits that are intended for the poor from the PLs computed from the NSSO household consumption surveys using the Tendulkar methodology; and (ii) set up a fresh Committee to go into every aspect of the issue.",""],[1,"309","4","STI Research, Consultation, and Indicator Development Programme","To commission 15 Study Reports, 10 Policy Research Studies, 3 Development of STI indicators for India, 5 Inter-country policy comparison studies, 12 External consultations and inter-country and 10 Technology and Innovation (STI) indicator reports have been planned.",""],[1,"318","4","Government Performance Management System","The govern- ment has initiated a performance management sys- tem which requires every ministry and department to undertake a stakeholder consultation to assess the",""],[1,"318","5","Results Framework Document Adoption","this system, generally called the Results Framework Document (RFD), is now adopted by almost all Ministries at the Centre.",""],[1,"318","5","State-Level Results Framework Adoption","Some State Governments such as Kerala and Himachal Pradesh have also begun to adopt this approach.",""],[1,"320","3","Government Plan Expenditure Effectiveness Improvements","The first relates to systemic improvements, which increase the effectiveness of government plan expenditure on new programmes.",""],[1,"320","3","Government Service Delivery Customer Satisfaction Improvement","The second relates to improvements in customer satisfaction on the delivery of services by government agencies.",""],[1,"320","6","Administrative Process Review and Decision-Making Reform","There is a need for an in-depth review of administrative processes at various levels to ensure expeditious decisions that can advance development priorities of the nation.",""],[1,"320","6","Civil Servant Protection Framework","An essential requirement for this is to ensure that the administrative system and ethos protects civil servants, who act bona fide, and in good faith.",""],[1,"324","3","Convergence and Rationalization of CSS Schemes","The number of CSS should be limited and only those schemes which are required as a part of the convergence process as a broader scheme have large outlays so as to make impact across the states to be implemented, the rest to be weeded out and to be converged with other schemes.",""],[1,"324","4","Classification of Existing CSS into Scheme Categories","The existing CSS should be categorised into Flagship schemes that have large outlays and address major national issues; major sub-sectoral schemes to address developmental problems of major sectors like agriculture, education and health and Sector-umbrella schemes which deal with the range of problems of concerned sector.",""],[1,"324","5","Transparent CSS Fund Allocation Guidelines","Distribution of CSS funds among States should be based on transparent notified guidelines which should be put on the website of the concerned Ministries.",""],[1,"324","8","Flexible Funding Allocation for Centrally Sponsored Schemes","All CSS must have 20 per cent flexi funds (10 per cent for Flagship schemes).",""],[1,"324","9","Flexible Financial Support for Flagship Programmes","Each flagship programme will provide a flexible pool of financial resources to be used to facilitate and incentivise innovative practices that blaze a trail for others to follow during the Twelfth Plan period.",""],[1,"324","10","Concurrent Monitoring and Evaluation of CSS","There should be a concurrent monitoring and evaluation of the CSS.",""],[1,"324","11","Centrally Sponsored Schemes Restructuring and Flexibility Reform","The Twelfth Plan will restructure the CSSs and provide flexibility in the light of the recommendations of the BKC Committee.",""],[1,"324","12","Cross-Programme Convergence of Implementation","The Twelfth Plan visualises a convergence of implementation across programmes to pool financial and physical resources across sectors to attain synergy to benefit the target group.",""],[1,"325","3","Central Ministry Knowledge Partnership and Capacity-Building Support for Scheme Implementation","the central minis- tries role would be to act as a knowledge part- ner and enabler to the project implementation, which will be typically in the states.",""],[1,"325","7","Consultative Machinery for Scheme Formulation","The schemes which may require formulation of laws or guidelines would need to have extensive consultative machinery.",""],[1,"325","7","Consultation Quality Improvement Support","Resources would need to be provided to improve the quality of consultations.",""],[1,"325","8","Scheme Evaluation and Feedback Mechanisms","The architecture of this scheme must have evaluation and feedback mechanisms.",""],[1,"325","10","Implementation Learning and Feedback Mechanisms","It is important to have learning and feedback mechanisms in place to ensure that implementation effectiveness improves.",""],[1,"325","10","Flexible Scheme Implementation Support","Developing flexibility and its effective use during the implementation of these schemes for improving their outcomes would need collective action.",""],[1,"326","7","Independent Evaluation Office","This evaluation function is being strengthened by setting up an Independent Evaluation Office (IEO), under the aegis of Planning Commission.",""],[1,"330","1","Results Framework Document Reform","In a recent reform initiative, a number of central ministries have adopted a Results Framework Document, which provides a summary of the most important results that the concerned departments and ministries expect to achieve in the year.",""],[1,"330","3","Planning Commission Systemic Analysis Support for Cross-Ministerial Outcomes","This critical \"system's input\" to the RFD process can be provided by the Planning Commission.",""],[1,"330","6","Implementation System Development","Build an implementation system, don't just do the task:",""],[1,"330","7","Systemic Experimentation and Learning Mechanisms","Systemic experimentation and learning help to progressively, and rapidly improve implementa- tion: Even carefully designed programmes are likely to face challenges from unforeseen changes in the environment.",""],[1,"331","4","Interdepartmental Manufacturing Coordination Review","Before setting up such an agency/committee, the tasks required to be performed by such an agency/committee must be analysed and the existing system of agency/ committees must be mapped to eliminate any overlaps and redundancies.",""],[1,"332","6","Backbone Capability for Multi-Stakeholder Policy Implementation","Hence, there is a need to establish an effective 'backbone' capability which will provide strength to multi-stakeholder policy and implementation processes.",""],[1,"332","6","Inter-Agency Coordination and Stakeholder Consultation Mechanism","Cohesion can be brought about through more effective co-ordination amongst agencies, and more effective consultation amongst stakeholders.",""],[1,"332","6","Specialized Consensus-Building and Programme Management Skills Development","For this, the government will require specialised skills such as consensus building and programme management to manage this process.",""],[1,"332","7","Backbone Capability of Small Catalytic Units","Therefore the 'backbone' capability must essentially comprise of small catalytic units located in many parts of the system, which can provide the 'tools and techniques' to the various states and ministries to effectively co-ordinate, design and implement their programmes.",""],[1,"332","8","Backbone Coordination Support for Existing Institutions","The 'backbone' capability must enable these institutions to fulfil their co-ordination and implementation roles more effectively, rather than aim to be a master co-ordinator over them.",""],[1,"333","2","Backbone Units Network for Planning and Coordination Support","The backbone capabilities must be delivered by a network of backbone units spread across the country.",""]],"28":[[1,"47","8","Public Information Access Improvement","With improvement in literacy and education, and developments in information technology, we are in a position to provide our people with access to information, including obtaining birth records, land records, payment records for utilities and so on.",""],[1,"49","3","Public Service Satisfaction Measurement and Public Reporting Mechanism","The first step in reforming public service delivery is to devise mechanisms for measuring the extent of public satisfaction with public services and publicising the results.",""],[1,"317","6","Government Innovation and Service Delivery Improvement Initiatives","there are also several innovative efforts underway within the government structures that aim to improve processes and service delivery, enhance collaboration and generate greater transparency and accountability.",""],[1,"326","8","Time-Bound Public Service Delivery","It is important that these are available within a prescribed time line.",""],[1,"326","9","Citizen Service Delivery Time-Limit Notification and Simplified Procedures","Simultaneously, necessary simplified procedures should be put in place within the government to enable these services to be available.",""],[1,"326","9","Swift Punitive Action for Service Delivery Failures","Failure to deliver the service must result in swift punitive action.",""],[1,"327","4","Government Citizen Communication Improvement","Government needs to communicate more effectively with the citizens.",""],[1,"327","6","Government Communication Modernization Initiative","government's communication processes must be modernised, become more proactive, and reach out to citizens more effectively.",""],[1,"328","1","Anti-Corruption Measures","strong measures need to be taken to combat corruption.",""],[1,"328","3","Transparent Public Procurement and Licensing Procedures","It is important that the use of e-tendering, mandatory posting of all major procurements on the websites and other transparent procedures are adopted for all major procurements.",""],[1,"328","3","Digital Transparency in Contract Award Processes","This can be strengthened by using e-technology more extensively.",""],[1,"328","6","Special Courts for Corruption Cases","Special courts may need to be set up to expeditiously try such cases.",""],[1,"328","6","Lokpal Anti-Corruption Institution","The government has also recently introduced a legislation to create Lokpal as an institution to enquire into corruption at higher levels.",""],[1,"328","7","Civil Service Reform and Recruitment Modernization","While these need to be implemented, three specific areas need special focus.",""],[1,"329","4","Agency-Based Administrative Reform","Once this policy is adopted, it will be necessary for ministries, both in the Central and State Government to scrutinize their functions and keep only those with them which relate to policy analysis, strategy decisions and other key areas which have to be performed by them only.",""],[1,"329","4","Executive Empowerment for Policy Implementation","The executive arm can then be empowered to execute the policies effectively.",""],[1,"329","4","Autonomy and Accountability Framework for Agencies","Right balance between autonomy and accountability needs to be struck while design- ing the framework of institutional agencies.",""],[1,"330","4","Regulatory Authority Accountability and Performance Assessment Reform","It is necessary that these regulatory authori- ties are made accountable and assessed for their per- formance.",""],[1,"369","8","Law and Order Improvement Efforts","Efforts have to continue to further improve the scenario.",""]],"29":[[1,"48","1","Aadhar Unique Identity and Electronic Payments Platform","Aadhar project, which provides a unique identifica- tion (UID) number, backed by biometric data cap- ture, to establish identity unambiguously, is a major step forward.",""],[1,"48","2","Aadhar-Linked Cash Transfer Reform for Subsidies and Beneficiary Payments","The Aadhar platform will also facilitate a shift from the physical delivery of subsidised commodi- ties through the Public Distribution System (PDS) to a system of cash payment, if desired.",""],[1,"70","3","Direct Cash Transfer Reform for Subsidies and Welfare Payments","Major subsidies and welfare related beneficiary payments to be shifted to a direct cash transfer",""]],"30":[[1,"53","2","Registry Database Development for Collateral and Actuarial Use","An area that government should take a lead is in creation of suit- able databases of registry information both for easier collateral and finer actuarial calculations.",""],[1,"53","2","UID-Supported Land Records and Property Titling Reform","The UID project can help with this, but there are also more basic requirements such as proper land records and property titling",""],[1,"118","1","State Treasury and Core Banking Solution for Scheme Tracking","State treasuries and Core Banking Solution (CBS) to enable real-time tracking of all Schemes for which resources are transferred to States and their agencies.",""],[1,"118","5","Expansion of CPSMS for Fund Tracking and Utilisation","The ongoing CPSMS would be expanded to facilitate better tracking and utilisa- tion of funds.",""],[1,"118","6","Plan Resource Transfer Through Consolidated Fund","The recommendation on transfer of Plan resources to States only through the Consolidated Fund (Treasury) route will be implemented along with other recommendations to improve accountability of resources.",""],[1,"233","8","Transition to a Torrens Land Records System","In order to move decisively in the direction of a Torrens System of land records in India, the National",""],[1,"234","2","Real-Time Land Records System","To usher in real-time land records",""],[1,"234","5","Conclusive Land Titling Reform","To ultimately replace the present deeds registra- tion and presumptive title system with that of conclusive titling with title guarantee",""],[1,"234","6","Digital Land Records and Single-Window Access System","Real-time records will be available, which will be tamper-proof.",""],[1,"234","7","Abolition of Stamp Papers and Bank-Based Payment of Registration Fees","Abolition of stamp papers and payment of stamp duty and registration fees through banks will also reduce interface with the registration bureaucracy.",""],[1,"234","8","Conclusive Land Titling Reform","Conclusive titling will reduce land disputes and litigation.",""],[1,"234","8","Digital Land Services and Certificate Delivery","Certificates based on land data (domi- cile, caste, income, and so on) will become avail- able through the web.",""],[1,"234","8","Land Passbook Issuance Simplification","Issue of land passbooks will become easier.",""],[1,"234","9","National Land Records Modernization Programme","The NLRMP is to be implemented in a time-bound manner and all the districts in the country are expected to be covered by the end of the Twelfth Plan.",""],[1,"234","9","Transition to Torrens Land Titling System","The country could move into a Torrens System during the Thirteenth Five Year Plan.",""],[1,"234","12","Ground Control Point Network Expansion and Digitization","So far, 300 GCPs (satellite) have been established at a spacing of 200-300 km; 2,220 points at a distance of 30 to 40 km (aerial) have to be under- taken in the second phase; the third phase will have GCPs at a spacing of 8 to 10 km (cadastral). Further, 42 million field measurement books and 1 million village maps will have to be digitised.",""],[1,"234","13","Registration Office Computerization and Interlinking Initiative","Of the 4,018 registration offices in the coun- try, 1,896 are yet to be computerised. Nearly all of them have to be interlinked with the state revenue departments.",""],[1,"234","13","Revenue Administration Training Programme","As many as 1.5 lakh patwaris, the staff of 5,000 tehsils, 4,000 registration offices, and 50,000 survey staff need to be trained.",""],[1,"235","1","Human Resource Capacity Building for NLRMP","There is need to both strengthen the profile of the personnel deployed, as also to train those currently in service, whose skill sets are currently completely out of sync with the demands posed by the radically new architecture vis- ualised for NLRMP.",""]],"31":[[1,"45","2","Teacher Quality, Motivation and Accountability Improvement","The quality of teachers and, even more important, their motivation and accountability will need to be improved.",""],[1,"45","2","Supplementary Instruction for First-Generation Learners","Many of the children who are presently in school are first-generation learners, and these children need supplementary instruction.",""],[1,"45","2","Multigrade Learning Adoption","New and innovative approaches such as multigrade learning, which has been successfully tried in Tamil Nadu, could be adopted in such cases.",""],[1,"45","4","Rashtriya Madhyamik Shiksha Abhiyan","the Rashtriya Madhyamik Shiksha Abhiyan (RMSA) addresses this issue.",""],[1,"45","4","Secondary School Curricular and Examination Reforms","Curricular and examination reforms in secondary schooling would receive special attention aimed at fostering critical thinking and analytical skills, and preparing students for further education.",""]],"32":[[1,"12","4","Productive Employment Promotion for Youth","It must also focus on promoting productive employment to meet the aspirations of youth.",""],[1,"45","6","Skill Development Mission","The Skill Development Mission is being launched to skill at least 50 million individuals by the end of the Twelfth Plan.",""],[1,"69","8","Non-Farm Employment Generation and Skills Certification Programme","Generate 50 million new work opportunities in the non-farm sector and provide skill certifica- tion to equivalent numbers during the Twelfth Five Year Plan.",""],[1,"369","12","State-Specific Employment Generation Approach","State-specific approach for creation of opportunities for employment gen- eration may be taken up.",""],[1,"369","12","Vocational Education Reform for Employability","Reforms in vocational education to ensure employability in the chang- ing market would help.",""]],"33":[[1,"46","1","Employer-Led Vocational Training and ITI Management Reform","special efforts are needed to ensure that employers and enterprises play an integral role in the conception and implementation of vocational training programmes, including managing Industrial Training Institutes (ITIs) and in the development of faculty.",""],[1,"46","1","Public-Private Partnership Framework for Vocational Training Investment","An enabling framework is needed that would attract private investment in Vocational Training through Public-Private Partnership (PPP).",""],[1,"46","1","Private Sector-Led Vocational Training Delivery Support","Mobilising the required investments, setting up first rate ITIs, ensuring efficiency in operations and management and enabling post-training employ- ment will be the primary responsibilities of private sector entities while the government will provide the enabling framework and the requisite financial sup- port especially in respect of SC, ST, Minorities and differently abled persons and other deprived sections of society.",""],[1,"52","3","ITI Development through Public-Private Partnership","a proposal for setting up 3,000 ITIs through PPP is under consideration",""],[1,"131","4","PPP-Based ITI Expansion Programme","the Prime Minister had announced setting up of 1,500 ITIs through PPP in unserved blocks.",""],[1,"131","5","Recurring Expenditure Support for ITI Training of Underprivileged Students","it is there- fore, proposed to provide support for the recurring expenditure incurred by an ITI towards training stu- dents from underprivileged families.",""],[1,"131","5","Capital Grant for ITI Infrastructure Development","Further, it is proposed to provide capital grant to meet a part of the cost of creating the infrastructure for setting up",""],[1,"131","6","Industrial Training Institute Vocational Training Scheme","It is expected that 30 lakh youth, including 15 lakh youth from socially and economically disad- vantaged groups would be initiated into vocational training and will acquire skills through the ITIs set up under this scheme.",""]],"34":[[1,"46","5","Expansion of Public Sector Health Care Capacity in Rural Areas","It is, therefore, essen- tial to expand public sector capacity in health care especially in the rural areas.",""],[1,"46","6","National Health Mission Expansion","The Twelfth Plan will therefore see the transformation of the NRHM into a National Health Mission, covering both rural and urban areas.",""],[1,"46","6","Urban Health Initiative for the Poor","An important component of the National Health Mission will be the Urban Health Initiative for the Poor, providing public sec- tor primary care facilities in selected low-income",""],[1,"47","4","Universal Health Care System Development","Putting a UHC system in place will take time, but we need to start building an appropriate architecture.",""],[1,"369","13","PPP Models for Education and Health Capacity Expansion","Development and operationalisation of PPP models in school and higher education and focus on increase in seats in medical colleges, nursing colleges and other licensed health professionals require special attention.",""],[1,"370","1","National Rural Health Mission Service Improvement","Improvement in the quality of NRHM services, rationalisation in the manpower requirement and involvement of PRIs/communities in improving health services is important.",""],[1,"370","1","Health Infrastructure and Workforce Expansion","Special focus required for development of infrastructure and availability of doctors, paramedics and nurses.",""],[1,"370","1","Public-Private Partnership in Secondary and Tertiary Health Care","Role of PPP in secondary and tertiary health care required to be encouraged.",""]],"35":[[1,"47","2","Expansion of Healthcare Professional Teaching and Training Programmes","We must therefore plan for an expansion of teaching and training programmes for healthcare professionals, particularly in the public sector institutions.",""]],"36":[[1,"47","3","Public Health Strengthening and Clean Water and Sanitation Provision","We need to give much greater attention to public health which has traditionally suffered from neglect.",""],[1,"47","3","Clean Drinking Water and Sanitation Expansion","We also need to focus much more on a provision of clean drinking water and sanitation, which can make a major con- tribution to improved health.",""],[1,"179","4","Rural Drinking Water and Sanitation Approach","A new approach to rural drinking water and sanitation.",""]],"37":[[1,"47","9","Mobile Telephony Innovation Promotion","Such innovations need to be encouraged.",""],[1,"50","6","Spectrum Auction Process","This process of auctioning is currently underway and is expected to be completed by January 2013.",""],[1,"51","2","Creative Use of the Universal Services Obligation Fund for Rural Mobile Access","There is scope for using the Universal Services Obligation Fund (USOF) creatively to enhance access to mobile telephone including especially as a platform for delivery of a range of services to the underserved in rural areas.",""]],"38":[[1,"69","25","Renewable Energy Capacity Expansion","Add 30,000 MW of renewable energy capacity in the Twelfth Plan.",""],[1,"129","3","Private Sector Participation in Thermal and Renewable Power Projects","To attract private sector participation, govern- ment has permitted the private sector to set up coal, gas or liquid-based thermal, hydel, wind or solar projects with foreign equity participation up to 100 per cent under the automatic route.",""],[1,"148","10","National Clean Energy Fund","The Government of India (GoI) has already set up a National Clean Energy Fund (NCEF) in 2010 by imposing a cess on coal at an effective rate of ₹50 per tonne.",""],[1,"148","10","Clean Energy Technology Support Programme","The NCEF will support projects, programmes and policies that promote clean energy technologies.",""],[1,"260","9","Solar Energy Expansion Mission","The Solar Mission aims at making solar electricity cost competitive to coal power and increasing the share of solar energy in the total energy mix",""],[1,"262","9","National Wind Power Mission","a new National Wind Power Mission needs to be launched under the NAPCC.",""],[1,"265","10","Guaranteed Procurement Policy","Guaranteed Procurement (public procurement policy)",""],[1,"265","13","Climate Change Budgetary Support and Sectoral Financing","Most of it would come as sectoral finance since some of the resources for adaptation and mitigation are built into the on-going schemes and programmes of the respective Ministries.",""],[1,"265","13","Central Grants for Forest Cover, Renewable Energy, and Water Sector","Three grants of ₹5,000 crore each, namely for forest cover, renewable energy and water sector, have been recommended for the State Governments.",""],[1,"266","11","National Clean Energy Fund","The cess has become operational and its revenue (of the order of about ₹3,000 crore every year) will go to a newly created National Clean Energy Fund (NECF), which will be used to finance innovative projects in clean energy technologies and to harness renewable energy sources to reduce dependence on fossil fuels.",""],[1,"266","11","Environment Remediation Programme Funding","From the Fund, allocation of ₹200 crore has already been proposed for environment remediation pro- grammes and another ₹200 crore for the Green India Mission.",""],[1,"266","12","Carbon Trust or Low Carbon Fund Establishment","It would be simpler and more useful to set up a 'Carbon Trust' or a 'Low Carbon Fund' managed by an autonomous body like the Bureau of Energy Efficiency, into which collections from an 'Energy Efficiency Surcharge or Levy', as suggested in the industry section, could be deposited.",""],[1,"266","14","Climate-Friendly Technology Diffusion Support Mechanism","a clearly planned strategy and mechanism for supporting diffusion, deployment and adoption of climate friendly technologies should be launched during the Twelfth Plan.",""],[1,"267","1","Climate Finance Mobilization through Multilateral Channels","A major channel for mobilising funds to the developing countries is likely to be the Green Climate Fund that is still under construction.",""],[1,"267","1","Climate Action Funding through Bilateral and Multilateral Channels","At the same time, the World Bank (Climate Investment Fund) and other multilateral agencies are offering their funds to be used for climate action on the basis of agreed terms and conditions.",""],[1,"267","1","Climate Finance Governance and Compliance Measures","It is important to ensure funds flows through these sources are indeed 'new and additional resources', and their terms of finance are in accordance with the multilateral rules of climate change.",""],[1,"267","5","Financial Institution Participation in Voluntary Project Financing","Efforts are being made to increase participation of financial institutions/banks in financing voluntary projects, including the bundling of small projects which may reduce transaction costs and increase the average project size.",""],[1,"268","2","Climate Change Action Financing from Domestic and International Sources","India's actions for climate change will, therefore, need to be financed from a pool of resources consisting of the domestic resources, international carbon finance and multilat- eral funds available to India in accordance with the agreed multilateral rules of the UNFCCC.",""]],"39":[[1,"12","4","Sustainable Growth Strategy","Finally it must ensure that the growth strategy is consistent with sustainability concerns which are now gaining importance.",""],[1,"43","13","National Action Plan for Climate Change","A National Action Plan for climate change has been evolved with eight component Missions.",""],[1,"43","13","Climate Change Mission Implementation in the Twelfth Plan","Implementation of these missions must be an integral part of the Twelfth Plan.",""],[1,"43","13","Climate Policy Monitoring and Emissions Intensity Reduction","Policies should be closely monitored to ensure that we achieve the stated objective of reducing the emissions intensity of our GDP by 20 per cent to 25 per cent between 2005 and 2020.",""],[1,"146","3","National Action Plan on Climate Change","The Government has also formulated the National Action Plan on Climate Change that provides for eight missions to help the country adapt to the effects of climate variability and change.",""],[1,"149","6","GDP Emissions Intensity Reduction Target","India has taken upon itself the voluntary target of reducing the emission intensity of its GDP by 20-25 per cent, over the 2005 levels, by 2020.",""],[1,"152","2","Economy-wide Modelling and Co-benefits Analysis for Low-Carbon Strategy","The final report of the Expert Group will include an economy-wide modelling, and an analysis of co-benefits in a cross-cutting framework.",""],[1,"152","2","Low-Carbon Strategy Policy Action Formulation","It will spell out the policy actions required to implement low carbon strategies up to 2030, and also suggest some finance strategies for the same.",""],[1,"152","2","Low-Carbon Finance Strategy Development","It will spell out the policy actions required to implement low carbon strategies up to 2030, and also suggest some finance strategies for the same.",""]],"40":[[1,"38","2","Science and Technology Innovation Promotion","This can be done by improving the business regulatory environment, strengthening the governance capacity of States, investing more in infrastructure rather than subsidies, and by using Science and Technology (S&T) to drive innovation.",""],[1,"53","6","Satellite-Based National Mapping and Resource Management Services","The Department of Space through satellite-based system has provided nationwide land use/land cover mapping for natural resources management, thematic mapping for national urban information system, the process of measuring forest and wasteland, locating potential drinking water zones and potential fishing zone and crop production forecasting.",""],[1,"53","6","Strengthened Science, Technology, and Innovation Drive","The Twelfth Five Year Plan must build on the scientific base created by earlier Plans and give a renewed thrust to emphasise creative and relevant research and innovation.",""],[1,"54","3","Science and Technology Solutions for Development Needs","Encourage and facilitate Indian Science to address the major developmental needs of the country like food security, energy and environ- mental needs, addressing the water challenges and providing technological solutions to afford- able health care requirements",""],[1,"54","4","Innovation Ecosystem Development for Global Competitiveness","Gain global competitiveness through a well- designed innovation ecosystem, encouraging global research centres of multinational corpora- tions (MNCs) to be set up in India.",""],[1,"54","5","Technology Partnership and Collaborative Research Initiative","it will be necessary to build technology partnerships with States and socio-economic ministries through new models of technological solutions, design, development and delivery.",""],[1,"54","5","Expanded Access to Global R&D Infrastructure","Indian researchers must also be able to gain access to the large global Research and Development (R&D) infrastructure and work in collaboration with others to develop necessary indigenous capabilities.",""],[1,"54","5","National Research Fund","there is a case for creating a new National Research Fund which can receive competing research proposals from different research institutions, or combinations of institutions, and select from these proposals to fund the most promising on a project basis.",""],[1,"54","5","Periodic Peer Review of Research Funding","Research funding for particular projects should be continued only on the basis of periodic peer reviews which indicate whether progress is satisfactory and also point to corrective steps which might help.",""],[1,"54","6","Science and Technology Outreach and Societal Linkage Framework","The Twelfth Plan envisages a more effective institutional framework in linking S&T with society through a variety of outreach strategies.",""],[1,"60","3","Strengthening Science and Technology Engagement Frameworks","non-financial aspects of India's engagement with the world need to be strength- ened.",""],[1,"60","3","Promotion of Global Research Collaboration and Partnerships","The need for more global collaboration and part- nerships in research on the part of our universities, research institutes and the corporate sector has been mentioned earlier.",""],[1,"148","9","Research and Development Funding Assistance","Research & Development (R&D) funding assistance paves the way for leveraging the knowledge of educational and research institutions to create technologies that can be viewed as cutting-edge and advanced.",""],[1,"148","9","Continuous Upgrading of Existing and New Institutions","Funds need to be allocated in a manner that both existing and infant institutions can be continuously upgraded.",""],[1,"269","5","Increase Research and Development Spending","The country needs to move up from investing 1 per cent of gross domestic product (GDP) in the R&D sector to 2 per cent of GDP and more, as has been the case with several developed and emerging economies for quite some time now.",""],[1,"270","2","Science, Technology and Innovation Policy Development","This calls for a well- enunciated Science, Technology and Innovation policy.",""],[1,"270","2","Innovation Deployment and Financing Mechanisms","It needs to evolve new delivery mechanisms for innovative deployment of technologies and business models for financing deployment of innovations.",""],[1,"270","2","National Innovation Ecosystem Development","The Twelfth Plan should therefore work to develop an ecosystem that addresses the national priority for sustainable, inclusive and accelerated growth taking along the education, research and corporate sectors.",""],[1,"270","2","Corporate Sector Research Capability Expansion","The corporate sector, in particu- lar, must play a much larger role in building research capability as happens in other countries.",""]],"41":[[1,"38","2","Infrastructure Investment Expansion","This can be done by improving the business regulatory environment, strengthening the governance capacity of States, investing more in infrastructure rather than subsidies, and by using Science and Technology (S&T) to drive innovation.",""],[1,"52","2","Infrastructure Investment Mobilization","The total investment in infrastructure sectors in the Twelfth Plan is estimated to be ₹55.7 lakh crore, which is roughly one trillion dollars at prevailing exchange rates.",""],[1,"52","2","Private Infrastructure Investment Expansion","It will have to increase to about 48 per cent during the Twelfth Plan if the infrastructure investment target is to be met.",""],[1,"52","2","Infrastructure Policy Initiatives Package","The committee has however qualified its projections as dependent on several policy initiatives that the government would need to take for ensuring this level of investment.",""],[1,"62","6","Private and Public Investment Revival Measures","This will require action to revive private investment, including private corpo- rate investment, and also action to stimulate public investment, especially in key areas of infrastructure especially, energy, transport, water supply and water resource management.",""],[1,"62","7","Infrastructure Investment Expansion","The Twelfth Plan aims to raise it further to 9 per cent of GDP by 2016-17.",""],[1,"80","3","Infrastructure Investment Expansion","A key component of the overall strategy for raising the rate of fixed investment is an increase in public and private investment in infrastructure.",""],[1,"91","6","Infrastructure Debt Fund Promotion Policy","That is an important objective of the policy to pro- mote infrastructure debt funds, which is now close to being in place.",""],[1,"91","7","Corporate Bond Market Development Efforts","In the Twelfth Plan, special efforts must be made to ensure that the corporate bond market takes off.",""],[1,"118","4","Expanded Public Sector Plan Coverage","the HLEC has recommended inclusion of investment outlays funded by IEBR of PSEs.",""],[1,"118","7","Enabling Environment for Infrastructure Investment","steps have been taken by the government to create an enabling environment to promote investment in infrastructure.",""],[1,"122","3","Twelfth Plan Infrastructure Investment Programme","The total public sector investment in infrastruc- ture envisaged in the Twelfth Plan is ₹16,01,061 crore by the Centre and ₹12,89,762 crore by the States.",""],[1,"122","4","Infrastructure Debt Financing Gap Measures","Measures would have to be taken for addressing this gap.",""],[1,"122","5","Infrastructure Investment Mobilization and Enabling Environment Reform","The projected investment in infrastructure over the Twelfth Plan would be possible only if there is a substantial expansion in internal generation and extra-budgetary resources of the public sector, in addition to a significant rise in private investment.",""],[1,"122","5","Enabling Policy and Regulatory Environment Strengthening","The scale of private investment would require a significant reinforcement of the enabling policy and regulatory environment.",""],[1,"125","6","Infrastructure Investment Enhancement Measures","It considers and decides on financial, institutional and legal measures required to enhance investment in infrastructure sectors.",""],[1,"126","1","Infrastructure Development Funds","With a view to overcoming these shortcomings, Infrastructure Development Funds (IDFs) are being set up for channelising long-term debt from domestic and foreign pension and insurance funds, as well as from other sources.",""],[1,"126","2","Infrastructure Debt Fund Refinancing Mechanism","the IDFs would refinance PPP projects after their construction is completed and operations have stabilised.",""],[1,"126","3","Infrastructure Project Long-Term Loan Financing Facility","Commission in 2006 for providing long-term loans for financing infrastructure projects that typically involve long gestation periods.",""],[1,"126","4","Infrastructure Finance Catalyst and Guarantee Facility","IIFCL is expected to graduate in the Twelfth Plan from the existing role of a normal lender to that of a catalyst mobilising additional resources for financing of infrastructure.",""],[1,"126","5","High Level Committee on Infrastructure Financing","In order to review the existing framework for financing of infrastructure and to make recommendations in this regard, a High Level Committee on Financing Infrastructure has been constituted.",""],[1,"132","4","Infrastructure Investment and Social Sector PPP Expansion","it is envis- aged that by the end of the Twelfth Plan, not only will there be ₹55,74,663 crore worth of investment in infrastructure sectors, but also that PPPs would have successfully forayed into the social sectors to promote universal access, while ensuring quality in the delivery of services.",""]],"42":[[1,"51","3","National Highway Development Programme Expansion through PPP Toll Roads","The National Highway Development Programme needs to be stepped up with an aggressive pursuit of PPP to construct toll roads on a Build-Operate-Transfer (BOT) basis.",""],[1,"51","3","State Road Programme Expansion for Universal Connectivity","The States too need to expand their road programmes to provide good quality connectivity in all areas.",""],[1,"51","4","Road Connectivity Acceleration in Special Category States","A special effort is needed to speed up road connectivity in Jammu & Kashmir, the North East and other Special Category States.",""],[1,"51","4","SARDP-NE Connectivity Programme","A good start has been made in the SARDP-NE in the Eleventh Plan and this needs to be pursued with greater vigour in the Twelfth Plan.",""],[1,"51","4","Enhanced North East Connectivity Priority","Enhanced connectivity of the North East should be a high priority.",""],[1,"69","19","All-Weather Village Road Connectivity Programme","Connect all villages with all-weather roads by the end of Twelfth Five Year Plan.",""],[1,"69","20","National and State Highway Upgrading Programme","Upgrade national and state highways to the minimum two-lane standard by the end of Twelfth Five Year Plan.",""],[1,"80","6","Accelerated Road Development Progress","We need to be able to accelerate the pace of progress in the coming years.",""],[1,"81","3","Road Network Reconditioning and Reconnection Initiative","We need to energise the reconditioning and reconnections of the other road networks through Moreh (Manipur) and Ledo (Assam) to Myanmar.",""],[1,"127","19","Model EPC Contract for Highways","The Planning Commission has published a model EPC contract for Highways.",""],[1,"127","19","Two-Lane National Highways Development under EPC Model","It is expected that about 20,000 km of two-lane National Highways would be developed under this model.",""],[1,"128","2","National Highway Development Project","The National Highway Development Project (NHDP), covering a length of about 54,000 km of highways, is India's largest road development programme in its history.",""],[1,"128","2","Public-Private Partnership Financing for Highway Upgrading","The government has encouraged increased private sector participation in upgrading the arterial road network of the country to world class standards.",""],[1,"128","2","National Highway Expansion and Upgrading Projects","With several key projects on the anvil spanning a length of about 45,000 km (including six-laning of four-laned roads, expressways and port connectivity projects) and a large number of projects in States, there are increas- ing opportunities for the domestic and foreign players in the sector.",""],[1,"128","2","National Highway Widening to Two-Lane Standard","The government has decided to widen 20,000 km of less than two-lane National Highways to two-lane standard in the EPC mode.",""]],"43":[[1,"51","5","Railway Capacity Development and Modernisation","Development of capability in the Railways is another urgent priority for the Twelfth Plan.",""],[1,"51","5","Railway Technological Modernisation","Expansion of the system must be accompanied by technological modernisation, greater attention to safety and steps to ensure financial viability.",""],[1,"51","5","Western and Eastern Freight Corridor Projects","These include flagship projects such as the Western and Eastern Freight",""],[1,"69","21","Dedicated Freight Corridors Completion","Complete Eastern and Western Dedicated Freight Corridors by the end of Twelfth Five Year Plan.",""],[1,"80","6","Railway Capacity Expansion and Modernisation","The Railways also require considerable investment to achieve the expansion in capacity needed and also to modernise and improve safety.",""],[1,"80","6","Railway Internal Resource Mobilisation Reform","other investments are constrained by inadequacies of internal resources of the Railways, largely the consequence of frozen and uneconomic tariffs on passenger routes.",""],[1,"80","6","Customer-Oriented Railway Service Adaptation","transport services need to adapt more to customer require- ments.",""],[1,"126","13","Procurement-cum-Maintenance Agreement for Locomotives","Procurement-cum-Maintenance Agreement for Loco- motives",""],[1,"129","5","Dedicated Freight Corridor Implementation","Dedicated Freight Corridor Corporation of India Limited (DFCCIL) has been set up for implementing the Dedicated Freight project and the Ministry of Railways would explore the possibilities of attracting private investment in some segments of this project.","Ministry of Railways"],[1,"129","5","Railway Station Redevelopment Programme","Indian Railways has decided to redevelop 50 railway stations in the metropolitan cities and major tourist centers like Delhi, Jaipur, Chandigarh, Patna, Bypanahalli, Bhubneshwar, Mumbai CST, Howrah and so on as world-class stations through PPP.","Indian Railways"],[1,"129","5","Rail Manufacturing Production Units through PPP","The proposal to set up of production units for manufacturing of electric and diesel locomotives at Madhepura and Marhowra respectively and passenger coaches at Kanchrapara through PPP has already been approved.",""],[1,"129","5","Private Sector Access to Container Train Operations","Further, movement of container trains has already been opened to the private sector, and this has acquired more than 25 per cent share of the market.",""]],"44":[[1,"51","7","Airport Expansion and International Hub Development","There is need for further expansion in the Twelfth Plan with the creative use of PPP wherever possible.",""],[1,"80","7","New Mumbai International Airport Bidding Process","The New Mumbai International Airport is yet to be bid out.",""],[1,"81","1","Airport Development and Modernization Programme","There are many other air- ports, small and big, that need to be developed in the Twelfth Plan.",""],[1,"81","1","Private Partnership Framework for Airport Projects","The rest need to be taken up, if possible with private partners.",""],[1,"81","1","Small Airport and Landing Strip Connectivity Framework","we have to work out a framework for executing of these projects and also develop a sub-model for air transport linkages to these dispersed and smaller airports.",""],[1,"128","5","Airport Operations and Maintenance PPP Programme","Airports Authority of India has identified 15 operational Airports for taking up operation and maintenance of both terminal and air side through PPP.",""],[1,"128","5","PPP Management and Operation of Large Airports","It is, therefore, recommended that these large airports should be awarded under the PPP mode for their management and operation.",""],[1,"128","6","Greenfield Airport Development through PPP","Five green field airports including Navi Mumbai, Goa, Kannur, Chandigarh and Kota have been identified for development through PPP.",""],[1,"128","6","Model Concession Agreement for Greenfield Airports","For building and operating a Greenfield airport on PPP basis, a precise policy and regulatory framework has now been spelt out in the Model Concession Agreement for Greenfield Airports.",""]],"45":[[1,"81","2","Inland Water Transport Acceleration","Inland water transport has been neglected and needs to be accelerated.",""],[1,"81","3","Multi-Modal Connectivity Corridor Development","We are working on a multi-modal connection through Ashuganj in Bangladesh to Tripura and the Sithwe-Kaladan River Project to Lunglei in Mizoram.",""]],"46":[[1,"128","7","Private Sector Participation in Urban Infrastructure","Private sector participation needs to be encouraged in urban infrastructure sectors like water supply and sewerage and solid waste management.",""],[1,"128","7","Private Sector Delivery of Urban Transport Infrastructure","In urban transport, private sector can provide more efficient transport services, construct and maintain modern bus terminals with commercial complexes, over bridges, city roads and so on.",""],[1,"128","7","PPP-Based Metro Rail Development","PPP initiatives are also being undertaken to develop metro rail systems in Indian cities (refer to Box 3.6 for details on Hyderabad Metro Rail Project).",""],[1,"129","1","Hyderabad Metro Rail Project","Hyderabad Metro Rail Project is presently under construction on PPP mode with a total project cost of ₹12,132 crore.",""],[1,"129","5","Mumbai Elevated Metro Rail Project","Construction of an elevated metro rail project in Mumbai is being undertaken through PPP.",""]],"47":[[1,"56","1","Land Acquisition Relief and Rehabilitation Bill","the government has introduced the Land Acquisition Relief and Rehabilitation Bill in Parliament which is expected to create a much more balanced framework protecting the rights of those whose land is being acquired, as well as those whose livelihood will be disrupted.",""],[1,"230","1","Fair Land Acquisition Law with Resettlement and Rehabilitation Provisions","there is need for a fair land acquisition law which resorts to compulsory acquisition only where it is unavoidable and in a manner that seeks assessment of social impact as participatory as possible, while also ensuring that both fair compensation and Resettlement and Rehabilitation of the dislocated persons.",""],[1,"230","5","Mandatory Resettlement and Rehabilitation Provisions","Resettlement & Rehabilitation (R&R) provisions must be made mandatory and not reduced to what they have generally tended to become-condi-tionalities without consequences.",""],[1,"230","5","Livelihood Reconstruction for Project-Affected People","We also require an unequivocal commitment to imaginatively explore ways of rebuilding the livelihoods of those adversely affected by development projects.",""],[1,"230","7","Transparent and Flexible Land Market Regulation and Enforcement","That is why there has to be a role for the government-to put in place, a transparent and flexible set of rules and regulations, and to ensure its enforcement.",""],[1,"230","8","Land Acquisition Compensation and Resettlement Bill","Government is in the final stages of formulating The Right to Fair Compensation, Resettlement, Rehabilitation and Transparency in Land Acquisition Bill.",""],[1,"231","1","Transparent and Time-Bound Land Acquisition for Public Purposes","that land acquisition for vital public purposes happens in a manner that is judicious, transparent and time-bound, so that public purposes can be served in an expeditious and efficient manner.",""],[1,"231","2","Land Legislation Reform","The Bill is a milestone in legislation that should lead to a reduction in instances of perceived injustices that have played a major role in fuelling Maoism.",""],[1,"231","2","Land Market Functioning Improvement","by improving the functioning of the land market, it should lead to an upgrading of the overall investment climate in the country.",""],[1,"231","5","Land Provision for Public Infrastructure Projects","Land for railways, highways, ports, power and irrigation purposes for use by Government and public sector companies or corporations;",""],[1,"231","13","Public-Private Partnership Projects for Public Goods and Services","PPP projects for the production of public goods or the provision of public services for physical infrastructure, social infrastructure and human development projects including those involving the production of intermedi- ate goods and services for these purposes.",""],[1,"231","14","Land Allocation for Public Goods and Service Delivery Projects","Land for private companies for the production of public goods or provision of public services for physical infrastructure, social infrastructure and human development projects including those involving the production of intermediate goods and services for these purposes.",""],[1,"231","15","Informed Consent Process for Project Affected Families","Under categories (7) and (8), consent of at least 80 per cent of the landowning Project Affected Families (PAFs) is sought to be obtained through an informed process as outlined in the Bill.",""],[1,"231","15","Government Land Ownership Framework for PPP Projects","Under PPP projects, ownership of land will continue to vest with Government so that the PPP framework can apply.",""],[1,"231","16","Fair Land Acquisition Compensation and R&R Provisions","In each case of land acquisition, fair compensation and R&R provisions as laid out in the Bill will apply.",""],[1,"231","17","Exemption for Linear Infrastructure Projects from Agricultural Land Acquisition Restrictions","These restrictions shall not apply in the case of linear projects (such as railways, highways, major district roads, power and telegraph lines and irrigation canals)",""],[1,"231","21","Project-Affected Family Compensation and Employment Package","Where jobs are created through the project, mandatory employment for one member per affected family or (ii) ₹5 lakhs per family or (iii) ₹2,000 per month per family as annuity for 20 years, with appropriate index for inflation.",""],[1,"231","22","Rural and Urban House Replacement Housing Support","If a house is lost in rural areas, a constructed house shall be provided as per the Indira Awas Yojana specifications.",""],[1,"232","2","Land Allocation for Irrigation Project Affected Families","One acre of land to each family in the command area, if land is acquired for an irrigation project",""],[1,"232","5","Expanded Rehabilitation and Resettlement Package for Affected Families","The Bill also seeks to provide the same R&R package to affected families on sale/purchase of land where sale/purchase exceeds a certain threshold.",""],[1,"232","6","Resettlement Area Infrastructure Provision","25 infrastructural amenities are to be provided in the resettlement area, including schools and play- grounds, health centres, roads and electric connec- tions, assured sources of safe drinking water for each family, panchayat ghars, Anganwadis, places of wor- ship and burial and/or cremation ground, village level post offices, as appropriate, with facilities for opening saving accounts, Fair Price shops and seed- cum-fertiliser storage facilities and so on.",""],[1,"232","7","Resettlement and Rehabilitation Time-Bound Delivery Framework","In order to avoid delays, stringent time-lines have been set.",""]],"48":[[1,"154","2","National Wind Energy Mission","We need to set up a National Wind Energy Mission, similar to the National Solar Mission for effective formulation and implementation of policies both at the National and State levels.",""],[1,"154","4","Mixed Land Use Policy for Wind Power and Agriculture","Land tenure policies that will encourage mixed land use for wind generation and agriculture (without having to pay commercial rents that will increase the cost of wind power).",""],[1,"154","5","Revision of Bidding Models and Feed-in Tariff Regulation","The bidding models currently being pursued need to be revisited, so that farmers, wherever willing, are able to benefit from mixed land use and a cost- plus approach can be used to determine feed-in tariffs provided it is done through an independent regulator.",""],[1,"154","6","National Clean Energy Fund Financing for Local Grid Development","Mechanisms for using the National Clean Energy Fund (NCEF) to finance development of local grids by state distribution companies that will help evacuate wind power and solve the load curve problems on the supply side.",""],[1,"154","7","Pumped Hydropower Storage Development","Prioritise the development of pumped hydro stor- age, which may be suitable for complementing wind power.",""],[1,"154","8","Energy Storage Research and Development Investment","Invest in R&D in energy storage options that can provide backup for longer durations, like compressed air and high power density batteries among others.",""],[1,"154","9","Offshore Wind Viability and Risk Studies","It is also important to undertake studies to examine the economic viability and risks associated with off-shore wind in the Indian conditions.",""]],"49":[[1,"171","5","Forest and Tree Cover Enhancement and Quality Improvement","The national strategy aims at enhancing and improving the quality of forest and tree cover, which in turn will",""],[1,"172","2","Forestry Carbon Sequestration and Ecosystem Services Actions","Strategy proposed to realize enhanced potential of forestry sector in mitigation and adaptation should therefore be two pronged-first, focus on actions that promote carbon sequestration; and second, focus on actions that improve and enhance ecosystem goods and services.",""],[1,"172","7","National Mission for a Green India","National Mission for a Green India.",""],[1,"172","11","Substitution of Energy-Intensive Building Materials with Lumber","Initiate part replacement of energy intensive building materials like cement, iron and steel with lumber.",""],[1,"172","12","Office and Domestic Furniture Replacement Initiative","Initiate part replacement of office and domes- tic furniture made with metals by commercial wood based furniture.",""],[1,"172","13","National Afforestation Programme","Our present initiatives like National Afforestation Programme (NAP), together with programmes in sectors like agriculture and rural development, are adding or improving about 1 mha of forest and tree cover annually in our country.",""],[1,"172","14","National Mission for a Green India","In the Twelfth Plan, the national afforestation programme needs to be re-organized into a more comprehensive 'National Mission for a Green India' (for details see Chapter 7).",""],[1,"172","15","Green India Mission","if implemented properly, the Green India Mission would help neutralize an additional 1.5 per cent of India's GHG emissions annually, bringing the total GHG removal by India's forests to 6 per cent by 2020.",""],[1,"237","4","Afforestation and Livelihood Improvement Schemes","Similarly, afforestation work including rehabilita- tion and livelihood improvement activities can be taken up under the schemes of Ministries of Rural Development (MoRD), Agriculture, Tribal Affairs, Panchayat Raj, Renewable Energy and so on.","Ministries of Rural Development (MoRD), Agriculture, Tribal Affairs, Panchayat Raj, Renewable Energy"],[1,"239","16","Green India Mission Operationalisation","The Green India Mission under NAPCC is going to be operationalised in 2012-13.",""],[1,"243","5","Green India Mission Afforestation and Eco-restoration Programme","Greening 5 million ha under Green India Mission including 1.5 million ha of degraded lands, afforestation and eco- restoration of 0.9 million ha of ecologically sensitive areas.",""],[1,"250","16","National Afforestation Programme","National Afforestation programme",""],[1,"254","6","National Mission for a Green India","The Government of India has taken initiatives by formulating National Mission for a Green India (GIM) as one of the 8 missions under the National Action Plan on Climate Change (NAPCC).",""],[1,"254","7","Green India Mission","During the Twelfth Five Year Plan, provisions have been kept for the GIM for increasing forest and tree cover on 2.5 mha area (non forest through agro/social/farm forestry), improv- ing quality of forest cover on another 2.5 mha area, improving ecosystems services, and increasing forest based livelihood income and enhanced annual CO2 sequestration.",""],[1,"258","8","Green India Mission","the GIM launched during the Eleventh Plan period envisages a combination of adaptation and mitigation measures aimed at (i) enhancing carbon sinks in sustainably managed forests and other ecosystems, (ii) adaptation of vulnerable species/ecosystems to the changing climate patterns and (iii) adaptation of the forest-dependent communities.",""],[1,"259","1","Forest and Tree Cover Expansion Programme","and tree cover by 10 mha of forest/non-forest lands through (i) qualitative improvement of forest cover/ ecosystem in moderately dense forests (1.5 mha), open degraded forests (3 mha), degraded grassland (0.4 mha) and wetlands 0.1 mha; (ii) eco-restoration/ afforestation of scrub, shifting cultivation areas, cold deserts, mangroves, ravines and abandoned mining areas (2 m ha); (iii) bringing urban/ peri-urban lands under forest and tree cover (0.20 mha); and d) agro- forestry/social forestry (3 mha).",""],[1,"259","2","REDD+ Programme for Sustainable Forest Management","The GIM should also aim at implementing a programme of Reduced Emissions from Deforestation and Forest Degradation (REDD+) that ensures sustainable management of forests.",""],[1,"259","3","Afforestation Programme Convergence and Regulatory Reform","one of the keys to success of this Mission will be its ability to establish convergence with other flag- ship programmes like MGNREGA, Compensatory Afforestation Fund Management and Planning Authority (CAMPA) and National Action Plan (NAP); and effecting regulatory improvements that help local communities access and benefit from local forest resources.",""],[1,"261","7","Green India Mission Afforestation Programme","The Green India Mission focuses on enhancing eco-system services and carbon sinks through afforestation on degraded forest land, in line with the national policy of expanding the forest and tree cover in the country and improving the quality of forests.",""],[1,"263","20","Forest Planning and REDD+ Programme","Forests: Forest planning and development of programmes that will minimise the adverse impact of climate vulnerability and change, implement REDD+ activities programme.",""],[1,"266","11","Green India Mission Funding","From the Fund, allocation of ₹200 crore has already been proposed for environment remediation pro- grammes and another ₹200 crore for the Green India Mission.",""]],"50":[[1,"150","7","Hill Area Development Programme Continuation","The Hill Area Development Programme (HADP) and the Western Ghats Development Programme (WGDP) need to be continued in the Twelfth Plan with renewed vigour so that natural resources of these fragile areas can be preserved and used in a more sustainable manner.",""],[1,"150","7","Priority Support for Hill Area Flagship Services","These areas deserve high priority under the flagship programmes, particularly Sarva Shiksha Abhiyan (SSA) and the National Health Mission (NHM).",""],[1,"150","7","Local Solutions and People's Participation in Hill Area Planning","Local solutions and people's participation in decision-making need to be encouraged.",""],[1,"150","7","Sustainable Livelihoods for Fragile Hill Communities","The programme should therefore have a twofold objective of preserving ecological balance and creating sustainable livelihood opportunities for the local communities.",""],[1,"150","7","Increased Funding for Western Ghats Fragile Pockets","The highly fragile and backward pockets of the Western Ghats should be allocated more funds by the respective State Governments.",""],[1,"150","8","Sixth Schedule Inclusion of Darjeeling Gorkha Hill Council Area","The Bill to include the Darjeeling Gorkha Hill Council Area in the Sixth Schedule needs to be expeditiously considered.",""],[1,"150","8","GBPIHED Activity Reorientation","the G.B. Pant Institute for Himalayan Environment and Develop- ment (GBPIHED) should reorient its activities to",""],[1,"360","1","Hill Area Development and Watershed Development Programmes","The HADP/WGDP has been in operation since the Fifth Five Year Plan in identified hill areas.",""],[1,"360","1","Eco-preservation and Eco-restoration for Biodiversity Conservation","The main objectives of both programmes are eco-preservation and eco-restoration with a focus on sustainable use of biodiversity.",""],[1,"360","1","Community Participation in Biodiversity Conservation and Sustainable Livelihoods","They also focus on the needs and aspirations of local communities, particularly their participation in the design and implementation of the strategies for conservation of bio-diversity and sustainable livelihoods.",""],[1,"360","7","Western Ghats Development Programme","Currently, the programme is being implemented in 175 talu- kas (Maharashtra-63, Karnataka-40, Kerala-36, Tamil Nadu-33 and Goa-3).",""],[1,"360","8","Livelihood Schemes under SCA","The SCA may be used for livelihood schemes which preserve and even increase productivity without disturbing the environment such as, minor forest produce, afforestation, horticulture, pisciculture, and so on.",""],[1,"360","8","Watershed Scheme Cost Norm Alignment","In the case of watershed schemes, the cost norms of the guidelines of the NRAA/Ministry of Rural Development may be followed.","NRAA/Ministry of Rural Development"]],"51":[[1,"150","3","Focused Urban Environmental Pollution Remediation under JNNURM","The Jawaharlal Nehru National Urban Renewal Mission (JNNURM) needs a more focused approach over the Twelfth Plan period so that we resolve these issues at the earliest.",""],[1,"237","4","Integrated Urban River Cleaning Programme","Programmes such as the JNNURM and National River Conservation Programme (NRCP) need to be effectively combined to achieve the target of rivers cleaning.",""],[1,"237","4","Urban Waste Management System Development","The MoUD should also ensure creation of required waste management system in all urban local bodies.","MoUD"],[1,"237","4","Resource Dovetailing for Environmental Investment","There is thus, considerable potential for dovetailing of resources with the schemes of several Ministries and an attempt could also be made for earmarking of resources under these Ministries for investment in environment and greening of the country.","several Ministries"],[1,"238","5","Replication of Integrated Solid Waste Management Model in Cities","In the Twelfth Five Year Plan, every attempt will be made to replicate the similar model in maximum number of cities in the country.",""],[1,"240","3","Taj Protection Scheme Revival","it is proposed to revive the scheme in the Twelfth Five Year Plan.","National Environmental Engineering Research Institute (NEERI), Nagpur"],[1,"242","13","Cumulative Environmental Impact Assessments for Vulnerable Regions","Cumulative environmental impact assessments for vulnerable regions;",""],[1,"242","15","Water Resource Pollution Control","Maintaining acceptable water quality and quantity through pollution control of water resources;",""],[1,"242","17","Industrial and Commercial Wastewater Discharge Management","Management of waste water discharge from industrial and commercial establishments into major water bodies.",""],[1,"243","1","Contaminated Site Assessment and Remediation Programme","Assess and remediate 12 identified contaminated sites (hazardous chemicals and wastes) with potential for ground water contamination by 2017.",""],[1,"244","1","Epidemiological Health Impact Studies","Epidemiological studies to assess improvement in health status due to better management of environment and ecology.",""],[1,"245","3","Environmental Conservation and Pollution Control Strategy","The Twelfth Five Year Plan adopts specific strategies to meet emerging challenges concerning conservation and assessment of flora, fauna, forests and wildlife; prevention and control of pollution; afforestation and regeneration of degraded areas; protection of the environment; and issues related to the welfare of animals",""],[1,"245","9","Pollution Control and Waste Management Reform","Reforms would be carried out against the backdrop of the exponential expansion of the powers and functions of the existing authorities.",""],[1,"247","4","Load-Based Standards for Cumulative Environmental Impact Assessment","Implementation of load-based standards to facili- tate carrying capacity based cumulative EIAs, particularly for areas having concentration of developmental activities such as mines and ther- mal power plants;",""],[1,"247","5","Polluter Pays Principle Enforcement","Effective enforcement of the application of 'pol- luter pays' principle;",""],[1,"247","6","Cleaner Technologies Development and Deployment for MSMEs","Development and deployment of cleaner technol- ogies in the Micro, Small and Medium Enterprises (MSMEs) sector, particularly for the 17 categories of highly polluting industries;",""],[1,"247","7","National Water Quality Monitoring Network Strengthening","Strengthening of the National Water Quality Monitoring Network;",""],[1,"247","8","Coastal Water Quality Data Collection and Monitoring","Collection and monitoring of basic data on coastal water quality, oxygen zone in the sea, transport of nitrogen and phosphorous in the rivers from agriculture;",""],[1,"247","9","Policy Review and Effluent Standards Reform","Review of existing policies to enable drafting of mitigation strategies and introduction of new effluent standards;",""],[1,"247","10","Continuous Effluent Monitoring Systems","Implementation of continuous effluent monitor- ing systems at industries as well as CETPs.",""],[1,"247","11","National Environmental Monitoring Programme","It is proposed to setup a National Environ- mental Monitoring Programme (NEMP) for moni- toring forests, air and water quality, river and ocean pollution, noise and so on with sharing of real-time data from local to national levels which will also help in monitoring change.",""],[1,"247","13","Environmental Clearance Granting and Monitoring Strengthening","strengthening the processes for grant of environmen- tal clearances and monitoring thereof.",""],[1,"247","16","Continuous Online Air Quality Monitoring System","Promotion of continuous 24 x 7 online air quality monitoring which includes Continuous Ambient Air Quality Monitoring Stations (CAAQMS) and Continuous Emission Monitoring System (CEMS);",""],[1,"247","17","Bioremediation for Water Body Pollution Control","Introduction of cost-effective technologies such as bioremediation to address the pollution of water bodies is proposed;",""],[1,"247","18","Hazardous Waste Co-processing in Industrial Plants","Encouragement of use of hazardous waste of high calorific value in cement kilns, power or steel plants as a safe alternative to conventional incineration;",""],[1,"247","19","Environmental Cost-Benefit Analysis for Public Investment Decisions","Integration of environmental attributes into cost- benefit analysis while making public investment decisions, to encourage more efficient allocation of resources;",""],[1,"247","20","Environmental Pollution Charges and Incentive Fund","Amendment to the environmental laws to intro- duce pollution charges and other economic instruments to enable creation of fund in order to augment allocation to the sector.",""],[1,"247","21","National Environment Restoration Fund","Creation of a National Environment Restoration Fund (NERF) from voluntary contributions and the net proceeds of proposed economic instru- ments such as user fees for access to specified natural resources.",""],[1,"248","1","Environmental Resource Restoration and Contaminated Site Cleanup","restoration of environmental resources and clean- up of sites contaminated with toxic and hazardous waste;",""],[1,"251","2","Revision of CETP Guidelines and Scheme Recasting Strategy","the extant guidelines of CETPs are proposed to be revised for enforcement during the Twelfth Plan period.",""],[1,"251","2","Industrial Cluster Inventorization and CETP Demand Assessment Study","CPCB has initiated a study for 'Inventorization of industrial clusters in the country and assessment of the unmet demand for CETPs'.","CPCB"],[1,"251","2","Prioritized CETP Planning and Scheme Recasting","Based on the recommendations of the study, a prioritised list of required CETPs will be prepared and a strategy will be formulated for recasting of the existing scheme.",""],[1,"251","2","Sub-scheme for Environment Protection in Leather Complexes","A sub-scheme for Environment Protection was introduced for upgradation of CETPs in leather complexes in the Eleventh Plan by Department of Industrial Policy and Promotion (DIPP) which is to be strengthened during the Twelfth Plan.","Department of Industrial Policy and Promotion (DIPP)"]],"52":[[1,"186","9","Water Resources Information System Development","The Ministry of Water Resources has initiated the process of development of a Water Resources Information System (WRIS).","Ministry of Water Resources"],[1,"186","9","MMI Data Compilation and Verification System","Implementation of the NIMF will necessitate compilation of accurate statistics on area irrigated by MMI systems as well as ISF collected from farmers.",""],[1,"186","9","MMI Information, Planning and Control System","Such a data base will be the foundation of an information, planning and control system for improved management of MMI systems.",""],[1,"206","6","Working Group on Water Data Review and Action Programme","the Planning Commission decided to constitute, for the first time, a Working Group headed by Prof. A. Vaidyanathan (former Member, Planning Commission) to carry out a comprehensive and critical review of the present system for collection and dissemination of water related data, identify deficiencies in the data being generated and used for planning and policy, and to suggest a programme of action to overcome them.",""],[1,"207","5","Phased Data Improvement and Institutional Reform Programme","The Working Group spells out a concrete programme for phased action to improve physical facilities, methodologies and mechanisms to gener- ate more comprehensive, detailed and reliable data in each of these respects and outlines changes needed in institutional arrangements for collection valida- tion and dissemination of data and for facilitating intensive analysis through research.",""],[1,"207","7","National Data Improvement Institutional Arrangements","It is, therefore, suggested by the Working Group that the Central Government take the lead in creating appropriate institutional arrangements to ensure independent and professional conduct of the surveys, providing financial and technical support to the States and ensuring that all agencies follow prescribed protocols and transmit the data to the central pool.",""],[1,"207","8","Comprehensive Water Resources Database Development","the strategy, modalities and funding for building a comprehensive, technical and scientific data base on potential and utilisable water from different sources;",""],[1,"207","10","Integrated and Digitised National Water Resources Information System","the design of an integrated and digitised National Water Resources Information System by suitably expanding, reorganising and equipping the exist- ing WRIS in the CWC.",""],[1,"207","11","Data Improvement and Resource Assessment Facilities Upgrade","The bulk of the expenditure on the pro- grammes for data improvement in the Twelfth Plan will be for expanding and upgrading facili- ties for assessment of resource potential and utilisa- tion ..",""],[1,"207","11","Ground-Level Sample Survey Programme","Sample surveys to assess actual performance and impact of schemes at the ground level will be a small but critical component.",""],[1,"214","8","Water Resources Information System Development","The Central Sector Outlay for Twelfth Five Year Plan for Ministry of Water Resources (MoWR) is ₹18,118 crore, which envisages schemes like Irrigation Management Programme, Development of Water Resources Information System, Ground Water Management and Regulation (including aqui- fer mapping) and so on.","Ministry of Water Resources (MoWR)"],[1,"220","7","National Evapotranspiration and Soil Moisture Monitoring Network","an adequate set of lysimeters equipped with digital/ load cells, and data logger and GPRS transmission facility needs to be installed, and data collected and analysed to enhance the reliability of agro-met advisories on irrigation scheduling.",""],[1,"220","9","Gridded PET Data Availability and Soil Moisture Assessment","PET data will also be made available on 1° x 1° grid.",""],[1,"220","11","Inclusive Soil Moisture Monitoring System","The efforts undertaken by various agricultural universities and the agro-met divisions of the State remote sensing applica- tions centres (including the NRSC Agro-met Division) in terms of soil moisture monitoring need to be made inclusive and not limited to the 'research' domain.",""],[1,"221","1","Augmentation of Observational and Predictive Capabilities","The Twelfth Plan augmentation in observational and predictive capabilities will make the ensuing studies for other river basins more accurate and reliable.",""],[1,"221","3","Hydrological Observation Station Expansion and Upgradation","Expansion and up-gradation of the existing 878 hydrological observation (HO) stations and supporting infrastructure in site offices for repair and maintenance.",""],[1,"221","4","Expansion of Hydrological Observation Stations","1917 additional HO stations will be opened in order to meet the minimum requirement of HO stations",""],[1,"221","6","Glacial Lake Monitoring and Snow-Melt Forecasting System","Facilities for monitoring glacial lakes/water bodies and snow-melt forecasting in the Himalayan region.",""],[1,"221","7","Telemetric Reservoir Monitoring Expansion","Expansion in the number of reservoirs equipped for telemetric monitoring of reservoir water level and live storage.",""],[1,"221","8","Coastal Management Information System","Creating a Coastal Management Information System (CMIS) for collecting data on various natural phenomena occurring in coastal regions, and for appraisal and monitoring of projects for their protection.",""],[1,"221","9","National Water Resources Information Centre Establishment","Setting up a new organisation, namely National Water Resources Information Centre (NWR-IC), comprising professionals with specialised expertise in water resources, GIS, remote sensing, computer science and other related disciplines to manage the large volume of data on water resources and allied fields generated under India-WRIS project and also to update periodically for proper decision-making.",""],[1,"221","10","In-House Digital Data Management Capacity Upgrade","Strengthening in-house facilities for upgrading capacity for digitised management and dissemination of data.",""],[1,"222","3","Watershed Rainfall Utilization Studies","Commission properly planned and scientifically rigorous studies of current utilisation of local rainfall and potential for its fuller, more effective use in selected watershed in different agro-climatic regimes.",""],[1,"222","4","National Water Data Collation and Sharing System","It is essential to persuade/incentivise state agencies to collate both current and past series data from their records and make them available to the national data pool for scientific analysis and policy oriented research.",""],[1,"222","5","Water Delivery Measurement Infrastructure Upgrade","Upgrade physical infrastructure for measuring water deliveries in all major public water supply systems.",""],[1,"222","6","Central Data Recording and Validation System","Rationalise the system for recording and validation of these data and reporting them to a central pool.",""],[1,"222","14","Groundwater Extraction and Use Sample Survey Programme","The only way to get reliable estimates of actual groundwater extraction and use, is through sample surveys of all types of wells in rural and urban areas, distinguishing between wells which are primarily for irrigation as a sole source and used conjunctively with surface water, and those which are used primarily as a sources of domestic, commercial and different non-agricultural uses.",""],[1,"222","16","Household Survey Programme","These surveys will be entrusted to a consortium of government and non-governmental research institutions with experience in such studies who will use well-defined common concepts and methodologies to ensure comparability across regions and over time.",""]],"53":[[1,"237","2","Constitutional Amendment to Make Environment a Concurrent Subject","there is a need to include 'environment' as a concurrent subject in the constitution.",""],[1,"237","2","Environmental and Forestry Scheme Implementation Oversight","It is mandated with the responsibility of planning, promotion, cooperation and overseeing the implementation of various environmental and forestry schemes/programmes.","MoEF"],[1,"237","2","Environmental Protection and Pollution Control Programme","The main objectives of the MoEF include protection of the environment; conservation and survey of flora, fauna, forests and wildlife; prevention and control of pollution; afforestation and regeneration of degraded areas; ensuring welfare of animals; and international cooperation in forestry and environment.","MoEF"],[1,"237","2","Environmental Management for Health and Livelihoods","MoEF is also concerned with environmental management: to promote health considerations; to focus on poverty alleviation by enhancing access to poor of natural resources for livelihood; and to enhance the awareness regarding environmentally sound living process by focusing on nature-man synergy.","MoEF"],[1,"237","2","UNEP and International Mountain Development Nodal Coordination","MoEF is also designated as the nodal agency for the United Nations Environment Programme (UNEP) and the International Centre for Integrated Mountain Development and looks after the follow-up of the United Nations Conference on Environment and Development (UNCED).","MoEF"],[1,"237","5","Environmental Governance Legislative and Institutional Strengthening","International commitments in various sectors and their compliance through new laws, institutions of enforcement and programmes of action also impact environmental governance.",""],[1,"237","5","Mainstreaming Environmental Imperatives into Development Decisions","the National Environmental Policy has the object of ensuring that all developmental decisions duly recognise and take into account the environmental imperatives of conservation and sustainable development.",""],[1,"239","6","Environmental Impact Assessment Process Reform","The Environmental Impact Assessment (EIA) process has been made more efficient, decentralised and transparent, based on a comprehensive review of the existing environmental process and its re-engineering through the EIA Notification, 2006, and its amendments thereafter.",""],[1,"239","6","Mandatory EIA and EMP Consultant Accreditation System","A system of mandatory accreditation of EIA/Environmental Management Plan (EMP) consultants has also been introduced to improve the quality of impact assessment reports submitted by project proponents.",""],[1,"239","14","National Environment Assessment and Monitoring Authority","an exercise has been initiated to conceptualise and constitute a National Environment Assessment & Monitoring Authority (NEAMA).",""],[1,"244","2","Cleaner Technology Promotion and Environmental Governance Reform","Promotion and adoption of cleaner technology, strengthening and initiation of reforms in regulations, policy making and enforcement institutions for environmental governance.",""],[1,"245","4","State Departments of Environment Establishment","institutional mechanisms like establishment of a Department of Environment in the States for environmental management to resolve inter-sectoral issues needs to be addressed on priority.",""],[1,"245","4","Inter-Ministerial Standing Committees and Working Groups","Inter-ministerial Standing Committees and Working Groups in specific domains within broad areas like air quality management and waste man- agement need to be established both at the Central and State Government levels",""],[1,"245","5","National Environment and Forestry Council","It is proposed to set up a high powered body called the National Environment and Forestry Council (NEFC) with the Prime Minister as Chairperson, the Minister of Environment and Forests as Vice Chairperson, aided and advised by a group of experts.",""],[1,"245","7","State Environment and Forest Council Establishment","a high-pow- ered body called State Environment and Forest Council (SEFC) needs to be constituted to align the working of the other Departments with that of the Department of Environment and Forests in each State.",""],[1,"245","7","Environment Cells in Ministries and Departments","Environment Cells have to be constituted in the related Ministries and Departments at the Central and State levels so as to mainstream environmental concerns in their activi- ties and programmes.",""],[1,"245","8","Comprehensive Environmental and Biodiversity Law Reform","A comprehensive review and reform of laws concerning Environment, Forests, Wildlife and Biodiversity will be undertaken in the Twelfth Plan in order to make them more effective, work in harmony with each other and address new challenges.",""],[1,"245","9","National Environment Protection Authority Establishment","A National Environment Protection Authority (NEPA) is also proposed to be set up fully empowered to restructure the existing environmental management regime.",""],[1,"245","10","Environmental Clearance and Notification Reform","Reforms would be attempted to make the system more effective and to evolve better proactive legislative and administrative measures for:",""],[1,"246","1","Strengthening Impact Assessment and Coastal Laws","Strengthening the Impact Assessment Law and coastal laws by making local authorities more responsible and accountable;",""],[1,"246","3","Liability Regime Implementation","Giving effect to the new Liability Regime to which India has committed itself (2010 UNEP Guidelines on Liability, Response Action and Compensation for Environmentally Harmful Activities-a new legal regime that will have far-reaching implications on all perceivable development activities and the actors engaged in them, without exception);",""],[1,"246","6","Bio-safety Legal Safeguards Reform","Evolving effective and robust legal safeguards for addressing the issue of 'bio-safety';",""],[1,"246","9","CBD COP Presidency and Biodiversity Commitments","Ensuring that India receives international recognition as the president of the CoP of the Convention on Biological Diversity (CBD) starting from 2012 in compliance with its international commitments over biodiversity issues (primarily over bio-safety, conservation of TK, equity, benefit-sharing and so on);",""],[1,"246","10","Legal Harmonization with PESA in the Sector","Developing harmony in the working of laws in the sector with the Panchayat Extension to Scheduled Areas Act, 1996.",""],[1,"246","11","Environmental Regulation Capacity Building and Clearance System Professionalisation","A multi-pronged approach to environmental regulation in terms of capacity building of existing institutions, improved database management, professionalisation of environmental clearance system",""],[1,"247","12","National Environment Assessment and Monitoring Authority","A multi-disciplinary autonomous body namely National Environment Assessment and Monitoring Authority (NEAMA) is proposed to be set up",""],[1,"247","13","NEAMA Environmental Clearance Authority","NEAMA is also envisaged to grant clearances under the Environment (Protection) Act, 1986 including the coastal zone regulations and marine fisheries regulations.",""],[1,"247","14","Strengthening Environmental Rule Implementation Mechanisms","the Central and State Governments also need to invest in strengthening the mechanisms for implementing rules notified under the Environment (Protection) Act, 1986 including the CRZ Notification and the Marine Fishing Regulation Act.",""]],"54":[[1,"270","4","Science, Technology and Innovation Policy Reform","Evolve a new Science, Technology and Innovation policy to bring in more resources from both public and private sector for R&D for socially and strategically relevant projects and mainstream innovation-related activities with a focus on affordable and sustainable innovations;",""],[1,"270","10","Science and Technology Integration into National Development Processes","Ensure that S&T becomes an integral component of the national developmental processes by intercon- necting competencies and research resources and strengthening interconnections with the weakly connected stakeholders to the R&D outputs;",""],[1,"270","12","Increase National and Corporate R&D Investment","Increase R&D expenditure to 2 per cent of GDP and significantly enhance corporate sector R&D expenditure to at least 1 per cent of GDP by attracting investments and engaging the corporate sector in R&D through policy and reforms pro- cesses; earmark 10-15 per cent of public invest- ment exclusively for public-private partnership (PPP) R&D to private sector through the com- petitive grant process with a stipulation that com- parable provisions would be made by the private sector under PPP model;",""],[1,"270","15","Grand Challenge Programmes and PAN-India Missions","Initiate Grand Challenge Programmes and launch PAN-India missions to address national priorities",""],[1,"271","7","Technology Assessment Institutional Arrangement","Create an independent institutional arrangement for Technology Assessment capability.",""],[1,"271","8","Science and Technology Sector Structural Reform","Bring in structural reforms in the S&T sector by creating new financial appraisal and audit mecha- nisms and a new personnel policy based on best global practices coupled with seamless mobility of S&T personnel;",""],[1,"271","12","R&D Base Expansion and Research Institution Strengthening","it will have to aim at quadrupling its R&D base, stimulate research where R&D productivity is relatively lower, provide challenges to institutions for global positioning including in intellectual property (IP) generation, establish new academies and institutions, build up large publicly funded and privately managed facilities to help researchers and adopt aggressive mechanisms to attract the Indian diaspora for R&D positions.",""],[1,"273","3","Corporate Sector Funding for R&D Programmes","It is important that the corpo- rate sector (both public and the private) come for- ward to fund R&D programmes directed towards national developmental goals.",""],[1,"273","3","Increase National R&D Expenditure to 2 Percent of GDP","The target of total expenditure in R&D increasing to 2 per cent of GDP by the end of the Twelfth Plan could be achieved",""],[1,"277","2","Science and Technology Structural Reforms","In the Twelfth Plan, the suggested structural reforms for S&T sector need to be pursued so as to derive the best out of it.",""],[1,"277","2","Science and Technology Action Agenda for National Problems","SAC to PM has also prepared outlines of an agenda for action on the S&T inputs to pressing national problems which will be pursued for implementation.",""],[1,"277","3","Science, Technology and Innovation Measurement System Development","it is essential to develop suitable measurement systems for the science, technology and innovation output indicators for India.",""],[1,"277","3","Strategic Positioning in Niche Areas of Comparative Strength","It may accordingly be desirable to adopt strategic planning for positioning India in niche positions in areas of comparative strength rather than to invest through a broad spectrum approach.",""],[1,"277","5","National Policy Evolution and Basic Research Direction Mechanism","Setting up national-level mechanism for evol- ing policies and providing direction to basic research",""],[1,"277","13","Science Department and Agency Programme Consolidation and New Initiatives","The emphasis during the Twelfth Plan by the six science departments and agencies is to consolidate the gains of the Eleventh Plan period and propose new initiatives with the objective of enhancing global competi- tiveness of the Indian R&D system.",""],[1,"277","13","R&D System Competitiveness Enhancement Initiatives","The emphasis during the Twelfth Plan by the six science departments and agencies is to consolidate the gains of the Eleventh Plan period and propose new initiatives with the objective of enhancing global competi- tiveness of the Indian R&D system.",""],[1,"278","2","Science and Technology Policy Formulation and R&D Promotion Programmes","The DST, engaged in the formulation of S&T related policies and promotion of R&D through Extra Mural Research Schemes has mounted a large number of proactive schemes and measures during the Eleventh Plan period.","DST"],[1,"278","2","Twelfth Plan Science and Technology Programme Alignment","The department is committed to align its Twelfth Plan programmes and initiatives","DST"],[1,"278","3","Review and Consolidation of Science Sector Programmes","such initiatives should be preceded by a careful and critical review of all ongoing programmes and consolidation of successful schemes as well as the new Eleventh Plan initiatives.",""],[1,"278","3","Output-Directed Twelfth Plan Programme Formulation","the department has adopted an output-directed development path and related inputs to expected and targeted goals as well as likely impacts.",""],[1,"283","8","Focused Investment and Governance Reform Package","The strategy of the Twelfth Plan complement- ing the foundations laid during the Eleventh Plan shall be achieved through focused investments, pol- icy support, reforms in governance and management of projects around the following strategic goals:",""],[1,"287","2","Legal Framework and Legislation Acceleration","Expedite legal framework and legislations: BRAI Bill has been tabled in parliament for introduction.",""],[1,"287","2","Public Sector-Funded Intellectual Property Management Bill","It is proposed to bring other bills dealing with public sector-funded IP management; DNA profiling and Regional Centre for Biotechnology.",""],[1,"287","2","DNA Profiling Bill","It is proposed to bring other bills dealing with public sector-funded IP management; DNA profiling and Regional Centre for Biotechnology.",""],[1,"287","2","Regional Centre for Biotechnology Bill","It is proposed to bring other bills dealing with public sector-funded IP management; DNA profiling and Regional Centre for Biotechnology.",""],[1,"287","3","Strengthening and Scaling Up Successful Eleventh Plan Schemes","successful schemes shall be strengthened through stringent project management and scale up.",""],[1,"287","3","Phasing Out Outlived Projects and Programmes","certain projects and programmes that have outlived their rel- evance will be phased out.",""],[1,"287","4","Biotechnology Policy Research and Analysis Support","Besides general capacity building through workshops, training and research, centres/units for health and agriculture biotechnology policy research will be supported along regular policy dialogue among stakeholders through special meetings and seminars.",""],[1,"297","9","R&D Management Support Programme Strengthening","The entire programme is proposed to be strengthened considerably during the Twelfth Plan period and taken to new heights.",""]],"55":[[1,"272","1","Basic Research Promotion Strategy","Approaches for spotting, nurturing and encouraging sparks and talent in scientific research have to form one of the established strategies for promotion of basic research.",""],[1,"272","1","National Interest Basic Research Prioritization","there should also be a parallel effort to identify areas of national interest and gaps and promote basic research in such areas.",""],[1,"276","1","Development of Major Mega Research Facilities","During the Twelfth Plan period, India needs to invest into developing following major Mega facilities: (i) Laser Interferometer Gravitational Wave Observatory (LIGO) Experiments; (ii) India-based Neutrino Observatory (INO); (iii) Thirty Meter Telescope (TMT); (iv) Square Kilometre Array (SKA); (v) National Large Solar Telescope and (vi) Next Generation Synchrotron.",""],[1,"276","8","Mega Science Projects","Some of the mega science projects being considered in the Twelfth Plan are Laser Interferometer Gravitational Wave Observatory, National Large Solar Telescope and India-based Neutrino Observatory, besides the ongoing International Thermonuclear Experimental Reactor (ITER).",""],[1,"279","6","Basic Research Investment in Frontier Science and Technology","Basic research in frontier areas of S&T will remain a priority of the R&D sector.",""],[1,"280","1","Science and Engineering Research Board","the department has also established Science and Engineering Research Board as an autonomous agency and alternative mechanism for supporting basic research in India.",""],[1,"280","3","Strengthening of the SERB Scheme","The SERB scheme would be strengthened during the Twelfth Plan.",""],[1,"280","7","SERB New Vehicle for Funding Basic Research","National SERB-New Vehicle of Funding Basic Research: SERB is emerging as a new body and mechanism for promoting basic research in the country.",""],[1,"286","5","Investigator-Driven R&D Funding Programme","The department has been funding investigator-driven R&D projects across areas of basic agriculture, health care, environment, animal health and reproduction, bio resource utilisation and food S&T and so on.",""],[1,"296","4","High-Risk, High-Impact Research Initiative","Well-focused initiative to pursue innovative ideas and embark upon high-risk, high-impact projects, thus, would be pursued to travel traversed paths and open up newer vistas.",""],[1,"297","3","Grand Challenge Initiative","The Grand Challenge Initiative-would focus on solving unsolved problems or providing a com- prehensive solution to an enduring national prob- lem.",""],[1,"297","10","New Millennium Indian Technology Leadership Initiative Strengthening and Expansion","The programme is envisaged to be strengthened and broadened further during the Twelfth Plan by the following approach:",""],[1,"304","6","Basic Research Infrastructure Expansion","Large experimental facilities that were set up by DAE during the Eleventh Plan period to facilitate basic research include commissioning of High Altitude GAmma-Ray (HAGAR) array, which consists of seven telescopes, at the high-altitude (4,300 m) station Hanle (in Ladakh) for ground-based gamma ray astronomy, and a high-resolution spectrometer Indian Gamma Ray Array consisting of Germanium clover detectors at the Pelletron Linac Facility at the Tata Institute of Fundamental Research (TIFR).","DAE"],[1,"304","6","Supercomputer Development and Release","The first phase of parallel supercomputer (Anupam Adhya), delivering 47 terraflop of sustained lin- pack computational performance, has been developed and released to the users.",""],[1,"304","6","New Research Campus and Centres Establishment","New campus of TIFR at Hyderabad and two major new centres for basic research-International Centre for Theoretical Sciences at Bengaluru and the TIFR Centre for Interdisciplinary Sciences at Hyderabad are being established.",""],[1,"305","5","Research Reactor Development at BARC Campus Vizag","To meet the increasing requirement of various radioisotopes for use in the field of medicine, industry and agriculture, needs of special materials, and various facilities for basic and applied research, a 125 MW(th) Research Reactor and a 30 MW High Flux Research Reactor (HFRR) are proposed in the Twelfth plan at BARC Campus Vizag.",""],[1,"305","6","National Hadron Therapy Facility for Cancer Treatment","A national hadron therapy facility for cancer treatment at Advanced Centre for Treatment, Research and Education in Cancer at Tata Memorial Centre (ACTREC-TMC) will be set up.",""],[1,"305","6","INDUS 2 Beam Line Installation and Upgrade Programme","Several new beam lines will be installed at INDUS 2 and the existing ones will be upgraded with modern equipment for supporting high-quality research.",""],[1,"306","2","Participation in Mega Science Projects","DAE is participating in several Mega Science Projects.",""],[1,"306","2","Indian Synchrotron for Materials and Energy Research","setting up of an Indian Synchrotron for Materials and Energy Research is also proposed.",""],[1,"306","11","Strengthening and Expanding R&D Infrastructure","In order to meet the growing number of programmes and projects, including in greenfield locations, it is nec- essary to strengthen and expand the investments in infrastructure.",""],[1,"306","11","Development of New TIFR Hyderabad Campus","Development of the new TIFR Hyderabad campus will be given priority.",""],[1,"306","11","Establishment of Research Laboratories at TCIS Hyderabad Transit Campus","Laboratories for research in chemistry, biology, lasers and optics, magnetic resonance and con- densed matter will be set up in this transit campus during the twelfth plan.",""],[1,"309","16","High-Performance Computing Facility Upgrade","Strengthen HPC facility from the existing 124 T flops to 2.5 P flops.",""],[1,"310","2","International Training Centre for Operational Oceanography","Setting up of an International Training Centre for Operational Oceanography.",""],[1,"310","5","Ocean Research Vessel Acquisition","Acquisition of three state-of-the art ocean research vessels.",""],[1,"310","6","Antarctic Third Station Commissioning","Commissioning of third station at Antarctic to strengthen research activities in the Polar Regions.",""],[1,"310","7","Deep Bore Hole Drilling for Seismicity Research","Drilling a deep bore hole in Koyna-Warna region for better understanding of stable continental region earthquakes and Reservoir Triggered Seismicity.",""],[1,"310","15","Establishment of Five New CSIR Institutes","Establishment of the following five new institutes: CSIR Institute of Synthetic and Systems Biology; CSIR Fourth Paradigm Institute; CSIR Institute of Bio-mimetic Materials; CSIR Network Institute for Solar Energy and CSIR Network Institute for Manufacturing Technology.",""],[1,"310","16","CSIR Outreach Centres Establishment","Setting up of 10 CSIR Outreach Centres.",""],[1,"310","20","CSIR Offshore Joint Centres of Excellence","Setting up CSIR offshore Joint Centres of Excellence in Malaysia, Sweden and USA.",""],[1,"310","20","Innovation Complex Development Programme","Setting up of 12 world- class Innovation Complexes in identified locations across India.",""],[1,"310","25","Launch Vehicle Flight Programme","Realisation of total 25 launch vehicle flights-17 PSLVs + 6 GSLVs + 2 GSLV Mk III including one Experimental Mission (as against 14 flights of the Eleventh plan).",""],[1,"310","25","GSLV Mk III Developmental Flight Programme","First Developmental Flights of GSLV Mk III-the next generation launch vehicle.",""],[1,"311","9","Mars Orbiter Mission","Undertaking challenging Mars Orbiter Mission.",""],[1,"311","10","Chandrayaan-2 Mission with Rover and Lander","Realisation of Chandrayaan-2 with Rover and Lander.",""],[1,"311","19","MACE Commissioning at Hanle","Commissioning of the MACE at Hanle.",""],[1,"311","21","Indus Synchrotron User Facility Enhancement","Enhancement of INDUS synchrotron user facility.",""]],"56":[[1,"270","9","Leadership Stimulation in Science, Technology, and Human Resource Development","Leadership Focus-stimulate the Department/ Agency's leadership in identified domains of science, technology and human resource development.",""],[1,"270","13","Scientific Career Mobility and Gender Parity Programme","Provide more flexibility to the younger genera- tion of scientists to pursue their ideas and greater mobility between industry, academia and R&D institutions; strengthen gender parity in R&D by way of mobility and women re-entry pro- grammes; consolidate on the gains achieved dur- ing the Eleventh Plan in nurturing students to pursue science as a career;",""],[1,"271","4","Inter-University and Inter-Institutional Centres Development","Create new Inter-University Centres (IUCs) and Inter-Institutional Centres (IICs) in chosen areas of Science and Engineering, which will provide access to state-of-the-art facilities and academic ambience for researchers in universities and aca- demic institutions;",""],[1,"271","13","Expansion of R&D Personnel in Established Research Centres","Expanding the strength of R&D personnel in the established centres of R&D by about 10,000 within the Twelfth Plan period should be considered feasi- ble.",""],[1,"271","13","Mobilization of Research Capacity in Colleges and Universities","It is also imperative that the large latent potential in colleges, universities and some academic centres is tapped.",""],[1,"271","13","Quality Assurance Measures for Research Output","Adequate measures for ensuring quality of research output should also be looked at.",""],[1,"272","2","Science Education System Transformation","For this, the science education system, as it stands today, needs radical transformation.",""],[1,"272","3","Science Teacher Incentive and Professional Development Scheme","Science teaching as a profession needs to be incentivised, accorded the respect it deserves and once again placed on a high pedestal.","Ministry of Human Resource Development | Ministry of S&T"],[1,"272","3","Global Best Practices Exposure for Science Teachers","Equally important would be exposing these teachers at all levels in the country to the best global practices and pedagogy innovations to enable them to practise and spread superior methods of teaching and research.",""],[1,"272","5","International University Cooperation Expansion","As one of the strategies, international cooperation for deployment needs to be scaled up manifold, for enriching quality of research in the university sector.",""],[1,"272","5","New Inter-University Centres in Emerging Research Areas","Several new IUCs in carefully chosen areas should be set up during the Twelfth Plan in newer areas such as Biodiversity and Genetic Epidemiology; Mathematical Modelling; Computer Science and Cyber Security; Cognitive Sciences; Advanced Materials, Manufacturing and Fabrication; Technology Management; and Interdisciplinary Approaches in Humanities, Social Science and Natural Sciences.",""],[1,"277","1","Science Workforce Expansion","These include: (i) increasing density of scientists by about 60 per cent",""],[1,"277","1","Grant-Based Research Funding Expansion","These include: (vi) investing larger resources into performing individuals through grant model of funding",""],[1,"277","2","R&D Personnel Policy Development","the development of a new, expansive personnel policy for R&D sector, based on global best practices.",""],[1,"277","6","Scientific Manpower and S&T Infrastructure Strengthening","Enlarging the pool of scientific manpower and strengthening the S&T infrastructure and attracting and retaining young people to careers in science",""],[1,"277","8","Establishment of Globally Competitive Research Facilities and Centres of Excellence","Establishing globally competitive research facili- ties and centres of excellence",""],[1,"277","9","Public-Private Partnership Models for Higher Education Research","Developing new models of PPPs in higher educa- tion, particularly for research in universities and high technology areas",""],[1,"278","8","University Research, Innovation and Excellence Support Programmes","Promotion of University Research and Scientific Excellence (PURSE) and Consolidation of University Research, Innovation and Excellence (CURIE) have been launched to improve and support the R&D in the universities.",""],[1,"278","9","INSPIRE Science Talent Development Scheme","A major scheme known as INSPIRE for attracting talent in science and for nurturing students right from the school level has been initiated and around 14000 students have been awarded SHE Scholarships; more than 6 lakh awards for students in classes ranging from Class VI to X and 1200 INSPIRE Fellowships for pursuing doctoral degrees have been granted, and INSPIRE faculty awards have been made to 74 postdoctoral scholars.",""],[1,"280","4","University Research Rejuvenation through PURSE","During the last three years, DST has been investing into university sector through PURSE based on volume of scientific publications and h-indices of these universities.","DST"],[1,"281","2","R&D Personnel Expansion Schemes","DST has proposed schemes for increasing the volume and density of R&D professionals by enlisting researchers from the university sector.","DST"],[1,"281","3","Overseas Doctoral Scholarships","During the Twelfth Plan period, DST has proposed a number of new initiatives.","DST"],[1,"281","3","Overseas Postdoctoral Fellowships","During the Twelfth Plan period, DST has proposed a number of new initiatives.","DST"],[1,"281","3","Women Mobility Scheme for Employed Scientists","During the Twelfth Plan period, DST has proposed a number of new initiatives.","DST"],[1,"281","3","Expansion of Principal Investigator Base","During the Twelfth Plan period, DST has proposed a number of new initiatives.","DST"],[1,"281","3","Start-up Research Grants for Indian Diaspora Faculty","During the Twelfth Plan period, DST has proposed a number of new initiatives.","DST"],[1,"281","3","Disha Women in Science Programme","During the Twelfth Plan period, DST has proposed a number of new initiatives.","DST"],[1,"281","3","Science Teaching Educator Development Programme","During the Twelfth Plan period, DST has proposed a number of new initiatives.","DST"],[1,"281","3","National Centres for Advanced Research","During the Twelfth Plan period, DST has proposed a number of new initiatives.","DST"],[1,"282","1","Science Teaching Quality Partnership Programme","Alliance and partnership programmes with Ministry of Human Resource Development for enhancement of quality to science teaching and with DAE for the cre- ation of large R&D office structure for Mega Science would be further developed during the Twelfth Plan Period.","Ministry of Human Resource Development | DAE"],[1,"283","9","Research Scholar and Scientist Expansion Programme","Expand available pool of research scholars and scientists at all levels (PhD, PDFs, young faculty) in biological and interdisciplinary space by three to five folds: A major programme-based support will be provided for expanding biological/",""],[1,"283","10","Life Science Departments and Interdisciplinary Bio-based Science Clusters","life science departments and clusters in univer- sities, IITs, medical, veterinary and agriculture and pharmaceutical universities/departments, centres of excellence.",""],[1,"285","1","Centres of Excellence and Research Capacity Expansion Programme","support to centres of excellence, incubators, programmes for expanding existing research and human resource capacity by threefolds through increase in current areas of relative strength such as molecular and cell biology, structural biology, immunology, neurobiology, bioengineering and promoting career paths in clinical and translational research, regulatory sciences, Intellectual Property (IP) technology transfer and knowledge management, entrepreneurship and education, and so on, are proposed.",""],[1,"285","1","Expansion and Redesign of Extramural and Inter-Institutional Centres","It is also proposed to expand, redesign and create extramural and inter-institutional centres as a cost-efficient process of scale up, utilising the existing best people with some additional younger people.",""],[1,"285","6","DBT Grant-in-Aid Partnership Research and Translational Centres","Establish DBT Grant-in-Aid or partnership research and translational centres through long- term support in 10 best universities/institu- tions in at least 10 areas of interest, for example: Agriculture sciences and innovation for pre- breeding, genetic modification (GM) technology and molecular breeding; veterinary S&T for ani- mal productivity and health; biopharmaceutical sciences and health technology; chemical biology and synthetic biology.",""],[1,"285","8","National Life Sciences Resource Centre Establishment","A National Life Sciences Resource Centre (NLSRC) with special- ised research staff, informatics support and data- bases to network all research resources, training for skill development activities and organise a systematic information access management facilitating biology research community pro- posed to set up.",""],[1,"287","5","Establishment of Autonomous National Research Centres in Emerging Translational Research Areas","It is proposed to establish few institutes/research centres in emerging areas of translational research such as Bioinformatics and Computational Biology; Marine and Microbial Biotechnology; Biodesign, Bioscience and Bioengineering; Chronic Disease Science and Biotechnology and Infectious Science and Biotechnology Institute in North East (linking to Translational Health Science and Technology Institute [THSTI] as partner for Training and Education).",""],[1,"291","4","Research Education, Training and Outreach Facilities","Facilities will be created to provide necessary infra- structure.",""],[1,"291","4","Centre for Operational Meteorology","The other main activities would be set- ting up a Centre for Operational Meteorology and an International Training Centre for Operational",""],[1,"291","4","International Training Centre for Operational Meteorology","The other main activities would be set- ting up a Centre for Operational Meteorology and an International Training Centre for Operational",""],[1,"291","5","Oceanography Training and Capacity Building Initiative","Oceanography as part of UNESCO's endeavour for training and capacity building and Indo-African Centre for Medium Range Weather Forecast for extending weather forecasting services in the African region.",""],[1,"291","5","MoES Chair Professorships and Academic Programme Expansion","It is proposed to support Human Resource Development through establishment of MoES Chair Professorship in IITs and IISERs and initiation of academic programmes at IITs and IISERs.",""],[1,"292","2","Trans-Disciplinary Human Resource Development","The Twelfth Five Year Plan of CSIR focuses on achieving science and engineering leadership; developing innovative technological solutions; practising open innovation initiatives; developing and nur- turing human resource in trans-disciplinary areas; facilitating science-based entrepreneurship; and enabling socio-economic transformation through appropriate S&T intervention.",""],[1,"297","7","National S&T Human Resource Development Fellowships","CSIR envisages continuing its endeavour of strengthening S&T human resources in the country through fellowships at various levels.",""],[1,"297","7","Dyslexic Children Support Fellowship Programme","In addition, during the Twelfth Plan, it is envisaged to introduce novel fellowship programmes such as hand-holding support to dyslexic children;",""],[1,"297","7","Analytical Ability-Based Fellowship Programme","In addition, during the Twelfth Plan, it is envisaged to introduce novel fellowship programmes such as ... provision of analytical ability-based fellowships;",""],[1,"297","7","PC Ray Innovation Postdoctoral Fellowship","In addition, during the Twelfth Plan, it is envisaged to introduce novel fellowship programmes such as ... and also introduce the PC Ray Innovation Postdoctoral Fellowship.",""],[1,"303","2","Nuclear Technology Outreach and University Linkage Programme","of nuclear technology for societal benefits, out- reach programmes to enhance public awareness and acceptance, and strengthening of linkages with uni- versities and national laboratories.",""],[1,"304","5","Research-Education Linkage Initiatives in Atomic Energy","The Board of Research in Nuclear Sciences (BRNS) and University Grants Commission-Department of Atomic Energy (UGC-DAE) Consortium for Scientific Research stand out as important initiatives of DAE in the direction of linking research with education.",""],[1,"304","5","Homi Bhabha National Institute Academic Programme Platform","The Homi Bhabha National Institute (HBNI) is fully functional and plays an important role in conducting academic programmes under its own umbrella as well as in linking DAE with other academic institutes in the country and abroad.",""],[1,"306","3","Strengthening DAE-Academia Research Collaboration and Human Resource Development","The present methods of collaboration through BRNS and MOUs with select academic institutes will con- tinue to be supported and further strengthened.",""],[1,"306","3","Establishment of HBNI as a Deemed University","The initiative of the Department to set up HBNI as a deemed-to- be university is another step towards strengthening the linkage between the institutions of the DAE and also with the academic and research community in the country.",""],[1,"306","3","Expansion of Research Student Intake at TIFR","Similarly, TIFR has increased intake of research students after having been declared a deemed-to-be university.",""],[1,"306","4","Professional Incentive Scheme for DAE","incentive to competent professionals within and outside DAE, will be continued.",""],[1,"306","4","University Linkage for DAE Manpower Development","Increasing linkages with the national higher education institutions (uni- versities, IITs and NITs and so on.) will be continued so as to ensure availability of quality manpower for DAE programmes and projects.",""],[1,"306","5","Global Centre for Nuclear Energy Partnership Establishment","The Global Centre for Nuclear Energy Partnership (GCNEP), the sixth R&D centre of DAE, is being set up in Haryana near Delhi.","DAE"],[1,"308","14","Scholarship and Research Fellowship Expansion","To strengthen Human Capacities, 30,000 new scholars for Scholarship for Higher Education targeted, Award of Overseas Doctoral scholarships-3,000 man years, Overseas postdoctoral fellowships-500 man years, Women mobility scheme for employed scientists-1,000 positions, Start-up research grant for Indian diaspora undertaking faculty assignments in Indian academia-1,000 man years, enlarging the Principal Investigator base-1,500 man years, INSPIRE Award scheme-2 million awards.",""],[1,"308","15","Science and Technology Infrastructure Support Programme","Support under Fund for improvement of S&T Infrastructure (FIST)-1200-1500 departments and 500 col- leges, PURSE-50 Universities, CURIE-6 Universities and 50 Women colleges, IRHPA-15 research areas, SAIF-25 new centres and 10 select centres.",""],[1,"308","15","Autonomous Institutions Capacity Strengthening Initiative","Autonomous Institutions focused on Institutional Capacities, Water Technology Solutions-20 implementable solutions to be demonstrated in at least 15 clusters, 20 centres to be created for Nano S&T Mission, 5 National Centres in Advanced Research.",""],[1,"308","19","Solar Energy Research Initiative","Solar Energy Research Initiative-Support 250 doctoral-level researchers from 10 institutions.",""],[1,"309","5","Star Undergraduate Colleges Establishment Programme","it is proposed to establish 100 Star Undergraduate Colleges",""],[1,"309","5","Ramalingaswami Fellowship Scheme for Returning Scientists","100 Ramalingaswami fellowships for returning scientists from abroad",""],[1,"309","5","Industry-Ready Graduate Finishing Schools","10 finishing schools for industry-ready graduates",""],[1,"309","5","Biomedical Fellowship Support Scheme","award 200 Welcome trust-DBT biomedical fellowships, junior research fellows and 250 postdoc- toral fellowships in life sciences",""],[1,"309","6","Centres of Excellence and Innovation Network","Under Promotion of Excellence and Innovation, the targets are to create 25 Centres of excellence in plant sciences, animal sciences, human biology systems and industrial research;10 new centres for translational science education and innovative research in Medical Schools, 20 IICs connecting basic sciences with trans- lational R&D and 2 centres for policy research in agriculture and health care biotechnology.",""],[1,"309","7","Biotech Research Facilities and Life Science Education Upgrade","For Biotech Facilities and Research Resources the targets are to establish five research resources and service facilities, upgrade and redesign life science research and education in 38 universities.",""],[1,"309","10","National Research Centres and Institutes Establishment","Establish five national research centres/institutions in the areas of Bioinformatics and Computational Biology; Marine and Microbial Biotechnology; Bio-design; Bioscience and Bioengineering; Chronic Disease Science and Biotechnology; and Infectious Science and Biotechnology Institute in the North-East.",""],[1,"309","12","Expansion of All India Institutes and Research Infrastructure","Expanding existing AIs threefold in terms of human resource, setting up of Extra Mural Research centres on or off site to promote translational science; starting of number of disease-specific network programmes; and physical infrastructure.",""],[1,"309","14","Translational and Strategic Research Project Network Launch","For translational and strategic research in agriculture, health care and environment, about 50 projects/ networks shall be launched in system biology, synthetic biology, computational sciences, nano-biology, pre-breeding of crops, photonics, molecular imaging and tissue engineering, biopharmaceuticals and drug development and other emerging areas.",""],[1,"310","14","Trans-Disciplinary PhD Training Programme","Training of 3,000 PhDs in trans-disciplinary areas of science and engineering through AcSIR.",""],[1,"310","18","Research Fellowship and Scholarship Award Scheme","To award 15,000 fellowships under the JRF-NET, 1,000 Syamaprasad Mookherjee Fellowships to be awarded, 100 awards under Trans-Disciplinary Fellowship Scheme yearly, 100 awards under CSIR Nehru Science Post-Doctoral Fellowship Scheme yearly, 250 scholarships for dyslexic students.",""],[1,"310","22","Open Source Drug Discovery Expansion","Expand OSDD to OS drug discovery, OS drug development, OS drug delivery and OS disease diagnostics for MTb.",""],[1,"310","23","OSDD Programme Extension to Malaria","Extending OSDD programme to malaria.",""],[1,"311","18","Medical Cyclotron Establishment","Establishment of 30 MeV Medical Cyclotron.",""]],"57":[[1,"308","20","State Spatial Data Infrastructure Development","Under Natural Resources Data Management System (NRDMS) and National Spatial Data Infrastructure (NSDI), State Spatial Data Infrastructure has been targeted.",""],[1,"311","1","Indian Regional Navigational Satellite System Establishment","Establishment of Indian Regional Navigational Satellite System (IRNSS) with a constellation of seven satellites.",""],[1,"311","2","Operationalization of the GAGAN Base","Implementation of fully operational base of GAGAN.",""],[1,"311","3","INSAT/GSAT Capacity Expansion","Augment the INSAT/GSAT capacity to ~500 Transponders in C, Ku, Ka, MSS and BSS bands.",""],[1,"311","4","GSAT-11 Advanced Communication Satellite Realisation","Realisation of GSAT-11-Advanced Communication Satellite.",""],[1,"311","5","Advanced Remote Sensing Technology Deployment","Realisation of Advanced Remote Sensing Technology for 0.25m resolution.",""],[1,"311","6","Geo Imaging Satellite for Disaster Management Support","Realisation of Geo Imaging Satellite (GISAT) for Disaster Management Support.",""],[1,"311","7","Space-Based Information Support for Decentralised Planning","Implementation of Space based Information Support for Decentralised Planning.",""],[1,"311","10","National Database and Decision Support System Operationalisation","Operationalisation of NDEM with multi-thematic, multi-scale database and relevant Decision Support systems.",""],[1,"315","5","Rural Broadband Applications Development Initiative","currently working on applications for rural broad- band in collaboration with Ministries of Rural Development, Panchayati Raj, HRD, Health and the Prime Minister's National Council on Skill Development so that even as hardware connectivity is under progress, applications also get addressed.","Ministries of Rural Development | Panchayati Raj | HRD | Health | the Prime Minister's National Council on Skill Development"],[1,"316","8","Courts of Tomorrow Digitalisation Initiative","NInC is also working on the 'Courts of Tomorrow' initiative to give effect to the extensive computerisation plan as laid down by the e-courts Mission Mode Project.",""],[1,"317","7","Aadhaar Unique Identity Programme","This programme will create a foundation for more transparent and efficient public service delivery and is internationally considered as a game-changing approach to inclusion.",""],[1,"318","1","Aadhaar-Enabled Direct Benefit Transfer Infrastructure","Going forward, the Aadhaar-enabled bank account and payment infrastructure will enable e-payments to the beneficiaries' bank accounts for government's social welfare schemes such as Mahatma Gandhi National Rural Employment Guarantee Act (MGNREGA) and mitigate delays and losses.",""],[1,"318","1","Aadhaar-Based Subsidy Transfer for PDS and Essential Commodities","The gov- ernment is also looking at using the Aadhaar plat- form for PDS and achieving substantial economies in subsidy outgo in areas such as Fertiliser, Liquefied Petroleum Gas (LPG) and Kerosene by enabling direct transfer of subsidies.",""],[1,"318","2","ICT-Enabled Governance and Legal System Modernization","Government is also leveraging ICT to reduce pendency in the legal system, encourage a move towards e-governance, e-procurement, e-tendering and e-office.",""],[1,"318","2","Panchayat Fiber Broadband Connectivity Initiative","It is also undertaking an ambitious initia- tive to connect 2,50,000 panchayats with fiber-based broadband to improve governance and service deliv- ery at the last mile.",""],[1,"318","2","National Geographic Information System Organization","A national geographic informa- tion system (NGIS) organisation is also being thought of to map information, assets and data accurately, which will assist in policy and works planning and improve delivery of services in urban and rural areas.",""],[1,"318","3","National Knowledge Network Expansion","The National Knowledge Network (NKN) of the Government of India which is a high-speed multi-gigagibit network is not only connecting educational and research institutes in the country, but is getting connected to global research networks to enable real-time collaboration and research.","Government of India"],[1,"326","6","Nationwide UID Platform Roll-out for Direct Fund Transfers","The Twelfth Plan will see the roll-out of the unique identification (UID) platform across the country that will enable efficient and expeditious transfer for funds to ultimate beneficiaries without leakage.",""],[1,"327","2","E-Governance and Public Service Delivery Expansion","An important aid to delivery of services can be the use of e-governance and technology.","Ministry of Corporate Affairs | Department of Customs"],[1,"327","3","Unique ID Expansion with Biometrics","The expansion of UID in the Twelfth Plan, along with the National population register should result in a UID Number by the end of Twelfth Plan to every resident.",""],[1,"327","3","Technology-Enabled Financial Inclusion Services","The financial inclusion services are also feasible by using UID and the telecom services.",""],[1,"327","3","Technology-Enabled Banking Correspondent Expansion","It is also possible to use technology for expanding banking correspondence and thereby expand the extent of banking services.",""],[1,"327","3","Digital Public Service Delivery Expansion","The use of technology in delivery of public services will need to be expanded rapidly to reduce delays and discretion used to the advantage of the citizen.",""],[1,"328","2","E-Governance Expansion and Connectivity Scheme","During the Plan, it is proposed to further expand e-governance.",""]],"58":[[1,"365","5","Infrastructure Deficit Identification and Major Infrastructure Funding Arrangement","action for identification of infrastructure deficit in the key areas and funding arrangement for the major infrastructure resulted in prioritisation of projects of connectivity (Road, Rail, Air), develop- ment of power, investment in Human Resource Development, expansion of Skill Development, and so on.",""],[1,"365","7","Special Accelerated Road Development Programme for the North-Eastern Region","Special Accelerated Road Development Programme for the North-Eastern Region (SARDP-NE) connecting State Capitals, District Headquarters and strategic border roads by 2/4 lane roads (approved in 2005-06)-to be implemented in Phase-A and Phase-B-10,141 km (NH 4,798 km and State Road 343 km),",""],[1,"365","10","Broad Gauge Line Conversion and Rail-Cum-Road Bridge Connectivity","Broad Gauge (BG) line conversion-connect- ing Guwahati-Dibrughar-Tinsukia, Rangia- Murkongselek Bridge (rail-cum-road) across Brahmaputra at Bogibeel",""],[1,"365","14","New Railway Line Construction","5. New lines: Agartala-Akhura, Tetelia-Byrnihat- Shillong, Harmuti-Itanagar, Silchar-Jiribam- Imphal (Tupul).",""],[1,"365","16","New Airport Development","New airports at Itanagar, Ceithu (Kohima), Pakyong;",""],[1,"365","17","Small Airport and Advanced Landing Ground Upgradation","Up-gradation of smaller airports and Advanced Landing Grounds ( ALGs) in Arunachal Pradesh.",""],[1,"367","6","Road Connectivity with Myanmar Operationalisation","early operationalisation of road connectivity with Myanmar",""],[1,"367","6","Indo-Bangladesh Joint Communique Connectivity Measures","items of interven- tion as per Indo-Bangladesh Joint Communique (flight between Agartala and Dhaka, access to sea through Chittagong Port, Agartala-Dhaka-Kolkata direct bus service, access to Ashuganj Inland Port, construction of bridge over river Feni, connect- ing Sabroom-Chittagong),",""],[1,"367","6","Kaladan Multi-Modal Transit Transport Project","Kaladan Multi-modal Transit Transport Project linking Mizoram and Myanmar up to Sittwe port.",""],[1,"368","9","Trans-Arunachal Highway and District Connectivity Improvement","Trans-Arunachal Highway along with identified district connectivity.",""],[1,"368","11","Construction of Important Bridges on Major Road Projects","Important bridges include Dhola-Sadia over Brahmaputra and all other crucial bridges on the major road projects.",""],[1,"368","12","Four-Lane Highway Survey and DPR Preparation","Four-lane highway from Tizit in the north to Dimapur via Tuli-Jalukie-Khelma (proposed by the State Government for survey in investigation and DPR preparation, and so on).",""],[1,"368","13","Broad Gauge Line Conversion and Bogibeel Rail-Cum-Road Bridge Connectivity","Broad Gauge (line conversion)-connect- ing Guwahati-Dibrughar-Tinsukia, Rangia- Murkongselek Bridge (rail-cum-road) across Brahmaputra at Bogibeel.",""],[1,"368","19","New Airport Development and Airfield Upgrading","New airports at Itanagar, Ceithu (Kohima), Pakyong (Gangtok). In addition, there are smaller airports, ALGs to be upgraded in Arunachal Pradesh.",""],[1,"369","4","Explore Access Through Bangladesh","access through Bangladesh needs to be explored.",""]],"60":[[1,"166","6","Urban Public and Non-Motorized Transport Promotion","The way forward therefore is to promote public and non-motorized transport in cities",""],[1,"166","6","Intercity Rail Passenger Transport Promotion","and rail for intercity passenger travel",""],[1,"166","6","Private Vehicle and Air Transport Discouragement Policy","while discouraging the use of private vehicles in cities, as well as intercity transport by air",""],[1,"166","9","Air Pollution Reduction Initiative","3. Reduce air pollution in the country's cities, towns and villages.",""],[1,"167","1","Urban Public and Non-Motorized Transport Promotion Policy Instruments","We should focus on policy instruments to encour- age greater use of public and non-motorized trans- port in India's cities and towns, while discouraging the use of private motor vehicles.",""],[1,"167","2","Bus Utility Tax Relief and Fuel Tax Refund Reform","Government needs to revisit its taxation policy of vehicles and ensure that tax burden on bus utilities is considerably lowered.",""],[1,"167","3","Urban Local Body Capacity Strengthening for Integrated City Planning","This needs to be addressed urgently and capacities of ULBs need to be strengthened to enable mixed land use planning and preparation of an integrated transport plan for each city in the country.",""]],"61":[[1,"45","5","India Excellence Initiative","All these initiatives should be pooled into an India Excellence Initiative in the Twelfth Plan.",""],[1,"69","10","Higher Education Seat Expansion","Enhance access to higher education by creating two million additional seats for each age cohort aligned to the skill needs of the economy.",""]],"62":[[1,"5","6","Macroeconomic Stabilization and Structural Reform Strategy","The Twelfth Plan has therefore proposed a two pronged strategy focusing initially on the need to bring the macro economic imbalances under control and to reverse the slow down, while also pushing for structural reforms in many areas that are critical for maintaining medium term growth.",""],[1,"5","7","Fiscal Deficit Reduction and Medium-Term Fiscal Correction Roadmap","The Government has commenced the process of macro-economic rebalancing.",""],[1,"6","2","Fiscal Deficit Reduction","the targeted reduction in the fiscal deficit is an important instrument for bringing the current account deficit under control.",""],[1,"14","3","Macroeconomic Stabilization Strategy","The Twelfth Plan has commenced at a time when there are weaknesses in these areas but it outlines a macro economic strategy for correction.",""],[1,"17","1","Macroeconomic Stabilization and Plan Implementation","With determined effort to correct macro-economic imbalances, and to achieve effective implementation of both the programmes and sectoral policies outlined in the Plan, there is a good chance of bringing the economy back to an 8 percent growth path, which is war- ranted by the underlying fundamentals.",""],[1,"82","5","National Savings Mobilization Strategy","The savings strategy in the Twelfth Plan must be to reverse the decline in savings that occurred after 2007-08 in order to finance the increase in the rate of investment projected for the Twelfth Plan period.",""],[1,"89","7","Credible Fiscal Consolidation Path","the Twelfth Plan must aim at a credible fiscal consolidation path that would bring the central government's fiscal deficit back to tolerable levels.",""],[1,"89","8","Fiscal Deficit Reduction Path","The Finance Ministry's original fiscal consolidation path envisaged reducing the fiscal deficit from the targeted 5.1 per cent of GDP in 2011-12 to 3 per cent of GDP by 2014-15",""],[1,"89","8","Tax Revenue Increase and Subsidy Reduction","this will require a substantial increase in tax revenues, and also a reduction in subsidies as a percentage of GDP",""]],"63":[[1,"14","4","Tax Rationalization and GST Introduction","Rationalisation of tax policies, including especially, the introduction of GST, is another very important area, as is the rationalisation of subsidy policy to progressively reduce dysfunctional subsidies, and make essential subsidies better targeted through the use of Aadhar.",""],[1,"14","4","Subsidy Rationalization and Aadhaar Targeting Reform","Rationalisation of tax policies, including especially, the introduction of GST, is another very important area, as is the rationalisation of subsidy policy to progressively reduce dysfunctional subsidies, and make essential subsidies better targeted through the use of Aadhar.",""],[1,"15","3","Goods and Services Tax Implementation","Some, such as the implementation of the GST, which is an extremely important initiative, depend upon joint action, since it involves a constitutional amendment.",""],[1,"63","3","Goods and Services Tax Reform","The Centre must persevere with reforms of the tax structure, notably the introduction of Good and Services Tax (GST), which will represent a major modernisation of the indirect tax sys- tem.",""],[1,"63","5","Targeted Subsidy Reform","Some subsidies such as under the proposed Food Security Act will be predetermined.",""],[1,"63","5","Fertiliser Subsidy Redesign","Others such as on fertiliser can be redesigned to serve their purpose at less cost.",""],[1,"63","5","Petroleum Subsidy Reduction","They will have to be reduced.",""],[1,"63","6","Power Sector Subsidy Reduction Measures","The State Governments also need to take steps to reduce the growing burden of subsidies, most especially the large and growing losses in the power sector.",""],[1,"67","7","Phased Petroleum Product Price Adjustment","The Twelfth Plan must ensure a move to more rational petroleum product pricing, It may not be possible to remove all distortions imme- diately, but a phased price adjustment is needed that would reduce subsidy to manageable levels.",""],[1,"89","9","Tax Administration Improvement","This should not be onerous and can be achieved primarily through efforts to improve tax administration.",""],[1,"89","9","Goods and Services Tax Implementation","The implementation of Good and Services Tax (GST) is the most promising prospect in this regard.",""],[1,"108","6","Unified Goods and Services Tax Reform","Efforts are being made to introduce unified Goods and Service Tax (GST) in consultation with States.",""],[1,"109","3","Goods and Services Tax Reform","The introduction of GST will usher in major reforms of indirect taxes in the Centre and States.",""],[1,"114","3","Tax Revenue Mobilization and Tax-to-GDP Ratio Improvement","Efforts will continue in the Twelfth Plan period towards achieving the targeted tax-to-GDP ratios.",""],[1,"114","4","Subsidy Regime Reform","The subsidy regime needs to be urgently reformed to keep the total subsidy within the ceiling of 1.5 per cent of GDP in 2016-17 that the resources projections have built in.",""]],"64":[[1,"12","4","Poverty Reduction and Inclusive Growth Strategy","The Plan must retain its traditional focus on reducing poverty - an area where the results in the Eleventh Plan have been heartening.",""],[1,"12","4","Inclusion and Upliftment of Marginalised Groups","It must ensure upliftment of specific groups such as the SCs/STs/OBCs, minorities and other marginalised groups that suffer from historical exclusion.",""],[1,"12","4","Gender Gap Reduction Strategy","It must systematically close the gender gap which is a blot on our social structure.",""],[1,"12","4","Balanced Regional Development Strategy","It must also ensure balanced development of all the regions.",""],[1,"41","3","Equal Opportunity for Children Initiative","the Twelfth Plan should aim at making substantial progress in this dimension.",""]],"65":[[1,"40","4","Gender Inequality and Violence Prevention Initiative","Ending of gender based inequities, discrimi- nation and all forms of violence against girls and women is being accorded overriding priority",""]],"66":[[1,"16","2","Rationalization and Flexibilization of Centrally Sponsored Schemes","A major new initiative taken in the Twelfth Plan, to improve implementation of the Centrally Sponsored Schemes is to rationalise the number of the CSS, reducing them from 142 to 66, and also permitting greater flexibility in the guidelines.",""],[1,"112","10","Expansion of Centrally Sponsored Schemes","Substantial plan transfers take place through the Centrally Sponsored Schemes (CSS) which have been greatly expanded in the Twelfth Plan.",""]],"67":[[1,"36","7","Stakeholder Consensus-Building Planning Process","Finally, the planning process must serve as a way of getting different stakeholders to work together to achieve broad consensus on key issues.",""],[1,"36","8","Wide Consultation and Expert Group Advisory Process","The Planning Commission has consulted widely over the past two years with other Ministries, with State Governments, with experts and also with Civil Society Organisations (CSOs).",""]],"68":[[1,"46","2","Integrated Child Development Services Expansion and Restructuring","Meanwhile, the ICDS programme will be expanded and comprehensively restructured in the Twelfth Plan to make it more effective.",""]],"69":[[1,"50","3","Electricity Generation Capacity Expansion","The Twelfth Plan aims to add another 88,000 MW.",""],[1,"50","3","Fuel Supply Security Measures for Power Generation","delivery of power depends critically on solving serious fuel availability problems that have arisen relating to coal and natural gas.",""],[1,"50","3","Price Pooling for Imported Fuel","The problem can be resolved by resorting to some form of price pooling and this must be explored.",""],[1,"61","4","Urgent Fuel Supply Assurance for Power Stations","The fuel supply problem affecting electric power generation stations that have been commissioned but do not have adequate assurance of supply of coal or gas, and the problems of power stations currently under implementation which have yet to tie up fuel supply agreements, need to be addressed urgently.",""],[1,"61","5","Coal Pricing Pooling System","One way of resolving this problem is through a system of pricing pooling.",""],[1,"80","4","Acceleration of Power Sector Projects","attending to the outstanding issues facing these projects must be given a high priority.",""]],"70":[[1,"61","4","Coal India Domestic Coal Production Expansion","Coal India must take steps to enhance its domestic production capability",""],[1,"67","4","Inter-Ministerial Coordination Mechanism for Coal Project Clearances","A special mechanism for inter-Ministerial coordination needs to be set up to accelerate processing of",""],[1,"67","5","Time-Bound Energy Project Implementation","these projects in a time bound manner.",""],[1,"67","5","Review of Coal Nationalisation Policy","the policy of nationalisation of coal itself needs to be reviewed as was pointed out in the Eleventh Plan.",""],[1,"67","5","Private Sector Participation in Coal Production","If private sector producers are allowed in petroleum, which is a more valuable resource, there is no reason why they should not be allowed in coal.",""]],"71":[[1,"14","4","Energy Pricing and Primary Energy Sector Liberalization Policy","It also includes policies related to energy pricing, and greater involvement of the private sector in exploration and development of primary energy sources.",""],[1,"60","2","Energy Supply Security and Fuel Diversification Strategy","We need to have sufficient flexibility to be able to alter our fuel composition to respond to movements in energy prices.",""],[1,"60","2","Long-Term Energy Supply Agreements and Overseas Asset Acquisition","We also need to develop stable long-term steady sources of supply for different fuels relying on long-term supply agreements with countries in different geographies, and through asset acquisition abroad.",""],[1,"61","7","NELP Contract Clarification Committee","A committee under Dr. C. Rangarajan has been set up to make",""],[1,"62","1","Future NELP Contract Pricing Rules","recommendations on future NELP contracts, which would avoid uncertainty and establish clear rules regarding the pricing of oil and gas from future NELP fields.",""]],"72":[[1,"43","10","Supercritical and Ultra-Supercritical Coal Power Technologies","efficient technologies in coal-based electricity gen- eration such as the introduction of super critical and ultra super critical boilers.",""],[1,"43","10","Industrial, Agricultural, and Office Energy Efficiency Promotion","It would require active promotion of energy efficiency in industries, farms and offices, and the promotion of more energy effi- cient appliances through policies of branding and mandatory standards.",""],[1,"43","10","Energy-Efficient Vehicle Policy Development","Transport policies and related technologies for more energy efficient vehicles will need to be developed and adopted.",""]],"73":[[1,"44","1","Green National Accounts Estimation Template","the Planning Commission has commissioned an Expert Group under Professor Partha Dasgupta to prepare a template for estimat- ing green national accounts which would measure national production while allowing for negative effects on national resources.",""],[1,"147","1","Green National Accounts Estimation Template","the Planning Commission has commissioned an Expert Group under Professor Partha Dasgupta to prepare a template for estimating green national accounts, which would measure national production while allowing for the negative effects on national resources.","Planning Commission"],[1,"147","9","Environmental Tax on Polluting Products","An 'environment' or 'green' tax is imposed on a product (or a complementary product) that damages the environment, in an attempt to reduce its produc- tion or consumption.",""]],"74":[[1,"14","4","Urbanization Management Policy","Policies for effective management of urbanisation are increasingly important and this is a new challenge facing the economy.",""],[1,"68","5","Urban Governance and Project Implementation Strengthening","cities, especially the larger ones, and progressively even the smaller ones, are encouraged and enabled to draw resources from the market and the private sector.",""],[1,"68","5","Strategic Urban Land Management","They will also have to manage their land resources more strategically, both to ensure better land use and to secure what will be a principal resource for their future financial needs.",""],[1,"68","5","Urban Service Charge Recovery Reform","They must become able to recover adequate service charges, and equitably, from their inhabitants, which will require them to demonstrate an ability to deliver better and more reliable services.",""],[1,"68","6","JNNURM-II Urban Governance and Capacity Strengthening Programme","A new JNNURM-II incorporating the learning from JNNURM-I will be a major feature of the Twelfth Plan.",""]],"75":[[1,"14","5","Public-Private Partnership Infrastructure Development Strategy","One such departure is the adoption in the Eleventh Plan of Public Private Partnership as a strategy for developing infrastructure.",""],[1,"15","1","Public-Private Partnership Infrastructure Delivery","Emphasising PPPs where possible, does not imply a blind pursuit of PPPs as the only means of implementing infrastructure projects.",""],[1,"15","1","PPP Transparency and Competitive Bidding Procedures","The Central Government has evolved procedures and bidding documents which seek to achieve the objectives of transparency and robust competitive bidding for PPP projects and has encouraged state Governments to proceed on the same basis.","Central Government | state Governments"],[1,"15","2","Infrastructure Development through Public-Private Partnerships","Both the Central Government and many State Governments have embarked on ambitious programmes of developing infrastructure through PPPs.",""],[1,"15","2","PPP Project Monitoring and Problem-Resolution Mechanism","Both the Centre and the States must evolve mechanisms to moni- tor PPP projects closely to ensure that they come up to the expected standards of service delivery and also to identify problems in project implementation so that these can be resolved as speedily as possible in a fair and transparent manner.",""],[1,"52","1","Public-Private Partnership Infrastructure Development","efforts have been made to induct private participation in the development of infrastructure.",""],[1,"52","4","PPP Concession Agreement and Compliance Monitoring Framework","These are important concerns but they can be addressed by well-drafted concession agreements and strict monitoring to ensure that PPP concessionaires abide by their commitments.",""],[1,"52","4","People's Participation in PPP Design and Monitoring","the need for 'people's' participation in the design and monitoring of PPP schemes becomes crucial.",""],[1,"121","4","Private Sector Participation and PPP Promotion","The strategy for the Twelfth Plan encourages private sector participation directly as well as through various forms of PPPs, wherever desirable",""],[1,"122","6","Public-Private Partnership Initiative","The government's current initiatives in the area of PPPs are designed to achieve these objectives.",""],[1,"122","7","PPP Project Appraisal and Approval Institutional Structure","Setting up robust institutional structure for appraising and approving PPP projects",""],[1,"122","8","Standardized Model Concession Agreement Development","Developing standardised documents such as model concession agreements across infrastructure sectors",""],[1,"125","7","PPP Project Appraisal and Approval Process","The project proposals are appraised by the Planning Commission and approved by the PPPAC.",""],[1,"125","10","PPP Project Technical Assistance Scheme","Planning Commission has operationalised a scheme for technical assistance to project authorities by providing consultants for projects.","Planning Commission"],[1,"125","10","India Infrastructure Project Development Fund","The Ministry of Finance has also created an India Infrastructure Project Development Fund (IIPDF) to provide loans for meeting development expenses, including the cost of engaging consultants for PPP projects.","Ministry of Finance"],[1,"125","11","Viability Gap Funding Scheme","The VGF Scheme was notified in 2006 to enhance the financial viability of competitively bid infrastructure projects, which are justified by economic returns, but do not pass the standard thresholds of financial returns.",""],[1,"126","7","Standardized PPP Concession Bidding and Award Framework","The government has decided to formulate standard documents for bidding and award of PPP concessions.",""],[1,"127","3","Model Request for Qualification Document for PPP Projects","Model Request for Qualification (RFQ) Document for PPP projects",""],[1,"127","4","Model Request for Proposal Document for PPP Projects","Model Request for Proposal (RFP) Document for PPP projects",""],[1,"127","10","PPP Project Monitoring Guidelines","The government has recently issued Guidelines for Monitoring of PPP Projects.",""],[1,"127","11","Viability Gap Funding Scheme for Infrastructure PPPs","Guidelines for Financial Support to PPPs in Infrastructure (VGF Scheme)",""],[1,"127","18","PPP Project Monitoring and Compliance Reporting Mechanism","for monitoring of PPP projects that would ensure compliance of the contractual framework contained in the concession agreements with a view to safe- guarding the interests of the public exchequer and the users.","Central Ministries"],[1,"127","20","Private Infrastructure Investment Promotion Framework","Private investment in infrastructure is being encouraged in an environment which ensures competition and transparency.",""],[1,"127","20","Public Interest Safeguards for Infrastructure Investment","Protection of public interest is being ensured by institutionalising the necessary frameworks and processes for due diligence, checks and balances.",""],[1,"131","9","State PPP Nodal Secretariat Establishment Support","Therefore, the Planning Commission may need to provide financial assistance (ACA) to the State Governments for the setting up a nodal Secretariat for PPP in each State.",""],[1,"131","10","State PPP Secretariat Capacity-Building and Project Appraisal Support","The aforesaid PPP Secretariat in each State would be responsible for identifying areas in the respective States amenable to PPP, conceptualise the projects, initiate and approve feasibility stud- ies, appraise and approve bid documentation, guide the process and so on.",""]],"76":[[1,"52","3","Model Schools in PPP Mode","The Central Government has approved setting up of 2,500 Model Schools in PPP mode",""],[1,"130","7","PPP School Establishment Scheme","A scheme for setting up 2,500 schools under PPP mode is being rolled out in the Twelfth Plan.",""],[1,"130","8","Private Service Delivery Agreement for Underprivileged Student Tuition Support","The respective rights and obligations of the private entity and the government will be codified in an agreement with the former undertaking to deliver the agreed service on the payment of a uni- tary charge by the government.",""],[1,"130","9","Public-Private Partnership Schools Scheme","The scheme for 2,500 PPP schools should be viewed as an opportunity to evolve innovative ways to empower and enable non-government players to engage in providing world-class education, especially to children from low-income families.",""]],"77":[[1,"131","2","Secondary and Tertiary Care Hospitals through PPPs","Planning Commission is also in the process of preparing a scheme for setting up secondary and tertiary care hospitals through PPPs at various District Headquarters.",""],[1,"131","2","Multi-Specialty Health Care Delivery Mechanism","The principle objective of the scheme is to create a health care delivery mechanism comprising multi-specialty hospital to meet the growing health care needs of the poor, and for supplementing human resources in the sector by setting up nursing schools and medical colleges.",""],[1,"131","3","District and Tertiary Hospital Expansion Scheme","It is expected that in the Twelfth Plan, the proposed scheme will be rolled out by the Government, and a 200-bed district-level hospital would serve a catchment area of about 8-10 lakh of population (20 lakh for a 300-bed tertiary care hospital).",""]],"78":[[1,"131","7","Financial Support Scheme for Social Sector PPPs","A scheme for financial support to PPPs in the social sectors is being formulated as part of the Twelfth Plan initiative to enhance investments and coverage in social sectors, and also to expand the role of private participation.",""],[1,"131","8","Viability Support Funding for EWS Service Delivery Concessions","The scheme envisages that capital invest- ment and recurring costs to be incurred by a non- government entity on the delivery of services to EWS families, based on a concession agreement between government (or a statutory authority) and a non- government entity, will be provided by the respective State Governments, who in turn will be eligible for Viability Support Funding (VSF) from the Central Government.",""],[1,"132","3","Expansion and Reinforcement of Private Participation in Social Sectors","These initiatives will be expanded and reinforced during the Twelfth Plan, especially in social sec- tors such as health, education, skill development and so on with a view to meeting the investment",""]],"79":[[1,"129","7","Micro Irrigation Systems PPP Scheme","A scheme for setting up Micro Irrigation Systems (MIS) through PPP will be launched",""],[1,"130","1","Integrated Cluster Irrigation Scheme","by employing more efficient means of irrigation in integrated clusters.",""],[1,"130","2","Modern Storage Facilities Scheme under PPP and VGF","A scheme for setting up modern storage facili- ties through PPP under the VGF has been formulated in pursuance of the Government decision to create 2MMT of modern storage facilities in the form of silos.",""],[1,"130","3","PPP Silo Construction and Operation Programme","Silos will be constructed and operated under the PPP mode across several states.",""]],"80":[[1,"58","3","Negotiation of Additional Preferential Trade Agreements","More such agreements are being negotiated with the European Union and with Australia.",""],[1,"60","4","Regional Cooperation and Bilateral Engagement Initiative","India needs to engage more pro-actively with the global community at bilateral, regional and multilateral levels.",""],[1,"60","4","Neighbourhood Cooperation Priority","Special attention needs to be paid to our immediate neighbours.",""],[1,"60","4","Dialogue Mechanism Between Apex Planning Agencies","it may be desirable to begin the process of instituting dialogue between the apex plan- ning agencies of neighbouring countries.",""],[1,"60","5","Proactive Multilateral Engagement Strategy","India needs to be proactive in traditional multilateral forums such as the United Nations (UN), and also participate proactively in new emerging forums of importance such as the G20, IBSA, BASIC and so on.",""],[1,"60","5","Active Role in WTO and UNFCCC Deadlock Resolution","India will also need to play an active role in breaking deadlocks and ensuring progress on two economically important multilateral forums, the World Trade Organization (WTO) and United Nations Framework Convention on Climate Change (UNFCCC).",""],[1,"98","2","Five-Year Foreign Trade Policy","the government announces a Foreign Trade Policy (FTP) every five years.",""],[1,"98","16","Broad-based Trade and Investment Agreement Negotiation","India is negotiat- ing a Broad-based Trade and Investment Agreement (BTIA) with European Union and on its comple- tion it would result in increase bilateral trade and flows of investment between the two trading part- ners.",""],[1,"98","17","Export Market Diversification Strategy","India has been pursuing a policy of market diversification directing her export promotion efforts at Asia and ASEAN, Latin America and Africa through Focus Market initiatives and bilateral trade agreements.",""],[1,"99","1","ASEAN Trade and Investment Negotiation Engagement","Having signed the India-ASEAN Trade in Goods Agreement, negotiations on Agreements on Trade in Services and Investment continued with a view to be concluded by end 2012.",""],[1,"99","3","SAFTA Tariff Liberalization Programme","Agreement on SAFTA, inter alia, prescribes a phased Tariff Liberalization Programme (TLP) according to which peak tariff rate maintained by India for all items other than those included in the sensitive list is 8 per cent which will reduce to 5 per cent with effect from 1 January 2013.",""],[1,"99","6","Double Taxation Avoidance Agreement with Nepal","Double Taxation Avoidance Agreement (DTAA) with Nepal was signed, which will help exporters and investors of both the countries in further improving mutual busi- ness engagements.",""],[1,"99","7","Focus: LAC Trade Promotion Programme","An integrated programme 'Focus: LAC' which was launched in November 1997 has been extended up to March 2014 in order to consolidate the gains of the previous years and significantly enhance India's trade with the Latin America and the Caribbean (LAC) region.",""],[1,"99","7","Focus Market Scheme Expansion for Latin America and the Caribbean","The new FTP (2009-2014), pays special attention to LAC and 16 new markets of LAC region have been incorpo- rated under Focus Market Scheme (FMS) bring- ing the total 231.",""],[1,"99","7","Market-Linked Focus Product Scheme Expansion","Under the Market-Linked Focus Product Scheme (MLFPS), 13 markets have been identified, includes Brazil.",""],[1,"99","7","Preferential Trade Agreement Expansion with Mercosur and Chile","The Preferential Trade Agreements (PTAs) with Mercosur and Chile are being expanded.",""],[1,"99","8","Focus Africa Trade Expansion Programme","The 'Focus Africa' Programme was initially launched with focus on seven countries of Sub- Saharan African (SSA) Region, namely South Africa, Nigeria, Mauritius, Tanzania, Kenya, Ghana and Ethiopia.",""],[1,"100","7","WTO Coalition Engagement for Doha Round Conclusion","India has been working closely with various coalition groups in the WTO towards an early development-oriented conclusion of the Doha Round.",""]],"81":[[1,"98","4","Export Promotion for Employment-Intensive Industries","Give a focused thrust to exports of employment- intensive industries.",""],[1,"98","8","North-Eastern Region Export Promotion","Encourage exports from the North-Eastern Region given its special place in India's economy.",""]],"82":[[1,"59","4","Domestic Food Trade and Buffer Stock Policy Calibration","Domestic import and export policies and our buffer stock policy have to be calibrated to meet domestic demand while responding to developments in global markets.",""]],"83":[[1,"56","6","Public Confidence and Nuclear Safety Enhancement","However, much greater attention will have to be paid towards improving the confidence of the people and especially in providing world-class systems to counter the risks associated with this form of energy.",""]],"84":[[1,"62","2","Twelfth Plan Government Programmes","The Twelfth Plan lays out an ambitious set of government programmes, which will help to achieve the objective of rapid and inclusive growth.",""],[1,"108","5","Thirteenth Finance Commission Award Implementation","The 13th FC award for 2011-15 is under implementation with some changes in the fiscal responsibility and budget management",""],[1,"110","18","State Revenue Realisation Improvement and Non-Plan Expenditure Control","therefore, have to look at improving revenue realisation and controlling non-Plan expenditure.",""],[1,"112","9","State Own-Resource Mobilization and Revenue Enhancement Measures","The States will have to step up efforts to increase their own tax and non-tax revenue collections through better tax administra- tion, plugging the scope for leakages and levying of cost-based user charges.",""],[1,"116","9","State and Union Territory Plan Assistance Allocation","The Twelfth Plan proposes to provide ₹8,57,786 crore at current prices as CA to State/UT Plans.",""],[1,"116","9","Gadgil-Mukherjee Formula Driven Normal Central Assistance","20.84 per cent (that is, ₹1,78,739 crore) has been earmarked for the Gadgil-Mukherjee Formula driven NCA.",""],[1,"116","9","Special Plan Assistance for Special Category States","Special Plan Assistance (SPA) for Special Category States",""],[1,"116","9","Special Central Assistance for Border Areas Development Programme and Hill Area Development","Special Central Assistance (SCA) for the Border Areas Develop- ment Programme (BADP)/Hill Area Development",""],[1,"118","6","Medium-Term Expenditure Framework and Revenue Deficit Accounting Reform","recognition to the concept of 'Effective Revenue Deficit' and to introduce medium-term expendi- ture framework statement along with the other three statements envisaged under the FRBMA.",""],[1,"118","6","Priority Scheme Resource Reallocation","This would help Ministries/Departments to reallocate resources on priority schemes and weed out those which have outlived their utility.","Ministries/Departments"]],"85":[[1,"35","4","Infrastructure Project Implementation Constraint Removal","This calls for urgent action to tackle implementation constraints in infrastructure which are holding up large projects, combined with action to deal with tax related issues which have created uncertainty in the investment climate.",""],[1,"49","1","National Investment Approval Board for Large Infrastructure Projects","set up a National Investment Approval Board chaired by the Prime Minister and including all key Ministers and to amend the Transaction of Business Rules so that statutory clearances under various Acts for all infra- structure projects above a given size are given by the Board",""],[1,"61","3","Removal of Infrastructure Project Implementation Bottlenecks","The next important short-term action must be to remove the impediments to implementation of projects in infrastructure, especially in the area of energy.",""]],"86":[[1,"152","13","Lighting, Labelling and Super-efficient Equipment Programme","10. Lighting, Labelling and Super-efficient Equipment Programme",""],[1,"159","4","Energy-Efficient Technology Diffusion in Cement Manufacturing","Diffusion of energy-efficient technologies in var- ious sub processes of cement manufacture.",""],[1,"159","5","Waste Heat Recovery Systems","Waste heat recovery systems for moisture reduc- tion in coal and raw materials and for power generation.",""],[1,"159","7","Waste-to-Fuel Promotion and Policy Rationalization","Increased use of waste as alternate fuels, ratio- nalizing the various policies that regulate this activity.",""],[1,"159","8","Increase Use of Blended and Composite Cements","Increased blending using fly ash from thermal power plants and granulated blast furnace slag from steel plants, and the increased use of composite cements.",""],[1,"159","9","Coal Quality Improvement Initiative","Improving quality of coal before its use in the industry.",""],[1,"159","11","Blended Cement Public Procurement Reform","Increase of blended cements in the public pro- curement process.",""],[1,"162","25","Energy-Efficient Technology Support for Non-PAT Industrial Units","preparation of detailed project reports and finance for adoption of energy-efficient technologies, are particularly important for non-PAT industrial units, which are smaller in size and cannot arrange such help on their own.",""],[1,"162","27","Energy Conservation Fund for Industrial Energy-Efficient Technology Upgradation","Energy Conservation Fund could be used to leverage and/or finance energy-efficient technology upgradation of the domestic industry, particularly non-PAT industry, on terms softer than commercial borrowing.",""]],"87":[[1,"167","4","Energy Efficiency Standards and Labelling Programme","Over the Eleventh Plan period, the standards and labelling programme of the Bureau of Energy Efficiency BEE has enabled consumers to identify and purchase more energy-efficient appliances.",""],[1,"167","5","Tightening of Appliance Energy Labelling Norms","The BEE has tightened the labelling norms for refrigerators and air conditioners w.e.f. 1 January 2012, and has notified a second tightening of norms to come into effect from 1 January 2014.",""],[1,"168","3","Enhanced LED Bulb and Tube Light Procurement","During the Twelfth Plan period, enhanced procurement of LED bulbs and LED tube lights could create the sales volume necessary to bring down prices to levels where large scale penetration of LED lights in India would become a reality.",""],[1,"168","5","Super-Efficient Fan Promotion and Market Penetration Initiative","During the Twelfth Plan period, development, introduction and market penetration of super-efficient fans, which are rated at 35 or less, will be promoted in a manner that boosts their sales volume, while also making them affordable.",""],[1,"168","6","Energy-Efficient Motor Labelling Programme","A more aggressive labelling programme that will help in selection of energy-efficient motors may be more effective.",""],[1,"168","6","Energy-Efficient Motor Product Branding Initiative","Branding of products containing efficient motors (for example, 'energy efficient motor inside') could help inform the end-consumers about the energy efficiency of products they are buying.",""],[1,"168","7","Super-Efficient Equipment Programme","During the Twelfth Plan period, the Super- Efficient Equipment Programme (SEEP) for super- efficient fans, LED bulbs and tube lights, seeks to incentivize the sale of these products to increase their volumes and bring down their prices for large-scale adoption.",""],[1,"177","17","Top Runner Energy-Efficient Appliances Program","Top runner program: Developing the world's best energy efficient appliances, Ministry of Economy, Trade and Industry, Government of Japan.","Ministry of Economy, Trade and Industry, Government of Japan"]],"88":[[1,"152","10","Vehicle Fuel Efficiency Programme","7. Vehicle Fuel Efficiency Programme",""],[1,"163","2","Fuel Efficiency Norms for the Automobile Industry","India needs to urgently introduce fuel efficiency norms for the automobile industry to address both energy and environmentchallenges.",""],[1,"163","3","Fuel Efficiency Standards and Vehicle Labelling Framework","Fuel efficiency norms can be defined within a 'standards and labelling' framework.",""],[1,"163","4","Vehicle Efficiency Labelling Scheme","Vehicles should carry prominent labels similar to those made popular by the appliance labelling scheme introduced by the BEE.",""],[1,"163","12","Vehicle Weight Standards Reform","Therefore, standards must contain an explicit disincentive against up-weighting of vehicles.",""],[1,"164","2","Passenger Car Fuel Efficiency Scheme","The BEE has already proposed a fuel efficiency scheme for passenger cars, and sought feedback on the scheme at a public consultation held on 1 November 2011.",""],[1,"164","3","Alternative Proposal Publication and Public Consultation Process","BEE is in the process of publishing an alterna- tive proposal based on the inputs received.",""],[1,"164","4","Diesel Fuel Consumption Standards for Heavy Commercial Vehicles","Therefore, norms must be defined for these vehicles also at the earliest- say, by the end of 2012.",""],[1,"164","6","Fuel Efficiency Norms Development","The definition of fuel efficiency norms must not only be expedited, but also be based on public consultations with all stakeholders including the citizens groups and the automo- bile industry.",""],[1,"164","7","Electric Vehicle Promotion Policy and Charging Infrastructure","A clear-cut policy should be put into place for encouraging electric vehicles, including facilities for recharging.",""]],"89":[[1,"154","3","Incentives for Indigenous Wind Turbine Design and Manufacturing","Incentivising the industry to invest in indigenous design and manufacture of turbines suited for India's low wind speed regimes.",""]],"90":[[1,"154","10","Jawaharlal Nehru National Solar Mission","The Jawaharlal Nehru National Solar Mission (JNNSM) envisages grid parity for solar power by 2022 and sets an ambitious target of setting up 20,000 MW for solar power with phased scale-up of capac- ity, coupled with technological innovation.",""],[1,"154","10","Feed-in Tariff Incentives for Grid-Connected Solar Plants","The Government has facilitated generous financial incen- tives for grid-connected solar plants in the form of feed-in tariffs valid for 25 years.",""],[1,"154","10","State-Level Solar Capacity Acceleration Incentives","The Government has also incentivized state-level utilities to acceler- ate solar capacity addition by mandating a three per cent solar power target by 2022 (under the National Tariff Policy) and by providing opportunity for addi- tional revenue streams through instruments such as Renewable Energy Certificates (RECs).",""],[1,"155","3","Relaxation of Micro-grid Project Capacity Caps","First, there is a need for relaxing the cap on total and site-based project capacity.",""],[1,"155","3","Generation-Based Incentive for Off-Grid Solar Projects","Second, there is merit in providing a generation-based incentive, similar to that provided for grid-connected systems.",""],[1,"155","6","Single Nodal Agency for JNNSM Implementation and Monitoring","it is important that a single nodal agency is made responsible for the overall monitoring and implementation of the JNNSM.",""],[1,"155","7","Priority Lending Classification for Solar Power Projects","The government should immediately classify solar power projects as 'priority lending' so that banks start giving it due importance in their credit plans.",""],[1,"155","8","Institutional Design for Decentralized Off-Grid Solar Power Systems","Further discussion is needed in designing the institutional structures for ownership and operation of decentralised off-grid solar power systems.",""],[1,"156","3","Technology-Neutral Solar Electricity Tariff Policy","The electricity tariff policy of the Government should be neutral to the type of solar technology being deployed in the approved projects.",""]],"91":[[1,"155","9","Domestic Solar PV Manufacturing Mandate","In order to encourage indigenous manufacturing of components used in solar power generation, GoI has mandated for all the projects allotted in 2010-11 that 100 per cent PV modules should be manufactured in India.",""],[1,"155","9","Domestic Solar Cell Manufacturing Requirement","It has been further mandated that from 2011-12 onwards, 100 per cent of cells used in indigenous modules should be manufactured in India.",""],[1,"156","1","Balanced Solar Photovoltaic Manufacturing Policy","The manufacturing policy should strike a balance between these two objectives, and mandate a more gradual indigenisation of cell and module manufacture.",""],[1,"156","2","Customs Duty Reform for Solar Industry Inputs","Our customs duty structure should not be inverted along solar industry's value chain (basic and intermediate inputs should not attract higher tariffs than finished products).",""],[1,"156","4","Tariff and Domestic Policy Support for Domestic Solar Manufacturers","Export subsidies (explicit and implicit) avail- able to foreign manufacturers must be matched by tariff/domestic policy to the extent it pro- vides a level playing field to the domestic solar manufacturers.",""],[1,"156","5","Research and Development Funding Access for Indigenous Manufacturers","R&D efforts for indigenous manufacturers should be incentivised by permitting them to compete with government laboratories for research funding through the budgetary sources.",""]],"92":[[1,"152","20","Super-Critical Coal Power Plant Adoption","It has already been announced that 50 per cent of the Twelfth Plan target and the coal-based capacity addition in the Thirteenth Plan would be through super-critical units, which reduce the use of coal per unit of electricity produced.",""],[1,"152","21","Ultra-Supercritical Power Technology Research and Development","It is also necessary to invest in research and development of ultra-supercritical (USC) units (Box 4.2).",""],[1,"153","2","Ultra-Supercritical and Supercritical Coal Technology Development","from a medium term perspective, development of USC and SC technology should be pursued vigorously.",""],[1,"153","3","Ultra-Supercritical Plant Development and Deployment Support","The government should support research and development to promote indigenous manufacturing of USC units.",""],[1,"153","3","Ultra-Supercritical Plant Incentive Scheme","Deployment of USC plants may be suitably incentivised and targeted during the Thirteenth Plan period.",""],[1,"153","4","Underground Coal Gasification Research and Pilot Projects","Given India's coal shortage, there should be greater research in this technology, including execution of a few pilot projects.",""],[1,"153","4","Coal Bed Methane Pilot Projects","Another potentially promising technology is coal bed meth- ane and it may be desirable to undertake some pilot projects in this regard.",""]],"93":[[1,"156","9","Prioritized Hydropower Development and Project Monitoring","Prioritised development of this resource, along with close monitoring of a few carefully selected hydro-projects is important during the Twelfth and the Thirteenth Five Year Plans.",""]],"94":[[1,"149","1","National Gene Fund for In Situ Conservation Capacity Building","Another fund, the National Gene Fund, has been established, which will be used to build capacity at Panchayat level for in situ conservation of genetic diversity of indigenous crop varieties.",""],[1,"150","7","High-Priority Ecological and Biodiversity Protection","The ecological and biodiversity issues should be dealt with on high priority.",""],[1,"151","1","Indian Alpine Initiative","An Indian Alpine Initiative should also be started for tracking the dynamics of alpine biomes in the context of climate change.",""]],"95":[[1,"151","4","Stakeholder Participation Policy Instruments","As part of the national sustainable development agenda, the Indian Government has taken measures to develop policy instruments that encourage the active participation of stakeholders and environmental NGOs in national development programmes at the grass-roots level.",""],[1,"151","4","Multi-Stakeholder Platform Engagement","The engagement of multi-stakeholder platforms such as Green Rating for Integrated Habitat Assessment (GRIHA), Joint Forest Management (JFM), women empowerment under Integrated Infrastructure Development (IID), National Knowledge Network (NKN) and Waste Minimization Circles (WMC) have",""],[1,"151","5","Strengthening Local Democratic Institutions for Natural Resource Management","At the local level, strengthening democratic institutions will lead to better and more sustained management of natural resources.",""]],"96":[[1,"149","3","Non-Monetary Incentives for Sustainable Resource Conservation","These incentives can be used as a bargaining tool by the Government to encourage conservation of resources in an economy.",""],[1,"172","6","Improved Wood-Burning Cook-Stove Dissemination","Dissemination of improved and efficient wood- burning cook-stoves.",""]],"97":[[1,"199","11","Urban Water Supply Source Reduction and Local Water Body Revival","First, we will have to reduce the length of the pipeline to bring water to homes, thus reducing costs, including electricity and pumping costs and 'leakage'. This means giving higher priority to reviving local water bodies and recharging groundwater, so that we can source water from as close as possible.",""],[1,"199","11","Demand Reduction and Water Efficiency in Households","Secondly, we must use less, not more water in our homes, so that we have less to treat and less to dispose off.",""],[1,"199","11","Decentralized Sewage Treatment and Reuse Systems","Thirdly, we must also cut the costs and transportation of sewage-use decentralised networks and use a variety of technologies to treat sewage as locally as possible.",""],[1,"199","11","Wastewater Reuse and Recharge Standards Reform","This would require change of standards so that groundwater pollution boards incentivise the reuse of wastewater for recharge.",""],[1,"200","4","Local Water Systems Protection and Management Investment","Invest in protection and management of local water systems",""],[1,"200","8","Sewage Management and Treatment System Reinvention","Reinvent sewage management and treatment systems for sustainability",""],[1,"200","9","Water and Waste Recycling and Reuse Plan","Plan to recycle and reuse every drop of water and waste",""],[1,"200","11","Municipal Water Demand Management and Loss Reduction Programme","The water supply programme of each city must provide for demand management and reduction in costs of supply.",""],[1,"200","13","Local Water Body Supply Requirement for City Water Projects","Each city must consider, as first source of sup- ply its local water bodies.",""],[1,"200","17","Sewage Treatment and Wastewater Reuse in Cities","cities would then consider treatment of sewage in open drains and treatment using alternative biological methods of wastewater treatment.",""],[1,"201","1","Wastewater Reuse and Recycling Planning","Cities must plan for reuse and recycling of waste at the very beginning of their water and waste plan and not as an after-thought.",""],[1,"201","1","Decentralized Sewage Treatment Plant Development","While a larger sewage treatment plant affords economies of scale in operation, a plant fitted to size-collecting the waste of a group of houses, an institution or even colonies- may have higher costs of operations but there are sub- stantial savings in the piping and pumping cost.",""]],"98":[[1,"201","4","Water-Efficient Manufacturing Technologies","reducing the consumption of fresh water through alternative water-efficient technologies or pro- cesses in various manufacturing activities;",""],[1,"201","5","Wastewater Reuse and Recycling for Industrial Secondary Use","reusing and recycling the waste water from such water intensive activities and making the reclaimed water available for use in the secondary activities within or outside the industry.",""],[1,"201","8","Industrial Water Audit and Water Efficiency Programme","The first step in this direction during the Twelfth Plan period will be to make comprehensive water audits a recurring feature of industrial activity so that we know what is being used by the industrial sector at present and so that changes can be monitored and the most cost-effective basket of water efficiency technologies and processes designed and implemented to reduce water demand and increase industrial value added per unit of water consumed.",""],[1,"201","9","Mandatory Corporate Water Footprint Reporting","The Planning Commission is working with leading representatives of Indian industry, as also the Ministry of Corporate Affairs, to make it mandatory for companies to include every year in their annual report, details of their water footprint for the year.","Ministry of Corporate Affairs"],[1,"201","12","Corporate Water Footprint Reduction Commitment","a commitment with a time-line that the company would reduce its water footprint by a definite amount (to be specified) within a definite period of time (to be specified).",""],[1,"201","13","Industrial Water Use Benchmarking and Clearance Enforcement","the Planning Commission, working with concerned government institutions, would develop benchmarks for specific water use in different industries and would ensure their application in the grant of clearances for industrial projects.","Planning Commission | concerned government institutions"],[1,"202","1","Water Use Efficiency and Water Auditing Initiative","Ministry of Water Resources has constituted a Committee with Industry Associations to carry out base line studies and to increase water use efficiency through water auditing, water footprints, and so on, including amendment in the Companies Act.","Ministry of Water Resources"],[1,"202","2","Water Use Charging and Conservation Incentive Reform","The second step would be to examine the measures to levy charges for water use and incentives for water conservation.",""],[1,"202","3","Industrial Water Audit Validation and Compliance Enforcement","The third step would be to publicly validate the water audit of industries so that this builds experience and confidence on the best practices.",""],[1,"202","4","Water Audit and User Behaviour Change Support","The water audit would also help identify training requirements and the best way of achiev- ing behavioural change within the business.",""],[1,"202","5","Government Water Use Audits and Conservation Targets","central and state governments need to set an example by undertaking their own audits of water use in their premises and setting targets for ensuring less water use and changes in technology and behav- iour that will reduce waste.",""],[1,"202","8","Water Audit and Water Use Advisory Services Development","undertake to develop expertise in water audits and water use advisory services;",""],[1,"202","10","Water Efficiency Information Gateway","provide a 'gateway' for accessing information about water saving and water efficiency technologies in rain-water harvesting, recycling and reuse, water conserving devices and support to helping behaviour change.",""],[1,"203","3","Consumer Water Use Information Requirement","A requirement to provide a minimum level of information to consumers on how to use water more wisely.",""],[1,"203","4","Corporate Water Efficiency Evidence-Building Requirement","A requirement that each company actively helps to improve the evidence base for water efficiency.",""],[1,"203","9","Industrial Water Use Regulatory Strengthening","The Planning Commission will work with the regulatory institutions to ensure that the system of water assessment is strengthened for enforcement.",""]],"99":[[1,"181","2","Completion of Ongoing Large Irrigation Projects","We also have many large projects underway that need to be expeditiously completed.",""],[1,"181","10","Accelerated Irrigation Benefits Programme","While financial capacities are being taken care of through programmes such as the Accelerated Irrigation Benefits Programme (AIBP),",""],[1,"182","2","Plan Progress Monitoring and Resource Allocation Linkage Mechanism","the Twelfth Plan proposes to put in place a systematic mechanism to monitor progress achieved and suggest measures needed to restore time schedules and link it to the annual allocation of plan resources to the States.",""],[1,"182","3","Accelerated Irrigation Benefit Programme","The AIBP was launched in 1996 to fast-track the implementation of ongoing major and medium irrigation projects which were in an advanced stage of completion.",""],[1,"182","9","Canal Repair and Maintenance Programme","Adequate funds should be allocated for timely repair and maintenance of the canals.",""],[1,"182","9","Canal Lining and Lift Irrigation Expansion","High priority should be given to the task of lining of the whole canal system and lift irrigation system should be installed on the banks of canals.",""],[1,"182","9","Farmer Cropping Pattern Adoption Support","Farmers should be persuaded to adopt appropriate cropping pattern for optimum use of water.",""],[1,"182","9","AIBP Support for Field Irrigation Canal Networks","AIBP assistance should be extended even for construction of Field Irrigation Canal networks.",""],[1,"182","9","Pre-Approval Land Acquisition Requirement for Irrigation Projects","Land acquisition needs to be completed before the project proposals are approved under AIBP.",""],[1,"182","9","Irrigation Agency Restructuring and Farmer Management Transfer","Institutional reforms such as restructuring of irrigation agencies including WUAs, irrigation management transfer to the farmers and promotion of self-financing of irrigation schemes is also required.",""],[1,"183","6","Strengthening the CADP and Participatory Irrigation Management Initiatives","The Twelfth Plan proposes major changes to strengthen these initiatives.",""],[1,"184","4","Completion of Ongoing MMI Projects","Complete, as far as possible, the huge backlog of ongoing MMI projects by prioritising the allocation of investible funds to ongoing projects while taking up new only as a matter of exception;",""],[1,"184","5","Command Area Development and Management Investments","Close the gap between IPC and IPU by at least 10 million ha by prioritising investments in Command Area Development and Management (CAD&M) projects",""],[1,"184","5","Restoration of Lost Irrigated Potential","restore an additional 2.2 million ha of lost irrigated potential through ERM works in old MMI projects",""],[1,"184","6","Irrigation Department Reform and Capacity-Building Programme","Catalyse, support and incentivise deep reform in irrigation departments by strengthening and broad-basing their human resources, by building capacities of civil engineers to move from a narrow construction-orientation to management roles, capacitating Water and Land Managment Institutes (WALMIs) and other irrigation training and research institutions and strengthening incentives in irrigation service provision and Irrigation Service Fee (ISF) collection;",""],[1,"184","7","MMI Information Architecture Redesign","Redesign the information architecture of the MMI sector to promote and support strong Management Information System at the MMI level and for improved water resources manage- ment at various levels.",""],[1,"184","9","Completion of Ongoing Project Backlog","Sixty-five per cent of total outlays will be ear- marked for completing the backlog of ongoing projects.",""],[1,"184","10","CAD&M and ERM Irrigation Expansion Projects","Fifteen per cent will be earmarked for CAD&M and ERM projects that are likely to quickly add to the irrigation potential.",""],[1,"184","10","Enhanced Central Assistance for CAD&WM Works","central assistance to such works will be enhanced from present 50 per cent to 75 per cent of the project costs.",""],[1,"184","12","National Irrigation Management Fund","The Twelfth Five Year Plan proposes the set- ting up of a National Irrigation Management Fund (NIMF) to catalyse and support demand for irriga- tion management and institutional reform.",""],[1,"185","2","Increase Funding for MMI System Operation and Maintenance","The starting point in breaking out of this low- level equilibrium has to be increasing resources available with MMI systems managers for proper operation and maintenance (O&M) of systems.",""],[1,"185","6","Non-Lapsable National Irrigation Management Fund","Government of India should establish a non- lapsable NIMF, which will reimburse to each State Irrigation Department a matching contribution to its own ISF collection from irrigators on a 1:1 ratio.",""],[1,"185","7","Irrigation Department Matching Grant Support","States desiring to avail of this matching grant maintain their own non-plan allocations to Irrigation Departments at the normal rate of growth of the aggregate non-plan budget of the State; that is, ensure that the Government of India (GoI)'s matching support is additional to the",""],[1,"186","2","Central Grant Allocation Incentive for MMI Systems","States allocate central grant to various MMI systems in proportion to the ISF collection of each MMI system; this would incentivise ISF collection among MMI staff and generate competition in augmenting GoI incentive;",""],[1,"186","3","State Matching Grant for Irrigation Service Fee Collection","States desiring to avail of this matching grant will-through their regulator-present a certified, audited state- ment furnishing detailed data on the actual ISF collected from irrigators from different MMI systems preferably through Independent Water Regulator (or comparable independent agency).",""],[1,"186","3","Independent Verification of Irrigation Service Fee Claims","The Central Government will have an indepen- dent verification undertaken of the claims on ISF collection (including a scrutiny of a sample of vouchers), based on which central matching grant will be released each year.",""],[1,"186","4","Performance Incentive Bonus for Water User Associations","the NIMF will provide a bonus on that portion of each State's ISF collection which has been collected through WUAs, as certified by the State's Water Regulator and verified by an independent agency designated by the Central Government.",""],[1,"186","5","Additional Bonus for Volumetric Water Delivery and ISF Collection","NIMF will provide an additional bonus on that portion of a State's ISF collection which accrues through volumetric water supply to WUAs at the outlet level under an irrigation service contract with each WUA.",""],[1,"186","6","Water Sector Reform and Governance Programme","NIMF will act as a catalyst to under- take reforms in the water sector such as improving water use efficiency, participatory community based managment of aquifers, reg- ulation of groundwater, revamping irrigation/ water resource departments and so on.",""],[1,"186","7","Irrigation Management Fund","It is expected that such an Irrigation Manage- ment Fund which incentivises ISF collection, with proper implementation, will produce myriad ben- eficial impacts.",""],[1,"186","8","Water Sector Reform Benchmarking Study and Reform Framework","The Union Ministry of Water Resources is instituting a study to evolve benchmarks for water sector reforms and gradation of States on their reform-friendliness.","Union Ministry of Water Resources"],[1,"186","10","Training and Research Capacity Initiative","Institutional and management reform will also require major initiatives in training and research.",""],[1,"187","1","Centres of Excellence in Irrigation Management","the GoI will provide a core grant of up to ₹20 crore to interested national institutes of eminence such as Indian Institutes of Technology, Indian Institutes of Management, National Institutes of Technology, Indian School of Business, and so on to establish centres of excellence in irrigation management to undertake research, education and training for senior MMI managers.",""],[1,"187","1","Management Training for Senior MMI Managers","Leading management institutes will be invited to develop and offer practical management training to senior MMI managers, with focus on performance management through planning, budgeting and monitoring systems.",""],[1,"187","1","Management Information Systems for MMI Schemes","provision is being made to involve leading players of the Information Technology enabled services of the country to work with State Governments to develop management information systems for MMI schemes with the specific purpose of generating real-time information on the working and performance of these systems to enable their benchmarking.",""],[1,"187","2","WALMI Training, Research, and Extension Strengthening Grant","the GoI will pro- vide each of the 14 WALMIs a grant-in-aid of ₹5 crore over the five-year period to strengthen their training, research and extension work",""],[1,"187","4","Central Assistance for Special Category and Tribal Area Projects","Central assistance at the rate of 90 per cent will continue for the projects in special category States, projects in KBK (undivided Kalahandi, Bolangir and Koraput) districts of Orissa and projects benefiting tribal areas, drought prone",""],[1,"187","6","Increased Central Assistance for Ongoing AIBP Projects","For general category States, the rate of cen- tral assistance under AIBP will be increased to 50 per cent in place of 25 per cent for all ongoing projects, provided the States initiate necessary actions and fully implement the reform agenda set out under the NIMF within the first two years of the Twelfth Plan, that is, during 2012-13 and 2013-14.",""],[1,"187","8","Lift Irrigation Schemes with Micro-Irrigation Conditional Support","Lift irrigation schemes will be taken up for AIBP support only on the condition of implementing micro-irrigation (drip and sprinkler) in the command area of the project.",""],[1,"187","8","Cluster-Based Micro-Irrigation Systems through Public-Private Partnership","Innovations may be tried in setting up micro irrigation systems (MIS) in clusters through Public-Private Partnership (PPP).",""],[1,"187","9","Monitoring of Centrally Assisted Schemes","Monitoring of all schemes under central assistance should include a specific mention of the progress made in respect of implementation of the reform agenda of the NIMF.",""],[1,"187","10","Command Area Development Mainstreaming in Irrigation Projects","To emphasise the centrality of Command Area Development (CAD) to all irrigations projects, the following steps will be initiated:",""],[1,"187","11","Integrated Irrigation Project Design with CAD Works","All irrigation project proposals (major, medium or minor) will include CAD works from the very beginning.",""],[1,"187","13","Mandatory CAD Integration for Irrigation Project Clearance","No investment clearance will be provided to any irrigation project devoid of CAD integration.",""],[1,"187","14","Command Area Development Parallel Works","There will be parallel action in each irrigation command wherein works in the distributary network and software activities of CAD will be undertaken simultaneously with head works",""],[1,"188","3","Field Channels and Drainage Improvement","CAD will concentrate on field channels and drainage.",""],[1,"188","4","Pipeline-Based Field Irrigation Channels","Pipeline-based field channels will be allowed, if necessary, especially in desert and drought-prone areas.",""],[1,"188","6","CAD Component Requirement for Revised Investment Clearance","Whenever projects come up for revised invest- ment clearance, they will need to incorporate a CAD component.",""],[1,"188","8","Integrated Water Planning and CAD Coordination Arrangement","Beginning with the Twelfth Plan, a Chief Engineer CAD) will work under the Member (Water Planning and Projects) of the CWC so that a more integrated view can be taken of AIBP and CAD.","Ministry of Water Resources | CWC"],[1,"188","9","Strengthening of the CWC with Additional Technical and Social Expertise","The CWC itself will be strengthened to also include agronomists, hydrogeologists and social scientists such as sociologists/anthropologists and social workers who understand the dynamics of engagement with civil society organisations for mobilising farmers in the command areas.",""],[1,"214","8","Irrigation Management Programme","The Central Sector Outlay for Twelfth Five Year Plan for Ministry of Water Resources (MoWR) is ₹18,118 crore, which envisages schemes like Irrigation Management Programme, Development of Water Resources Information System, Ground Water Management and Regulation (including aqui- fer mapping) and so on.","Ministry of Water Resources (MoWR)"]],"100":[[1,"179","2","Aquifer Mapping Programme","we need a participatory approach to sustainable management of groundwater based on a new programme of aquifer mapping.",""],[1,"189","8","Aquifer Mapping Programme","The Twelfth Plan proposes to initiate a comprehensive programme for the mapping of India's aquifers as a prerequisite and a precursor to the National Groundwater Management Programme, which will also be started during the Twelfth Plan period.",""],[1,"189","8","National Groundwater Management Programme","The Twelfth Plan proposes to initiate a comprehensive programme for the mapping of India's aquifers as a prerequisite and a precursor to the National Groundwater Management Programme, which will also be started during the Twelfth Plan period.",""],[1,"189","9","Integrated Aquifer Mapping and Groundwater Management Programme","Implementation of an integrated aquifer map- ping and groundwater management programme is possible only through strong partnerships between Government Departments, research institutes, gram panchayats/urban local bodies, industrial units, civil society organisations and the local community.",""],[1,"190","1","Aquifer Mapping and Groundwater Management Planning","This typology will become the starting point for planning aquifer mapping and designing interventions with regard to groundwater management, including work on recharging aquifers.",""],[1,"190","3","National Groundwater Management Programme","The Twelfth Plan will launch a National Groundwater Management Programme building upon these diverse experiences and carrying them to scale.",""],[1,"190","6","Groundwater Recharge Area Identification and Protection Strategy","Identification of groundwater recharge areas, resulting in protection and augmentation strategies;",""],[1,"190","9","Community-Level Groundwater Regulation Framework","Regulatory options at community level, includ- ing the appropriate regulatory mechanisms at the panchayat level.",""],[1,"190","13","Micro-Watershed Water Balance and Groundwater Planning","Foundation for Ecological Security (FES) taking a micro-watershed unit for water balance and planning groundwater use along with communities in Rajasthan, MP and AP.",""],[1,"190","15","Local Youth Para-Professional Training and Drinking Water Initiative","Training programmes and drinking water initiatives by ACT in Kutch training local youth as para-professionals in their quest for improved groundwater management.",""],[1,"192","1","Detailed Aquifer Mapping and Hydrogeological Survey","detailed aquifer map- ping exercises at a scale 1:50000 will be conducted to delineate aquifers to be tapped, assess their storage and transmission potential, seasonal fluctuations in water levels, extent of natural monsoon recharge and the quantum of base-flow or rejected recharge.",""],[1,"192","1","Water User Groups and Aquifer Management Associations","Given the relatively small size of holdings, Water User Groups (WUGs) will be formed around each new tubewell, which would federate into larger Aquifer Management Associations (AMA).",""],[1,"192","2","Steering Committee for Scheme Operational Guidelines and Project Sanctioning","A Steering Committee under the aegis of the Planning Commission and the Ministry of Water Resources, headed by an experienced and renowned professional in the field, will be responsible for preparing the detailed Operational Guidelines and sanctioning projects under the scheme.","Planning Commission | Ministry of Water Resources"]],"101":[[1,"179","7","Non-Structural Flood Management Mechanisms","Renewed focus on non-structural mechanisms for flood management.",""],[1,"204","3","Reservoir Construction and Flood Protection Works","Engineering/structural measures, including construction of reservoirs for impounding monsoon flow and its release after peak flows have passed (attenuation) and providing river embankments/ flood walls;",""],[1,"204","4","Non-Structural Flood Risk Management Measures","Non-structural measures, including flood plain zoning, flood forecasting, flood warning and flood proofing;",""],[1,"204","5","Catchment Area Treatment and Watershed Management","Catchment area treatment, including watershed management and restoring the health of natural drainages.",""],[1,"204","9","Traditional Drainage System Rehabilitation for Flood Management","some State governments (such as Bihar) have decided to broaden their strategy of tackling floods by placing greater emphasis on rehabilitation of traditional, natural drainage systems, leveraging the funds available under MGNREGA.",""],[1,"204","9","Civil Society Partnership for Flood Rehabilitation","civil society organisations will work in close partnership with the State government in this",""],[1,"205","3","Floodplain Management and Disaster Preparedness Measures","far greater priority will be given to non-structural measures such as the efficient management of flood plains, flood plain zoning, disaster preparedness and response planning, flood forecasting and warning, along with disaster relief, flood fighting including public health measures and flood insurance.",""],[1,"205","4","Extension of the CWC Flood Forecasting Network","The Twelfth Plan will draw up a concrete plan for extension of CWC's flood forecasting network in consultation with the State Governments and IMD to cover A, B-1, B-2 and C-class Cities located near rivers under the","Central Water Commission | State Governments | IMD"],[1,"205","5","Expansion of Reservoir Inflow Forecasting Network","In the Twelfth Plan this will be extended to an additional 160 reservoirs, which will cover 80-90 per cent of the total live storage capacity.",""],[1,"205","7","Flood Cushion Requirements for Water Storage Projects","Adequate flood cushion needs to be provided in all water storage projects, wherever feasible, to facilitate better flood management.",""],[1,"205","7","Flood Moderation Priority in Reservoir Regulation Policy","In highly flood prone areas, flood moderation will be given overrid- ing consideration in reservoir regulation policy, even at the cost of sacrificing some irrigation or power benefits.",""],[1,"205","8","Embankment Upkeep and Monsoon Surveillance","Given the large network embankments that already exist, great emphasis will be placed on their proper upkeep and surveillance during the monsoon season, centrally involving primary stakeholders in this process.",""],[1,"205","9","Flood Plain Zoning Model Bill","The Ministry of Water Resources has pre- pared a Model Bill on Flood Plain Zoning.","Ministry of Water Resources"],[1,"206","1","Flood Plain Zoning and Settlement Relocation Measures","demarcation of the flood plain zones by the concerned States in accordance with criteria suggested by CWC in the Model Flood Plain Zoning Bill and zone-specific strategies about the use of flood plains (including schemes of incentives and disincentives) need to be implemented.",""],[1,"206","1","Flood Plain Building Standards and Zoning Norms","The States should also bring out standard norms for types of buildings which can be constructed in different zones of flood plains so that required water way is available for pas- sage of the flood discharge.",""],[1,"206","3","River Basin Authorities for Integrated Flood Management","Integrated water resources management, including integrated flood management, demands setting up of River Basin Authorities with requisite managerial skills and appropriate delegation of powers.",""],[1,"206","3","Strengthening of the Central Water Commission Flood Management Functions","The strengthening of CWC is required in view of the proposed expansion of its hydrological and flood data collection network, flood data transmission and management of floods.",""],[1,"206","3","National Water Academy Centre of Excellence for Flood Mitigation Training","During the Twelfth Plan, the NWA will be developed as a Centre of Excellence for international training pro- grammes on matters pertaining to flood mitiga- tion so that up-to-date globally available know-how could be shared under such training programmes.",""],[1,"206","3","Strengthening of the National Water Academy for NDMA Training","The NWA, Pune will also be suitably strengthened to meet the requirement of the NDMA for conducting",""],[1,"206","4","Disaster Risk Reduction Training Programmes","trainings on disaster risk reduction programmes.",""],[1,"206","4","State-Level Project Planning and Implementation Arrangements","Project-specific planning and implementation is to be ensured by the State Governments.",""],[1,"206","4","State Flood Control Department Revamping","The present structure of the State flood control departments needs to be revamped so that they can discharge their role as prime flood managers in the State.",""],[1,"206","4","Flood Management Human Resource and Skills Development","The specific needs of human resources and their skill development need to be addressed.",""],[1,"206","5","River Basin Flood Mapping and Forecasting Programme","Digital Elevation Models (DEM) along major river systems including area falling in the flood affected zone in the range of 0.5-1 m will be prepared for all river basins.",""],[1,"206","5","Expansion of the Disaster Management Support Programme","The Disaster Management Support Programme will be expanded to include more river basins and the NDMA will provide necessary support to NRSC in this regard.","NDMA | NRSC"],[1,"206","5","Basin-Wise Flood Management Models","Basin-wise flood management models including ALTM technology based Digital Elevation Models, Inundation Forecast Models, Bathymetric Surveys and Cubature Study Models will be undertaken jointly by NRSC, CWC and concerned States.","NRSC | CWC"],[1,"206","5","Integrated Flood and Runoff Forecasting Models","Development of integrated mathematical models will be undertaken jointly by IMD and CWC for flood/runoff forecasting using weather parameters, rainfall observed and rainfall forecast.","IMD | CWC"]],"102":[[1,"220","3","Real-Time Standardized Rainfall Data Monitoring Network","Setting up of a real-time, standardised rainfall data monitoring network geared to an automated data archiving and retrieval system, which will be relatively free from human errors.",""],[1,"220","4","National Real-Time Rainfall Monitoring Network","A reliable set of real-time precipitation data covering the entire country can be generated in a standardised manner through an optimal network of ~60 DWRs with a radius of perception equal to ~200 km, provided they are meticulously supported and calibrated by ~40,000 automatic tele-metring rain gauges.",""],[1,"220","5","Automatic Rain Gauge Network Expansion","The proposed network of 40,000 ARGs can be accomplished by setting up an additional 30,000 of them during the Twelfth Plan through a cooperative effort between IMD and the States.","IMD | the States"],[1,"220","6","State Network Upgrading and Personnel Training Support","the Centre will finance the capital and operating cost of upgrading State networks and arrange for the training of personnel with professional skills to operate them",""]],"103":[[1,"178","7","Participatory Irrigation Project Management Reform","A move away from a narrowly engineering- construction-centric approach to a more multi- disciplinary, participatory management approach to our major and medium irrigation projects,",""]],"104":[[1,"179","9","Climate Change Adaptation Strategies under the National Water Mission","Adaptation strategies to mitigate the likely impact of climate change to be pursued under the National Water Mission (NWM).",""]],"105":[[1,"242","18","In Situ Biodiversity Conservation and Sustainable Use Programme","It also emphasised in situ conservation and sustainable use of biodiversity to enhance liveli- hood security, promotion and evaluation of ecosys- tem services in the national planning process.",""],[1,"242","18","Ecosystem and Biodiversity Economics Study","This includes the study of the economics of ecosystem and biodiversity; abatement of marine pollution and prevention of traffic in marine resources; the need for safe storage and disposal facilities for hazardous",""],[1,"242","18","Marine Pollution Abatement and Resource Traffic Prevention","This includes the study of the economics of ecosystem and biodiversity; abatement of marine pollution and prevention of traffic in marine resources; the need for safe storage and disposal facilities for hazardous",""],[1,"243","6","Technology-Based Forest Monitoring System","Technology-based monitoring of forest cover, biodiversity and growing stock including change-monitoring on periodical basis through dedicated satellite by 2017 and establishment of open web-based National Forestry and Environmental Information system for research and public accessibility by 2015.",""],[1,"243","8","Forestry Seed Bank and Model Nursery Establishment","Establish forestry seed bank in forest circles and Model Nursery in every district with information on public portal by 2014.",""],[1,"243","13","Biodiversity Management Plan Mapping and Preparation","Mapping and preparation of biodiversity management plans for deserts (both cold and arid), coastal areas, important coral zones, wetlands, mangroves and so on to be completed by 2017.",""],[1,"244","9","Seed Orchard and Silviculture Plot Revival Programme","Revive seed orchards and silviculture plots for various forest types of the country, as well as, for enlisted species under Minor Forest Produce/Non Timber Forest Produce (MFP/NTFP) including genetic improvement of and establishment of clonal orchards.",""],[1,"244","13","National Biodiversity Targets and Indicators Development","Develop national targets and indicators related to biodiversity",""],[1,"244","13","Biological Diversity Act Implementation Support","support actions to strengthen implementation of Biological Diversity Act, 2002 and ensure bio-safety for economic and social development of local communities.",""],[1,"244","14","Coastal Biodiversity Assessment and Ecosystem Restoration","Assess coastal biodiversity resources, ensure sustainable management, restoration of mangroves, coral reefs and wetlands and support livelihood.",""],[1,"246","8","Protection of Traditional Knowledge and Folk Art Safeguards","Providing sufficient and effective safeguards for the protection of traditional knowledge (TK) and folk art concerning biodiversity;",""],[1,"248","2","Strengthening of Botanical and Zoological Survey Capacity","Strengthening of Botanical Survey of India (BSI) and Zoological Survey of India (ZSI) in terms of manpower and infrastructure to scale up their mandated task of inventorisation of flora and fauna of the country needs to be achieved;",""],[1,"248","3","Biodiversity Information Systems Validation and Updation","Validation and updation of the Indian Biodiversity Information System (IBIS), the Indian Bio- resource Information System (IBIN), India Biodiversity Portal (IBP) and the Indian Ocean Census of Marine Life (IOCoML) needs to be undertaken, for which a consortium of research organisations needs to be created;",""],[1,"248","4","Taxonomic Collections Digitization Pilot","An effort to digitise and make available existing collections of taxonomic collections should be piloted;",""],[1,"248","5","Expansion of Forestry, Biodiversity, and Wildlife Research Mandates","The mandate of different institutes engaged in forestry, biodiversity and wildlife research requires to be broadened to accommodate emerging needs for collaborative multidisciplinary research.",""],[1,"248","6","Ecosystem Research Institute Establishment","It is proposed to establish an Ecosystem Research Institute (ERI) under MoEF for undertaking research in ecosystems, biodiversity and sustainable development.","MoEF"],[1,"248","7","Implementation of the Biological Diversity Act","The Biological Diversity Act 2002 has to be implemented at all levels throughout the country.",""],[1,"248","7","Constitution of Biodiversity Management Committees","Immediate steps need to be taken to constitute Biodiversity Management Committees (BMCs) at Gram Panchayats, Taluka Panchayats, Zilla Panchayats, as well as Nagarpalikas and Mahanagarpalikas.",""],[1,"248","7","Mandatory Levy of Biodiversity Collection Charges","Further, the BMCs need to be obligated to levy 'collection charges' as provided in the Biological Diversity Act.",""],[1,"248","8","National Biodiversity and Environment Information Grid","It is proposed to develop a national informa- tion grid for biodiversity, ecology and environment data for monitoring and management of natural resources.",""],[1,"253","1","Institutional Mechanism for Ecosystem and Biodiversity Research","Institutional mechanism for research on ecosystems, bio-diversity and sustainable development is vital for ensuring sustainability of ecosystem services and biodiversity maintenance and hence an institution for achieving this is a necessity.",""]],"106":[[1,"243","7","Village Green Guards and Community Foresters Engagement","Engagement of Village Green Guards/Community Foresters for every Joint Forest Management (JFM) village by 2016.",""],[1,"243","9","Wildlife Veterinary Training Programme","Twenty per cent of veterinary professionals in the country will be trained in treating wildlife.",""],[1,"244","7","Forest-Rangeland Restoration and Community Grazing Land Management","Restoration and intensification of forest-rangelands/grazing-land management and establish community grazing land around forest fringe villages.",""],[1,"244","8","Village Forest Committee Capacity Building for Forest Resource Management","Build capacity of Village Forest Committees/Joint Forestry Management Committees for management of forest resources including ecotourism.",""],[1,"244","12","Ecotourism and Participatory Eco-Development Support","Promotion of ecotourism and participatory eco-development support livelihood of local population.",""],[1,"248","11","Autonomous NTFP Management Agency","an autonomous agency needs to be set up with branches in all states.",""],[1,"248","11","Sustainable Livelihoods through NTFP Management Scheme","a new scheme for sustainable livelihoods through NTFP management including bamboo needs to be formulated.",""],[1,"248","12","National Grazing Policy and Pasture Management Scheme","There is an urgent need to focus on pasture management and formulation of grazing policy at the national level",""],[1,"248","12","Rangeland and Silvi-Pasture Rehabilitation Scheme","A new scheme on rangeland and silvi-pasture management for rehabilitation and productivity enhancement of rangelands, traditional grasslands on common/revenue lands around forest areas is required.",""],[1,"248","12","Fodder Storage and Value Addition Infrastructure","Infrastructural and institutional mechanism for fodder storage, value addition facilities, maintenance of germ-plasm banks and nurseries is",""],[1,"253","3","Rangeland and Silvi-Pasture Development Scheme","A new scheme has been proposed for rangeland and silvi-pasture development.",""],[1,"253","4","Rangeland and Fodder Development Programme","and productivity enhancement of existing rangelands and potential grasslands in common/revenue lands around forest areas, fodder bank and storage, value addition technologies and facilities, establishing linkages with existing institutes/Centre of Excellence on fodder and pasture management, conducting fodder research, developing rangeland and silvi-pasture models, germ-plasm banks, fodder nurseries and so on.",""]],"107":[[1,"249","2","Satellite-Based Forest Resource Assessment Scheme","A proposed scheme on Satellite-based Forest Resource Assessment will put in place a system for technology-based collection of baseline data and evaluation of forestry schemes with Geographic information system (GIS) mapping of areas under the Scheduled Tribes and Other Traditional Forest Dwellers (Recognition of Forest Rights) Act, 2006.",""],[1,"249","3","Revival of the Central Board of Forestry","it is proposed that the Central Board of Forestry (CBF) be revived with Prime Minister as Chairperson and Minister of Environment & Forests as Vice Chairperson, on the lines of National Board for Wildlife.","Ministry of Environment & Forests"],[1,"249","4","ICFRE Reorientation and Funding Augmentation","Reorientation of the Indian Council of Forestry Research and Education (ICFRE) on the lines of Indian Council of Agricultural Research (ICAR) with augmentation of funding also needs to be taken up during the Twelfth Five Year Plan.",""],[1,"249","5","Amendment of the Forest Working Plan Code","The Working Plan Code based on which for- est working plans are prepared and adhered to needs to be amended to incorporate new dimensions along with assigning specific responsibility to the cutting- edge level workers and for transferring the rights in the field with proper documentation.",""],[1,"249","6","Green Fund for Forestry Activities","There is a need for creation of a 'Green fund' for forestry activities by imposing forest develop- ment tax on sale of forest produce and forest con- servation tax/cess on sale of petroleum products and coal mining.",""],[1,"252","2","National Forestry Information System Networking and Forest Rights Tracking","The National Forestry Information System should enable networking with States for tracking changes in forest development, harvesting, trade and utilisation scenario with particular focus on issues of ownership and rights under Scheduled Tribes and Other Traditional Forest Dwellers (Recognition of Forest Rights) Act.",""],[1,"252","3","National Invasive Species Programme","A national programme specific to invasive species needs to be launched.",""],[1,"253","5","Remote Sensing-Based Forest Cover Monitoring System","Remote sensing-based forest cover monitoring in close collaboration with Forest Survey of India, National Remote Sensing Agency and Indian Institute of Remote Sensing has been proposed.",""],[1,"254","5","Forest Satellite Monitoring System","forest satellite for monitoring forest cover, NTFP resource, bio-diversity on periodical basis along with change monitoring has been proposed.",""]],"108":[[1,"249","7","Integrated Development of Wildlife Habitats","Integrated Development of Wildlife Habitats (IDWH) will continue to be the umbrella scheme for conservation and management of wildlife with focus on all species other than the tiger.",""],[1,"249","8","Tiger Conservation Flagship Programme","Tiger conservation, as led by the National Tiger Conservation Authority, needs to be continued as a flagship programme of the MoEF.","National Tiger Conservation Authority | MoEF"],[1,"249","8","Tiger Reserve Buffer Area Protection and Coexistence Initiative","This includes strengthen- ing the protection and furthering the coexistence agenda in the buffer areas of tiger reserves and vol- untary relocation along with regular monitoring of tiger population and their habitat.",""],[1,"249","9","National Elephant Conservation Authority and Elephant Reserve Protection Measures","Project Elephant needs a new focus under the plan through the creation of the National Elephant Conservation Authority (NECA) and notification of critical areas of Elephant Reserves as Ecologically Sensitive Areas under the Environment (Protection) Act 1986.",""],[1,"249","9","Human-Elephant Conflict Mitigation under Project Elephant","Special focus is required for mitigation of human-elephant conflict through strengthening the existing Project Elephant Scheme.",""],[1,"249","11","Wildlife Veterinary Care Strengthening","Scientific and socio-economic issues related to wildlife conservation including strengthening of veterinary care for wild animals;",""],[1,"249","12","Scientific Management of Protected Areas and Wildlife Conflict Mitigation","Scientific management of PAs and wildlife-rich areas outside PAs as well as mitigation of human- wildlife conflict;",""],[1,"249","15","Strengthening of IDWH and Project Elephant Schemes","Strengthening of IDWH and Project Elephant schemes is necessary to achieve the above objectives.",""],[1,"249","15","Operationalisation and Strengthening of Ecotourism for Local Livelihoods","two new schemes, namely, Operationalisation and Strengthening of Ecotourism for Local Livelihoods",""],[1,"249","15","Private Sector and Philanthropist Participation in Animal Welfare","Promoting Participation of Private Sector and Philanthropists in Animal Welfare are also proposed to be taken up.",""],[1,"249","16","Establishment of Animal Welfare Boards and SPCAs","Animal Welfare Boards need to be setup in all the States, including Society for Prevention of Cruelty to Animals (SPCAs) under the Prevention of Cruelty (Establishment of Societies for the Prevention of Cruelty to Animals) Rules, in all dis- tricts within all States.",""],[1,"250","1","Wildlife Protection and Conservation Investment","Significant increase in investment for better protection and conservation of wildlife",""],[1,"250","1","Institutional Mechanism Strengthening for Wildlife Management","strength- ening of institutional mechanism",""],[1,"250","1","Livelihood Improvement for Forest Fringe Dwellers","improvement in livelihoods of forest fringe dwellers",""],[1,"250","1","Capacity Building for Local Management Committees","capacity build- ing of local level management committees",""]],"109":[[1,"244","11","Commercialisation of Permissible Marine Products","Commercialisation of permissible marine products rich in poly unsaturated fatty acids (PUFAs), vitamins and so on.",""],[1,"252","4","Coastal and Marine Conservation Scaling and CRZ Management","Conservation of coastal and marine conserva- tion in India requires to be scaled up and managed under CRZ guidelines.",""],[1,"252","4","Community-Based Coastal Resource Monitoring","Effective management of these habitats needs integration of science with tradi- tional knowledge systems and facilitation of greater involvement of communities/community based organisations in monitoring resource use, status, his- tory and on-going changes.",""],[1,"252","4","Coastal Conservation Information Sharing and Empowerment","This will lead to better information flow within and between target groups to ensure that the communities/resource manag- ers are empowered to play their roles effectively in conservation.",""],[1,"252","6","Long-Term Ecosystem Monitoring and Baseline Data Development","initiat- ing long-term monitoring of ecosystems and to develop valuable baseline information that will be critical in taking informed management decisions.",""],[1,"252","8","Assessment of Resource Exploitation Impacts and Management Practices","Evaluating impacts of resource exploitation (especially fisheries) on the functionality of coastal and marine ecosystems and evaluate efficacy of different management practices.",""],[1,"252","9","Coastal and Marine Biodiversity Monitoring and Digitisation Project","Continuous monitoring of coastal biodiversity and digitisation for sustainable utilisation of marine bio-resources which calls for identification of institutions for implementing a national coordinated project through concerned Ministry for assessing the coastal and marine biodiversity resources so as to plan sustainable use of the same.","concerned Ministry"],[1,"252","11","Marine Bio-resources Commercialization and Development Programme","The potential of marine bio-resources towards commercialisation of PUFAs, vitamins, essential amino acids needs to be popularised and commercialised.",""],[1,"252","11","Marine Drug Development Research Initiative","Drug development from marine bio-resources need to be intensified by studying potential marine organism like sea snakes.",""],[1,"252","11","Marine Livelihood Diversification Programme","There is significant potential for offering additional and alternative livelihood options by promoting marine cage cultures, marine ornamental fish culture such as clown and damsel, culture of algae and seaweeds towards organic fertilisers and growth promoters, micro-algae towards biofuels and so on.",""],[1,"259","6","Integrated Coastal Zone Management Priority","During the Twelfth Plan, this gap needs to be filled by according priority to the Integrated Coastal Zone Management (ICZM).",""],[1,"259","6","Coastal Construction Regulation Revision","Coastal zone regulations concerning construction activities have recently been modified to take into account the likely prospect of long-term rise in sea-levels.",""],[1,"259","6","Climate Change Impact Assessment Integration","Climate Change Impact Assessment needs to be integrated into the existing practice of cumulative impact assessment of the environment.",""],[1,"259","6","Coastal Process Modelling for Adaptation Planning","Comprehensive modelling of the coastal processes incorporating all necessary parameters is essential for planning mitigation and adaptation strategies.",""],[1,"259","6","Strengthening of MoES and ISRO Remote Sensing Capabilities","To enable this, existing capabilities of MoES and Indian Space Research Organization (ISRO) (Remote Sensing) may also need to be strengthened during the Twelfth Five Year Plan.","MoES | Indian Space Research Organization (ISRO)"],[1,"263","21","Coastal Zone Scientific Assessment and Displacement Planning","Scientific evaluation of potential changes in the coastal zone, estimation of inundation of vulnerable zones, planning for infrastructure and large scale displacement of people in coastal areas.",""]],"110":[[1,"252","12","Institutional Mechanism for Ecosystem Evaluation and Green Compensation Schemes","Successful and efficient ecosystem evaluation depends on development of appropriate institutional mechanism preferably by the Finance Commission, Planning Commission, Centre of Excellence in Environmental Economics and the MoEF.","Finance Commission | Planning Commission | Centre of Excellence in Environmental Economics | MoEF"],[1,"253","1","Stakeholder Negotiation Mechanism for Green Bonus","organisation against green bonus should be based on negotiations between stakeholders.",""],[1,"253","2","Environmental Performance Incentive for States and Union Territories","The Planning Commission is in the process of developing an EPI to incentivise states for environ- mental performance through budgetary allocations.",""]],"111":[[1,"257","1","Greenhouse Gas Emissions Intensity Reduction Target","Furthermore, in December 2009, India announced that it would aim to reduce the emissions intensity of its GDP by 20-25 per cent over the 2005 levels by 2020.",""],[1,"257","4","Sectoral Climate Vulnerability Assessment and Adaptation Measures","It ensures that plans of all sectors contain an assessment of their vulnerability to climate change, and specific adaptation measures are identified and implemented over the longer term.",""],[1,"257","5","National Low-Carbon Inclusive Growth Strategy","a nationally agreed strategy will be imple- mented to achieve lower carbon inclusive growth and to realise the domestic goal of reduction in the emissions intensity of our GDP.",""],[1,"257","5","Climate Change Assessment and GHG Monitoring Initiative","the Twelfth Plan will take scien- tific and institutional initiatives for regular climate change assessments, GHG measurement, capacity building for technical analysis, monitoring and man- agement of such complex systems at various levels.",""],[1,"260","8","Eight National Missions under the Eleventh Plan","Eight national missions were launched in the Eleventh Plan covering the areas of solar energy, energy efficiency, habitat, agriculture, water, Himalayan ecosystems, forestry and strategic knowl- edge.",""],[1,"262","2","Strategic Knowledge Mission for Climate Change Research","The Strategic Knowledge Mission intends to identify the challenges of, and the responses to, climate change through research and technology development and ensure funding of high quality and focused research into various aspects of climate change.",""],[1,"262","3","Dedicated Mission Implementation Machinery","For a Mission to succeed it must have separable objectives, dedicated implementation machinery and adequate funding.",""],[1,"262","3","Cross-Sectoral Policy Thrust Areas under NAPCC","For objectives which lie within the domain of other flagship programmes, or are completely cross-sectoral, it is better to identify a few policy thrust areas, which would still be part of the NAPCC and be regularly monitored by the Prime Minister's Council.",""],[1,"263","16","Identification of Climate-Vulnerable Areas and Communities","It will identify vulnerable areas and communities that need to be insulated against the adverse effects of climate change.",""],[1,"263","23","State Action Plans for Climate Change","The State Action Plans will include a strategy and a list of possible sectoral actions that would help the States achieve their adaptation and mitigation objectives.",""],[1,"263","23","Central Government Template for State Action Plans","Most of the States have already started working on a template provided by the Central Government.",""],[1,"263","24","Climate Change Action Programme","an umbrella scheme on Climate Change Action Programme is proposed to be launched during the Twelfth Plan.",""],[1,"264","3","Indian Network for Climate Change Assessment","To provide a systematic basis to the research in the area of climate change, the Ministry has set up an Indian Network for Climate Change Assessment (INCCA) as a network-based scientific programme.","Ministry of Environment and Forests"],[1,"264","4","Climate Change Scientific Assessment and Reporting Mechanism","Considering the importance of scientific assistance to policymaking, we need to create a more systematic and credible institutional arrangement that would enable us to continuously enhance the understanding of the 'science' of climate change.",""],[1,"264","5","Scientific Research Capacity Strengthening Programmes","new research programmes may be launched to strengthen scientific research, assess- ment, planning and management capability particularly in the following areas of Climate Change:",""],[1,"264","6","Climate Change Assessment Studies Programme","A specific programme aimed at Climate Change Assessment Studies (CCAS) and institutional- ising the obligatory and scientific work of the Ministry is urgently called for.","Ministry of Environment and Forests"],[1,"264","7","GHG Inventory Management System Institutionalisation","GHG Inventory Management System (GHG- IMS) needs to be institutionalised","MoEF"],[1,"268","5","Expert Group on Low Carbon Strategies for Inclusive Growth","It has also set up an Expert Group to evolve Low Carbon Strategies for Inclusive Growth, which has made important recommendations for power, industry, transport, buildings and forestry sectors.",""],[1,"268","6","Sectoral Climate Vulnerability Assessment and Adaptation Strategy Integration","regular and periodical assessment of vulnerability in different sectors and preparation of adaptation strategies should be part of sectoral plans of the relevant Ministries.",""],[1,"268","7","Reorganization of the National Action Plan on Climate Change","the NAPCC, like all major flagship programmes, needs to be reorganised in accordance with the updated priorities.",""],[1,"268","7","Dedicated Implementation Machinery for Mission Delivery","for any mission to succeed, it must have separable objectives, dedi- cated implementation machinery and adequate funding.",""],[1,"268","7","Prime Minister's Council Monitoring of Policy Thrust Areas","it is better to identify a short list of 'policy thrust areas', which could be separately listed under the NAPCC, and yet, be regularly monitored by the Prime Minister's Council.",""]],"112":[[1,"260","2","Climate and Health Research Programme","During the Twelfth Plan period, additional priority areas of research need to be taken up in the areas of climate variability, and its effect on diar- rhoeal and viral diseases, heat stress and certain types of cancer such as skin cancer.",""],[1,"260","2","Long-Term Multidisciplinary Climate Impact Studies","Other multi-dis- ciplinary long term studies would also be initiated in partnership with Indian Meteorological Department, Central Pollution Control Board and ISRO.",""],[1,"260","3","Integrated Climate Risk Management Framework for Infrastructure","An integrated climate change risks management framework for infrastructures should include market and policy induced enforce- ments and adaptation strategies.",""],[1,"260","5","Climate Risk Assessment for Infrastructure Projects","During the Twelfth Plan, detailed sectoral, regional and integrated studies need to be commissioned for assessment of risks to Indian infrastructure due to climate change, especially to establish the damage functions and costs.",""],[1,"260","5","Independent Review of Upcoming Infrastructure Projects","Assessments by neutral third parties may be conducted for all such upcoming infrastructure projects.",""],[1,"260","5","Climate Impact Assessment in Environmental Review","Environmental Impacts Assessment of new infrastructure projects should also include impacts of climate change on the project in near, medium and long-terms.",""]],"113":[[1,"270","6","National Innovation System Partnership Building","National Focus-build partnership with identi- fied players of the National Innovation System to build the scientific, technological and human resource niches for the country;",""],[1,"270","14","State Technology Partnership Development","Build technology partnerships with States through new models of technological solutions, design, development and delivery;",""],[1,"271","9","Participation in Global Mega Science Projects","Partner with high-value global mega projects in the areas of contemporary scientific interest and technological relevance and enhance India's role in global mega projects such as India-based Neutrino Observatory, Thirty Meter Telescope, Square Kilometer Array, Next Generation Synchrotron and so on;",""],[1,"271","10","International University and Agency Collaboration for Scientific Cooperation","Enhance collaboration with reputed foreign uni- versities/agencies towards addressing the scientific aspects of common interest and global in nature.",""],[1,"271","12","University-Industry and Research Linkage Strengthening","Emphasis should also be given on strengthening linkages between univer- sities, R&D institutions, science academies and industry.",""],[1,"272","7","R&D Programme Implementation Strategy for Social and Public Goods","A strategy needs to be evolved for implementation of R&D programmes focused on social and public goods for: (i) connecting competencies and research resources for scaling and impact; (ii) mounting Grand Challenge programmes on topics of national interest; (iii) adopting different funding strategies for basic and translational research under Extra Mural Research models; (iv) strengthening Intra Mural Research mechanisms for public and social goods in agencies like Council of Scientific and Industrial Research (CSIR); (v) forging State- Centre technology partnerships and technology coalitions among R&D agencies and (vi) promoting PPPs for public and social good by developing new models.",""],[1,"273","1","Science-Socioeconomic Ministry R&D Synergy Mechanism","For deployment of readily available technologies in States, the following need to be evolved: (i) a synergy among the S&T and socio-economic sector, (ii) a policy decision by socio-economic Ministries to allocate a certain minimum percentage (say 1-2 per cent) of their overall budget for supporting R&D, (iii) setting up of joint centres by the socio-economic Ministries in R&D institutions and universities, (iv) participation of socio-economic Ministries in PPP projects supported by the science sector and (v) involving enterprises for effective implementation of R&D solutions arising out of synergies among science sector and socio-economic Ministries.","socio-economic Ministries"],[1,"273","2","Special Competitive Fund for State Technology Absorption","Establishment of special competitive fund for States for absorption of indigenous technologies could form one of the strategies for creating demand pull for technologies in the States.",""],[1,"273","2","State-Centre Technology Coordination Mechanisms","Special mechanisms need to be developed to promote the technology relationships between the Centre and the States.",""],[1,"273","2","Link State Science Councils with National R&D Organisations","Emphasis should be given for connecting the State Councils for S&T to R&D organisations like CSIR, Indian Council of Medical Research (ICMR) and Defence Research and Development Organisation (DRDO) and so on.",""],[1,"275","2","Public-Private Partnership for Climate Change R&D","PPP for R&D for adaptation and mitigation of climate change will be another tool to be used.",""],[1,"275","5","Special Task Force for Programme Synergies","a special task force needs to be created.",""],[1,"275","5","Departmental PAN-India Mission Fund","A separate PAN-India Mission Fund needs to be built-in in every department so that this fund could be deployed for building synergies among the programmes proposed by various departments and address gap areas.",""],[1,"275","5","PAN-India Mission Mode Projects","PAN-India mission mode projects addressing national needs and priorities may be launched through extensive participation of stakeholders to achieve the goals and targets in a defined time frame.",""],[1,"276","2","Mega Science Project Coordination and Funding Sharing Mechanism","These mega science projects would be coordinated by DAE and DST through appropriate funding sharing mechanism.",""],[1,"276","4","National Biodesign Alliance","Box 8.3 provides a novel path to launch National Biodesign alliance through collaborative technology innovation for leveraging international collaboration, thereby strengthening national programmes.",""],[1,"277","1","Research Competency Linkage Initiative","These include: (ii) interconnecting competencies",""],[1,"277","1","Research Resource Synergy Development","These include: (iii) synergy development of research resources",""],[1,"277","1","International Research Partnership Programme","These include: (v) engaging in rewarding and mutual international partnerships",""],[1,"277","1","PPP for Research and Development","These include: (vii) deploying more effec- tively the tool of PPP for R&D",""],[1,"278","3","Connecting Competencies and Research Resources Initiative","One of the strategies evolved for implementation of Twelfth Plan proposals of the Indian science sector is connecting competencies and research resources.",""],[1,"280","5","International Science and Technology Cooperation Schemes for Universities","New schemes to promote the international S&T cooperation for the performing universities are proposed during the Twelfth Plan.",""],[1,"281","6","Public-Private Partnership and Centre-State Technology Cooperation Mechanisms","New mechanisms have been proposed for PPPs and Centre-State Technology partnerships.",""],[1,"281","6","Bilateral and Multilateral Science and Technology Cooperation","Established mechanisms are proposed to be employed for bilateral and multi-lateral S&T cooperation.",""],[1,"281","6","Virtual Centres and North East Technology Application Schemes","Contributions to PAN-India missions, like Joint (Virtual) centres and North East Centre for Technology Applications and Reach form new schemes.",""],[1,"281","6","Science and Technology Social Contract Programme","Social contract of S&T has remained an important programme of the department.",""],[1,"281","6","Council for Science and Technology for Rural India","DST has recently constituted a Council for Science and Technology for Rural India (CSTRI) providing new mechanisms for delivering technologies to the rural India.","DST"],[1,"282","4","Alliance and Partnership Expansion for R&D Outreach","Alliances and partnerships for larger outreach of R&D outputs of VP are proposed.",""],[1,"283","11","Interdepartmental and Institutional Bioeconomy and Social Impact Centres","Interdepartmental and institutional centres and extramural centres of DBT institutions supported with a novel contractual career path for faculty and scalability to connect existing competencies will improve interdisciplinary science, using inspired and",""],[1,"286","2","International Research and Technology Partnership Programme","The experience with existing global partnerships with countries and international agencies will be leveraged to bring about directional change in partnership strategies.",""],[1,"286","5","Sectoral Strategy Redesign for Multi-Mechanism Innovation","During the Twelfth Plan, it is proposed to redesign sectoral strategy in such a way that every sector utilises more than one mechanism or modality, linkages, partnerships and alliances and platforms that are required for successful development of both S&T.",""],[1,"292","6","Consultancy Promotion Initiative","The important activities envisaged during the Twelfth Five Year Plan period would include Consultancy Promotion, Services, Research and Analysis, National Programme for Competency Development in strategic focus areas, Technology Delivery Transfer and Commercialisation, National Knowledge Depository, Training and Development, Export promotion and International Collaborations.",""],[1,"292","6","Research and Analysis Services","The important activities envisaged during the Twelfth Five Year Plan period would include Consultancy Promotion, Services, Research and Analysis, National Programme for Competency Development in strategic focus areas, Technology Delivery Transfer and Commercialisation, National Knowledge Depository, Training and Development, Export promotion and International Collaborations.",""],[1,"292","6","International Collaboration Initiative","The important activities envisaged during the Twelfth Five Year Plan period would include Consultancy Promotion, Services, Research and Analysis, National Programme for Competency Development in strategic focus areas, Technology Delivery Transfer and Commercialisation, National Knowledge Depository, Training and Development, Export promotion and International Collaborations.",""],[1,"297","2","CSIR Initiative on Inclusive, Participative and Collaborative R&D","CSIR Initiative on Inclusive, Participative and Collaborative R&D: This new initiative for CSIR during the Twelfth Plan would comprise the following four sub-components: Grand Challenge Initiative, Inverted Innovation, Participative Science and Participatory Technology Development, and Centres for Collaborative Research.",""],[1,"297","6","Centres for Collaborative Research","Centres for Collaborative Research-CSIR- Academia, CSIR-R&D Institutes and CSIR- Industry: The centres would focus on collaborative R&D in the identified domains through desired networking.",""],[1,"302","5","Collaborative Research and Centres of Excellence Initiative","The DAE programmes also support collaborative research, establishment of centres of excellence as a part of efforts to establish linkages with academia.","DAE"]],"114":[[1,"271","5","Creation of New Trans-Disciplinary R&D Institutions","Create new R&D institutions in trans-disciplin- ary science and engineering to achieve leadership positions;",""],[1,"271","6","Peta-Scale Supercomputing and High-Performance Computing Facilities","Create Peta-scale supercomputing facilities and provide high-performance computing for various applications such as climate modelling, weather prediction, aerospace engineering, computational biology, nuclear applications, earthquake simula- tions, animation in movies, national security and finance;",""],[1,"276","3","Indigenous Peta-Scale Supercomputer Capacity Development","It is also time to embark upon indigenous efforts to build Peta-scale super- computer capacities and capabilities for the coun- try's requirements that will place India among the top five supercomputing power in the world.",""],[1,"281","7","National Supercomputing Capability and Capacity Programme","A national programme on creating",""],[1,"282","1","Supercomputing Capability Expansion to Peta Scale","supercomputing capabilities and scaling capacities to Peta scale is being envisaged for which DST has been assigned the coordinating responsibility.","DST"],[1,"291","3","High Performance Computing System Augmentation","it is proposed to augment computing power from existing 124 Terra-flops to 2.5 Peta-flop during the Twelfth Five Year Plan.",""]],"115":[[1,"274","2","Genetically Modified Crop Regulatory Oversight","The regulatory aspects for genetically modified (GM)-related crops will also be given due emphasis.",""],[1,"274","2","Biotechnology Regulatory Authority of India Bill","Biotechnology Regulatory Authority of India (BRAI) Bill is considered as essential for streamlining regula- tion of all modern biotechnology products.",""],[1,"285","2","Regulatory Science and Infrastructure Strengthening","Involves establishment of BRAI; central agency for regulatory testing and certification laboratories with some core activities and network of testing facilities in public sector laboratories; promotion of regulatory science research units; and human resource development.",""]],"116":[[1,"274","3","Water-Related Technology R&D and Demonstration Programme","R&D for development of technologies for man- aging water-related challenges is being undertaken by almost all the six science departments in associa- tion with the line departments of Central and State Governments.","DoS | MoES | DAE | DST"],[1,"290","5","Desalination Plants for Lakshadweep Islands","Desalination plants would be established in all major islands of Lakshadweep.",""],[1,"293","5","Water Disinfection and Purification Technology Development","CSIR laboratories have developed significant knowledge base on water and water-related technologies.",""]],"117":[[1,"274","4","National Solar Energy Mission Technology R&D","For achieving the full objectives of the National Solar Energy Mission, technology breakthroughs are required to increase the conversion efficiencies and to lower the costs of delivered power, for which it would be necessary to engage mainstream scientists drawn from the entire S&T sector of the country with expertise in relevant areas.",""],[1,"274","4","Clean Energy Research and Development Programme","R&D for clean energy systems is of paramount importance.",""],[1,"274","4","Science Department Energy R&D Support","On energy R&D, almost all six science departments are engaged in either performing research or supporting R&D or both.",""],[1,"274","4","Advanced Clean Energy Technology Research","Similarly, clean coal technology, fuel cells, hydrogen energy, materials for harvesting both light and heat, new inorganic chemistry for converting coal into liquid fuels, bio-inspired",""],[1,"275","1","Renewable Energy R&D Collaboration and Assessment","The MoES is engaged in the assessment of wind, wave and tidal energy potential as a part of tapping renewal energy, including gas hydrate exploration.","MoES"],[1,"275","1","R&D Synergy with Energy Efficiency and Concerned Departments","Since energy sector works in regulated environments, it is necessary for the R&D sector to develop adequate synergies with Bureau of Energy Efficiency and the concerned departments.","Bureau of Energy Efficiency"],[1,"275","1","Technology Collaboration for R&D Systems","Thus, collaboration and cooperation in areas of technology leads where synergies could benefit the R&D systems need to be promoted, including that from the defence research system during the Twelfth Plan period.",""],[1,"292","7","Dye Sensitized Solar Cell Manufacturing Capability Development","During the Twelfth Five Year Plan period, leveraging on its technology prowess, CEL plans to develop capabilities for the manufacture of Dye Sensitized Solar Cells (DSSCs or Grätzel cells), which",""],[1,"295","1","Micro-Inverter Solar System Design and Manufacturing Development","CEL has proposed to develop the design of systems for a relatively new approach for optimising solar system efficiency and improving reliability with the design and manufacture of micro-inverters that connect to individual solar panels.",""],[1,"295","1","Research and Development Division Establishment","CEL will establish an R&D Division to cater to the needs for design, development, testing and validation of a range of improved strategic electronic, special purpose vehicle (SPV), surveillance, safety/security products.",""],[1,"295","1","Special Purpose Vehicle Manufacturing Capacity Expansion","steps are being taken up by CEL to ensure that the present plan capacity of 10 MW for SPV products is increased to 80 MW.",""],[1,"295","1","National Silicon Wafer Production Facility","CEL has also proposed through a joint venture to set up a National Silicon Wafer production facility for producing silicon wafers of 1,000 MW/year capacity to reduce the nations' reliance on availability of this critical resource of silicon materials through import from other countries.",""]],"118":[[1,"275","2","Climate Change Research Missions Coordination","Accordingly, under the National Action Plan for Climate Change, DST shall coordinate two missions under which formation of knowledge networks and thematic centres has been proposed by DST.","DST"],[1,"275","2","Environment-Related Technology Planning and R&D Synergy","R&D sector may need to develop technology plans for solving the environment-related challenges of such sectors in association with the relevant line Ministry, and accordingly efforts of all the players need to be significantly synergised in the area of R&D on environment during the Twelfth Plan period.","relevant line Ministry"],[1,"275","2","Coastal Waters Health Monitoring","The MoES has been monitoring the health of coastal waters of India which would be of immense importance to UN endeavour on global assessment of marine environment.","MoES"],[1,"281","5","Climate Change Programmes","Climate change programmes, modernisa- tion of Survey of India (SoI) and National Atlas and Thematic Organization (NATMO), and district-level technology interventions for increases in per capita incomes are new objectives of the Twelfth Plan pro- grammes.",""],[1,"281","5","Technology Solutions for Agriculture, Chemicals, Water, Energy, Environment, and Health Care","Technology solutions for agricultural, chemicals, water, energy requirement, environmental sustain- ability and affordable human health care would form areas of thrust.",""],[1,"287","7","Earth System Science Research and Services Programme","The programs of the Ministry has been reinforced and restructured with a view to provide best possible services relating to earth system science towards socio-economic benefit of the Indian sub-continent and in the Indian Ocean region.","MoES/Earth System Science Organisation (ESSO)"],[1,"287","9","Atmospheric Observation Systems Network Modernisation","Atmospheric Observation Systems Network: The modernisation plan aims at commissioning of",""],[1,"288","2","National Monsoon Mission","A programme on 'National Monsoon Mission' was launched which will be equipped with the state-of- the-art infrastructure, namely, high-end computers, radars and scientific manpower to generate more detailed and accurate forecasts.",""],[1,"289","3","Atmospheric Observing Systems Modernisation Phase II","It is proposed to undertake phase II of the modernisation, focusing on the augmentation of the existing infrastructure established during the phase I of the modernisation in terms of observing systems and integrating the same with the rest of the network, namely, ground-based radiometers providing temperature and humidity profiles and complementing the sonde observations to be developed with priority.",""],[1,"289","3","Centre for Atmospheric Technology Establishment","A Centre for Atmospheric Technology (CAT) is also planned to coordinate development of instruments, calibrate instruments including satellite-based and provide overall technology support to atmospheric sciences, besides validation of satellite data.",""],[1,"289","3","Himalayan Regional Forecasting System","It is proposed to set up a dedicated forecasting system for the entire Himalayan region with a much focused objective of integrating and improving the weather related services.",""],[1,"289","4","Atmospheric Processes and Modelling Service","The sole purpose of the programme is to develop a wide range of atmospheric models for providing weather and climate forecasting services to various sectors by integrating all the process studies and models.",""],[1,"289","4","Expansion of Agromet Advisory Services to Block and Village Levels","The existing district-level Agromet Advisory Services (AAS) to deliver crop and location-specific AAS to farmers will be graduated to the block level with village-level advisory.",""],[1,"289","5","Metropolitan Air Quality and Weather Services","Metropolitan air quality and weather service pro- viding real-time weather, as well as now casting of weather and air quality in all metro cities as well, are proposed.",""],[1,"289","5","National Monsoon Mission","National Monsoon Mission will be set up with the state-of-the-art weather infrastructures, namely, high-end computers, radars and scientific manpower to generate more detailed and accurate forecasts.",""],[1,"289","5","Cloud Physics and Severe Weather Warning System","Other deliverables are Cloud Physics and severe weather warning system.",""],[1,"289","6","Climate Change Research Programme","It is proposed to develop long-term (multi-decadal) simulations of monsoon using coupled ocean-atmospheric models upon the commissioning of the HPC system upgrade for climate change research.",""],[1,"289","6","Seasonal and Intra-seasonal Monsoon Prediction Development","The development of seasonal and intra-seasonal prediction of monsoon through coupled model is to be taken up.",""],[1,"289","6","Geo-engineering Scheme Assessment","The utility of geo-engineering schemes to mitigate global warming has to be explored.",""],[1,"289","6","Climate Change Research Capacity Development","There is need to develop expertise in India to evaluate the benefits and risks of these schemes.",""],[1,"289","6","Changing Water Cycle Research Projects","The research projects would be taken up to enhance our understanding of the changing water cycle.",""],[1,"289","6","Paleoclimatic Studies Programme","Besides, paleoclimatic studies will be conducted to understand the past variations of climate for possible projections of climate scenarios.",""],[1,"290","1","Airborne Atmospheric Observation Programme","Airborne Platforms: A wealth of atmospheric, aerosol and cloud microphysics data will be generated using airborne platforms which will be useful to validate the convection and cloud schemes, and for improving the model physics.",""],[1,"290","2","Ocean Observation System","Ocean Observation System (OOS): The objective is to acquire time-series data from the seas around India and to develop a wide range of ocean atmospheric models towards augmentation of services.",""],[1,"290","3","Ocean Science and Services Programme","The OSS have been reoriented into a major programme during the Twelfth plan by integrating all the service-oriented ocean-related projects under one umbrella.",""],[1,"290","7","Polar Science and Cryosphere Programme","The Polar Science and Cryosphere programme entails the study of the Antarctic, Arctic and Glaciers of Himalayas that are important to understand the climate change and climate variability in the Indian region.",""],[1,"291","2","Integrated Ocean Drilling Programme for Deep Sea Drilling","Deep sea drilling in the Arabian Sea basin through the Integrated Ocean Drilling Programme is proposed to be undertaken.",""],[1,"291","2","Institute for Geo Technologies","An institute for Geo Technologies, integrating all the scientific and operational bodies and taking new initiatives on merit like finding geo technology solutions to serious problems like global warming is proposed to be established.",""],[1,"291","2","Advanced Isotope Geochemistry and Geochronology Research","For advanced research in isotope geochemistry and geochronology pertaining to earth, atmospheric and oceanic sciences, high-resolution Secondary Ionization Mass Spectrometry studies would be carried out.",""],[1,"300","10","EO Systems and Atmospheric Science Programme","EO Systems and Atmospheric Science Programme: The thrust areas of EO for the Twelfth Five Year Plan have been identified based on exten- sive interactions with users under the aegis of National Natural Resources Management System (NNRMS) as well as after detailed deliberations in the inter-centre committee of ISRO.",""]],"119":[[1,"281","5","Modernisation of Survey of India and NATMO","Climate change programmes, modernisa- tion of Survey of India (SoI) and National Atlas and Thematic Organization (NATMO), and district-level technology interventions for increases in per capita incomes are new objectives of the Twelfth Plan pro- grammes.",""],[1,"282","3","Strengthening of R&D Support and Knowledge Service Organisations","DST would continue to support the governance of Technology Development Board (TDB), Technology Information, Forecasting and Assessment Council (TIFAC), Vigyan Prasar (VP), National Accreditation Board for Testing Laboratories (NABL), Good Laboratory Practice (GLP), SoI and NATMO and National Spatial Data Infrastructure.","DST"],[1,"282","4","Modernisation of Survey of India Administrative Governance Systems","It is proposed to modernise SoI and new administrative governance systems for SoI and NATMO is proposed to be introduced.",""],[1,"282","5","National Geographical Information System","it is proposed to establish a robust information and decision support system as envisaged through setting up an Indian Geographical Organisation (INGO) under the programme NGIS.",""]],"120":[[1,"282","7","Biotechnology Research, Innovation and Development Acceleration Strategy","The overall strategy for DBT for the sector during the Twelfth Plan is to 'accelerate the pace of research, innovation and development to advance biotechnology as strategic area by taking India's strengths in foundational sciences to globally com- petitive levels and expanding the application of",""],[1,"283","4","Prototype Commercialisation Business Model Development","Phase II of this programme has been initiated for refinement, validation, testing developing business model for commercialisation of developed prototypes/technologies.",""],[1,"283","5","National Biodesign Alliance","During the Twelfth Plan, a programme \"National Biodesign Alliance\" has been established with various partners such as Regional Centre for Biotechnology, Gurgaon; Translational Health Science and Technology Institute, Gurgaon; International Centre for Genetic Engineering & Biotechnology, Delhi; IIT, Delhi; AIIMS, Delhi; and Christian Medical College, Vellore.",""],[1,"283","5","Centre for Biodesign and In Vitro Diagnostics","A Centre for Biodesign and In vitro Diagnostics has been established at Translational Health Science and Technology Institute, NCR region.",""],[1,"283","5","Biodesign Expansion to Additional Institutions","Efforts are being made to expand the concept of biodesign at other IITs, medical schools and other related institutions.",""],[1,"284","8","Translational Research Connectivity Initiative","Some such connectivi- ties proposed are: Biosciences with chemical sci- ences and synthetic biology for next-generation biofuels; Nano science; chemical sciences and pharmaceutical sciences with clinical research for novel drug delivery, novel diagnostic and",""],[1,"285","3","Expansion of Autonomous R&D Institutions","The expansion aims at expanding current strengths of researchers and scientists by three-folds at all levels through on-site expansion or establishment of second research campus; setting up of Extra Mural Research centres on or off site to promote translational science, launch mission programmes or to advance interdisciplinary science area and expanding physical infrastructure including technology platforms.",""],[1,"285","5","Expansion and Commissioning of Bio Clusters","Expansion and commissioning of bio clusters at Faridabad, Mohali, Kalyani and Hyderabad: This would involve adding new programme-based centres at each cluster: academic centres, medi- cal centres, bioengineering centre, contract labs, Genetically Modified Products (GMP) units, animal model resources, novel platforms for therapeutics for sharing by SMEs, technology incubators and parks of entrepreneurship train- ing centres and offices for technology transfer and management and to provide connectivity for innovation.",""],[1,"285","7","Grand Challenge Programme Reorientation","Reorient 'Grand Challenge Programme' scheme of the Eleventh Plan to address national priorities in various developmental sectors through bot- tom-up approach and also encourage discovery- led innovative ideas:",""],[1,"286","4","Welcome Trust Launch","Welcome Trust will be launched.",""],[1,"286","8","Centre for Biotechnology Communication","it is proposed to set up Centre for Biotechnology Communication for content creation and coordination; communication units in universities and institutions; commissioning regular programmes",""]],"121":[[1,"285","1","Bioscience Connectivity and Technology Innovation Support","This would receive high priority and use the instruments defined above for connectivity and for conversion of early leads to meaningful solutions and products.",""],[1,"286","3","Public-Private Partnership Support and Innovative Biotechnology Funding Schemes","Continued and sustained support to PPPs with new innovative funding schemes: Besides continuing with some reforms in SBIRI and BIPP schemes operations innovative funding schemes such as: Ignition Grant Scheme available to individuals or a team of individuals-in partnership with private investment agencies; schemes for creating and nurturing start-up for early-stage technologies; provision of 'bridge funding' firms to function between successive private equity funding or planning for IPOs; funding for technology access and acquisition and licensing and special investment incentives to industry for building more biotechnology/pharma special economic zones (SEZs).",""],[1,"286","6","New-Generation Biotechnology Industry Development Support","Innovative funding schemes and incentives within the framework of existing mechanisms shall be extended to develop capacity for setting up of new bio industries such as bulk/specialty chemicals/biochemicals; food and nutrition technologies; biotech-led/biotech-enabled ser- vices engineering, components and equipment manufacture; nano-bio industries and so on.",""],[1,"286","6","Biotechnology Product and Industry Reengineering Initiative","Efforts would also be made for reengineering the economic model for biotechnology product/ industry development.",""],[1,"286","7","Technology Acquisition, Transfer and Licensing Support","Major initiatives will be taken in Twelfth Plan such as establishment of Intelligence Innovation and Idea units to serve as 'think tanks' in life sciences and biotechnol- ogy to imagine the future and prepare for the future to analyse needs and opportunities and create product profile for products that will be usable and marketable; technology acquisition fund with legal process and mechanism tech- nology and IP management centres, particularly DBT partner universities and institutions.",""]],"122":[[1,"273","1","State Technology Deployment and Start-up Promotion","Focus should also be on creating start-ups and utilising the cutting-edge knowledge base.",""],[1,"281","5","District-Level Technology Interventions for Income Growth","Climate change programmes, modernisa- tion of Survey of India (SoI) and National Atlas and Thematic Organization (NATMO), and district-level technology interventions for increases in per capita incomes are new objectives of the Twelfth Plan pro- grammes.",""],[1,"281","5","Technology Deployment Promotion Initiative","Promoting technology deployment will receive as much focus as technology development.",""],[1,"281","6","Special Technology Schemes for Vulnerable Groups","Special schemes for vulnerable sections of the society will be taken through CSTRI.",""],[1,"292","2","Socio-Economic Transformation through S&T Intervention","The Twelfth Five Year Plan of CSIR focuses on achieving science and engineering leadership; developing innovative technological solutions; practising open innovation initiatives; developing and nur- turing human resource in trans-disciplinary areas; facilitating science-based entrepreneurship; and enabling socio-economic transformation through appropriate S&T intervention.",""],[1,"292","5","Technological Empowerment of Women Projects","support shall be provided for Technological Empowerment of Women projects, including projects spilling over from the Eleventh Five Year Plan;",""],[1,"294","4","CSIR 800 Affordable R&D Products and Processes Programme","CSIR has launched an ambitious, socially relevant programme named CSIR 800.",""],[1,"295","7","CSIR Outreach Centres","CSIR during the Twelfth Five Year Plan envisages setting up CSIR Outreach Centres that would essentially function in partnership with stakeholders.",""],[1,"296","7","CSIR 800 Programme Expansion","The programme on CSIR 800 that was launched during the Eleventh Five Year Plan for improving the quality of life and augmenting live- lihood for the people at the base of the economic pyramid is being expanded during the Twelfth Five Year Plan.",""],[1,"296","7","Technology Enabled Villages Rollout","As a part of the programme, CSIR would address the needs of rural communities also through implementation of 24 identified CSIR Technology Enabled Villages (TECHVILS) across the coun- try.",""],[1,"297","4","CSIR Initiative for Inverted Innovation","The CSIR Initiative for Inverted Innovation-a unique paradigm where children/young engineers invent, CSIR laboratories mentor and industries commercialise;",""],[1,"297","5","CSIR Participative Science and Participatory Technology Development Initiative","CSIR Initiative on Participative Science and Participatory Technology Development-an ini- tiative to pursue R&D that would provide mutual benefits to all the stakeholders participating in the scheme; inclusive innovation can be achieved, translational research can be carried out, a fluid team with like-minded people can be involved and the scientific outcome can be effectively leveraged.",""]],"123":[[1,"295","4","Establishment of Five New CSIR Institutes","CSIR envisages setting up five new institutes during the Twelfth Five Year Plan, in both physical and virtual mode.",""],[1,"295","6","National Laboratories Cluster-Based R&D Programme","The Twelfth Plan envisages strengthening and streamlining the cluster approach substantially.",""],[1,"296","2","R&D Infrastructure Creation and Refurbishment","R&D Infrastructure Creation and Refurbish- ment: Increase in the number of research pro- grammes and the number of scientist calls for a corresponding increase in R&D infrastructure.",""],[1,"296","3","Energy Efficient Green Campus Development","Energy Efficient Green Campus Development: During the Twelfth Plan, it is proposed to continue with this initiative so as to spruce up CSIR laboratories to substantially high standards such as green building.",""],[1,"296","3","Civil Infrastructure Expansion for Staff and Student Facilities","The building of civil infrastructure would also cover increasing the number of staff quarters, student hostels, guest houses and other fringe facilities.",""],[1,"296","3","Renovation and Improvement of Existing Residential Facilities","Initiatives would be undertaken to renovate and improve the existing staff quarters, hostels, guest houses and so on.",""]],"124":[[1,"296","1","CSIR Special Eco-Region Sustainable Development Initiative","CSIR during the Twelfth Plan would focus on special eco-regions of the country and facilitate their sustainable development through S&T intervention.",""],[1,"296","1","CSIR Technology Promotion for North-Eastern States","CSIR's efforts would include promoting innovation and CSIR technologies for the north-eastern States",""],[1,"296","1","S&T Intervention for Disaster Mitigation and Coral Reef Sustainability","undertake S&T intervention towards disaster mitigation and sustainable development of the coral reefs in the Lakshadweep, Andaman and Nicobar Islands.",""]],"125":[[1,"294","6","Traditional Knowledge Digital Library Access Agreements","CSIR's Traditional Knowledge Digital Library (TKDL) in collaboration with Department of Ayurveda, Yoga & Naturopathy, Unani, Siddha and Homoeopathy (AYUSH) has emerged as a unique resource for protecting Indian traditional knowledge from exploitation through IP filings.","Department of Ayurveda, Yoga & Naturopathy, Unani, Siddha and Homoeopathy (AYUSH)"]],"126":[[1,"298","2","Space Programme Expansion and Mission Development","The broad directions for the space programme for the next decade would include: (i) Strengthening/Expanding of operational services in communications and navi- gation; (ii) developing enhanced imaging capabil- ity for natural resource management, weather and climate change studies; (iii) space science missions for better understanding of the solar system and the universe; (iv) planetary exploratory missions; (v) development of heavy lift launcher, reus- able launch vehicles and (vi) the human space flight programme.",""],[1,"298","3","Communications and Earth Observation Missions Programme","communications and earth observations (EOs) will be pursued to achieve faster delivery of information to remote areas and finer observations of the earth.",""],[1,"298","5","Satellite Communications Capacity Augmentation Programme","it is proposed to augment the Indian National Satellite System (INSAT) capacity to bridge the gap between the demand and supply of the transponders for meeting all the requirements of the country and also to maintain sufficient spares capacity to meet contingencies.",""],[1,"298","5","Satellite Technology Development and Applications Programme","Development of state-of-the-art technologies and latest applications areas shall also be",""],[1,"299","7","Satellite Communications Capacity Expansion Programme","Based on the demand, about 400 transponders are planned to be realised by end of the Twelfth Plan period.",""],[1,"299","7","Communication Satellite Development Programme","Towards this, 14 communication satellites are planned to (i) increase the transponder capac- ity, (ii) introduce new-generation broadband very small aperture terminal (VSAT) systems, (iii) intro- duce Ka-band systems, (iv) build high-power S-band satellite mobile communications and (v) introduce new-generation geo-imaging satellite.",""],[1,"299","9","Communication Satellite Bus Development and Launch Programme","I-3K and I-4K buses are planned to be launched using procured foreign launcher.",""],[1,"299","9","High-Throughput I-6K-12KW Bus Development Initiative","It is also planned to initiate development of High throughput I-6K-12KW bus in higher fre- quency bands like Ka/Ku and the technologies asso- ciated with it.",""],[1,"299","10","Satellite Spectrum Securing Initiative","Maintaining and securing sufficient orbit- spectrum resources for country's Satcom activities will be a thrust area of the Twelfth Plan.",""],[1,"300","10","EO Systems and Atmospheric Science Programme","EO Systems and Atmospheric Science Programme: The thrust areas of EO for the Twelfth Five Year Plan have been identified based on exten- sive interactions with users under the aegis of National Natural Resources Management System (NNRMS) as well as after detailed deliberations in the inter-centre committee of ISRO.",""],[1,"301","1","Continuity of Remote Sensing-Based Services","the focus will be to ensure continuity of services in the areas of Natural Resources Census (1:50000 and 1:250000 scale), groundwater potential mapping, snow and glacier studies, coastal zone management, PFZ, Ocean State forecasting, weather forecast, Space-based Information Support for Decentralized Planning (SIS-DP), Accelerated Irrigation Benefit Programme (AIBP), India-Water Resource Information System (India-WRIS), National Urban Information System (NUIS), including the initiative to help user Ministries in the institutionalisation process for remote sensing-based services","MoEF | MoES | Ministry of Agriculture | MoA | Ministry of Water Resources | MoWR"]],"127":[[1,"300","1","GAGAN Satellite Navigation System Development","ISRO is working jointly with Airport Authority of India (AAI) in establishing the GAGAN system.",""],[1,"300","1","Indian Regional Navigational Satellite System Establishment","ISRO is establishing a regional satellite navigation system called Indian Regional Navigational Satellite System (IRNSS).",""],[1,"300","2","Satellite Navigation Programme","The Satellite Navigation Programme (SNP) has the primary objective of establishing a space- based infrastructure, Ground Segment for satellite- based position, navigation and timing services.",""],[1,"300","3","Final Operational Phase for GAGAN SBAS Deployment","Implement the final operational phase for satel- lite-based augmentation system (SBAS) GAGAN over the Indian Airspace jointly with AAI and providing position, navigation and timing services through an integrated receiver.",""],[1,"300","4","Independent IRNSS Implementation and User Segment Expansion","Implement an independent IRNSS over Indian region and encourage the growth of user seg- ment in Indian Market.",""],[1,"300","5","Indigenous GNSS Application and Receiver Development","Develop indigenous expertise in applications of GNSS for critical National applications, identify specific application software development areas and work towards development of receivers for IRNSS including GNSS through participation of Indian industry.",""],[1,"300","6","Orbit-Spectrum Resource Securing for Sat-Nav Programme","Secure sufficient orbit-spectrum resources for country's Sat-Nav Programme activities.",""],[1,"300","7","Indian Satellite Navigation Policy Formulation","There is a need to formulate the Indian Satellite Navigation Policy as ISRO is implementing and going to provide satellite-based navigation services in India.",""],[1,"300","8","IRNSS Satellite-Based Positioning System","IRNSS is an independent and indigenously developed Indian satellite-based positioning system for critical national applications.",""],[1,"300","9","Indian Regional Navigation Satellite System Deployment","The first IRNSS satellite is planned for launch in 2012-13.",""]],"128":[[1,"301","2","Disaster Management Support Programme","The DMS Programme of ISRO is intended to provide near-real-time support and services from imaging and communication satellites towards efficient management of disasters in the country.",""],[1,"301","3","Operationalisation of the National Database for Emergency Management","Operationalisation of National Database for Emergency Management (NDEM)",""],[1,"301","4","Natural Disaster Impact Mapping and Monitoring Enhancement","Continue impact mapping and monitoring of natural disasters with improved turnaround time and with newer capabilities",""],[1,"301","7","Extension of the Communication Network to District Emergency Operation Centres","Extension of the communication network to the District Emergency Operation centres",""]],"129":[[1,"299","2","Corrective Measures for Future GSLV Launches","The corrective steps based on Failure Analysis Committee are being effected for future launches.",""],[1,"301","14","Space Transportation System Development Programme","Twelfth Plan period will be towards achieving self-sufficiency in launching our satellites, developing launch vehicles for enhanced payload capability, adopting appropriate outsourcing strategies for assuring productionisation of launch vehicles, enhancement of infrastructure for launch vehicles and developing technologies for the future programmes of ISRO.",""],[1,"301","15","PSLV Production Expansion with Industry Participation","Enhanced level of production of PSLV systems with vigorous industry participation to meet the projected launch requirements.",""],[1,"301","16","GSLV MKII Development Completion and Operationalization","Complete the development flights and opera- tionalise GSLV MKII with indigenous Cryogenic Upper Stage",""],[1,"301","17","C25 Engine and Stage Development and Qualification","3. Complete development and qualification of C25 Engine and Stage",""],[1,"301","18","GSLV MkIII Development Flights Completion","Complete the development flights of GSLV MkIII with 4.0 T geostationary transfer orbit (GTO) capability",""],[1,"301","20","Launch Vehicle and Advanced Mission Infrastructure Enhancement","Enhancement of infrastructure to meet the launch vehicle requirements and advanced mission requirements.",""],[1,"301","21","Reusable Launch Vehicle and Dual-Mode Ramjet Technology Demonstration","Demonstrate critical technology related to reus- able launch vehicle (RLV) and dual-mode ramjet (DMRJ) through technology demonstration",""],[1,"301","22","Human Space Flight Technology and Subsystems Development","Develop the critical technology and subsystems related to Human Space flight programme",""],[1,"301","23","Launch Vehicle Critical Technology Development and Demonstration","Develop and demonstrate the critical technolo- gies that will make ISRO's launch vehicle more cost-effective and more capable.",""],[1,"301","24","Technology Development for Space Research","Continue the technology development efforts to improve the present capabilities and to contrib- ute for long-term Space Research.",""],[1,"301","25","Satellite Launch Mission Expansion","The mission profile for meeting the satellite launch demand includes 17 PSLV missions, 6 GSLV MK-II missions and 2 GSLV MK-III missions (this also includes one experimental mission).",""],[1,"301","25","Launch Vehicle Production and Test Facility Expansion","This demands increased stage and system production rates, expanding human infrastructure and test facilities and substantial technological achievements in cryogenic stage elements.",""]],"130":[[1,"302","1","Space Science and Planetary Exploration Missions","The work on Chandrayaan-2, Astrosat-1 and Aditya-1, initiated during the Eleventh Plan, is in progress and all these missions will be realised in the Twelfth Plan.",""],[1,"302","1","Mars Mission","the newer mission that is planned during the Twelfth Plan is Mars mission.",""],[1,"302","1","POLIX X-ray Polarisation Mission","In addition, POLIX (to study the X-ray polarisation from bright X-ray-emitting objects) shall also be persued.",""],[1,"302","2","Mars Orbiter Mission","The present plan is to launch a Mars-orbiter using PSLV-XL during the November 2013 launch opportunity.",""]],"131":[[1,"290","4","Ocean Survey and Mineral Resources Programme","This programme is primarily aimed at conducting surveys for harnessing the marine nonliving resources in a sustainable way, available in exclusive economic zone (EEZ) and deep sea region of the Indian Ocean.",""],[1,"290","5","Ocean Technology Programme","The Ocean Technology programme of India encompasses four core missions as Ocean Energy, Deep Sea Mining, Coastal end Environmental Engineering and Marine Instrumentation.",""],[1,"290","5","Marine Living Resources Development in Andaman and Nicobar Islands","National Institute of Ocean Technology plays a key role in undertaking ocean-related activities, Ocean Science & Technology and enhancement of marine living resources, development for breeding, rearing and fattening of lobsters, to begin with, for Andaman and Nicobar Islands.",""],[1,"290","5","Deep Sea Mining Technology Consolidation","Consolidation of deep sea mining technology such as integrated deep sea mining system, soil tester, Remotely Operated Vehicle (ROV) and manned submersible would be carried out, besides developing Marine Sensors and underwater equipment.",""],[1,"290","5","Ocean Technology R&D Programme Set","Under ocean technology, a set of eight in-house R&D programmes like Energy, Ocean Acoustics, Marine Sensor, offshore structures, Inter-institutional R&D of National Institute of Ocean Technology (NIOT) would be carried out.",""],[1,"290","6","Ocean Research Vessel Procurement","Two new vessels are proposed which will be greater than 100m, Ice class, with speed of 20 knots and fitted with winches and systems for exploration of deep sea living resources.",""]],"132":[[1,"290","8","Seismological Research and Earthquake Disaster Mitigation Studies","It is proposed to provide thrust to the earthquake-related stud- ies and to generate inputs for earthquake disaster mitigation.",""],[1,"291","1","National Centre for Seismology Establishment","To address these issues relating to earthquake in a holistic manner, a National Centre for Seismology (NCS) is being set up.",""]],"133":[[1,"314","1","India Inclusive Innovation Fund","NInC is creating an India Inclusive Innovation Fund (IIIF).",""],[1,"314","6","National Innovation Scholarships Stream","Creation of a separate scholarship stream of National Innovation Scholarships analogous to the National Talent Search Scheme.",""],[1,"315","3","School-Level Innovation Fellowships","The Ministry of Human Resource Development has green-lighted the proposals relating to the award of 1,000 Innovation Fellowships at the school level (Classes 9-12);","Ministry of Human Resource Development"]],"134":[[1,"314","7","Innovation Centres in DIETs","Setting up an Innovation Centre in each DIET (District Institute of Education and Training) to enhance teacher training and enable them to become facilitators of creativity and innovative thinking.",""],[1,"314","8","Local History, Ecology, and Cultural Heritage Mapping in High Schools","Mapping of local history, ecology and cultural heritage by each high School in the country to create critical thinking on their local environ- ment by students.",""],[1,"314","10","Meta University Establishment","Setting up a Meta University, as a redefinition of the university model in the 21st century by lever- aging India's National Knowledge Network to enable multidisciplinary learning and collabora- tive knowledge creation.",""],[1,"315","1","Design Innovation Centres","Setting up 20 Design Innovation Centres co- located in Institutes of National Importance.",""],[1,"315","1","Open Design School","Also, setting up an Open Design School;",""],[1,"315","1","Design Trainer Training Institute","creating an institute for facilitating training of trainers in design and introducing design thinking at the school level.",""],[1,"315","2","University Innovation Clusters Development","Identifying and facilitating the development of 20 University Innovation Clusters across the country where innovation would be seeded through CICs, as mentioned earlier.",""],[1,"315","3","Local History, Ecology, and Heritage Mapping in High Schools","introducing the Mapping of Local History, Local Ecology and Local Culture and Heritage by all high schools and setting up the first Meta University of the world for multi-disciplinary learning and collaborative learning.",""],[1,"315","3","Meta University for Multidisciplinary and Collaborative Learning","introducing the Mapping of Local History, Local Ecology and Local Culture and Heritage by all high schools and setting up the first Meta University of the world for multi-disciplinary learning and collaborative learning.",""],[1,"315","3","Repositioning of DIETS under Teacher Training Guidelines","MHRD has also incorporated steps on re-positioning DIETS in the country in the new Guidelines for the Centrally Sponsored Scheme on Teacher's Training.","MHRD"],[1,"315","3","Design Education Concept Note","MHRD is also working on a concept note on design educa- tion in consultation with the Council and Planning Commission.","MHRD"],[1,"316","6","Science Centre Innovation Outreach Enhancement","The NInC is partnering with the National Council of Science Museums (NCSM), National Museum of Natural History (NMNH) and others to enhance the impact of existing Science Centres in the country and use them as channels for innovation outreach.",""],[1,"316","6","Interactive Science Centre Upgrading Initiative","NInC will aim to invigorate the existing Science Centres in the country through more interactive exhibits, while leveraging locally available resources to showcase science in a hands-on manner.",""],[1,"316","6","Regular Science Innovation Showcase Programme","It will also use the Science Centres for showcasing innovations on a regular basis and improving outreach.",""],[1,"316","7","Innovation Spaces Pilot Programme","NIC is currently working on seven pilots of Innovation Spaces at Science Centres/Museums in Ahmedabad, Bangalore, Delhi, Kolkata, Mumbai, Sawai Madhopur and one in the North-East.",""]],"135":[[1,"321","6","Dedicated Social Mobilisation and Social Audit Resources in Flagship Programmes","specific provisions are being made during the Twelfth Plan in each flag- ship programme for dedicated time and human and financial resources for social mobilisation, awareness generation and social audit.",""],[1,"322","2","Civil Society Partnership and Volunteer Mobilization Framework","Government must strongly encourage partnerships with civil society including not only NGOs but also academic institutions, local colleges and universities.",""],[1,"322","6","Institutionalized Consultative Planning Mechanisms","To strengthen governance at the panchayat, block, district, State and Central levels with special focus at the critical level of the district planning board, it is essential to build mechanisms that institutionalise consultative planning to enable greater representation of the stakeholders.",""],[1,"322","6","Institutionalized Consultations with the Voluntary Sector","One way of achieving the same is through institutionalised consultations with the voluntary sector.",""],[1,"323","2","Participatory Representation and Local Institution Capacity Building","strengthening democracy and governance through improved participatory representation. awareness of rights and capacity building of local institutions;",""],[1,"323","7","Institutionalize Joint Consultative Groups and Forums","The Twelfth Plan should institutionalise the Joint Consultative Groups/Forums/Joint Machineries recommended by the National Policy on the Voluntary Sector in all forms of planning, right from the grass-roots levels up to the level of Central Government Ministries.",""],[1,"323","7","Civil Society Participation in Policy and Programme Design","Within the Joint Consultative Groups/Forums/Joint Machineries structure, members of civil society, including the voluntary sector would be made partners in the debates that precede designing and development of policies/schemes/programmes, and also be involved in mid-course corrective measures to create a window for improvements as well as incorporation of regional/cultural specificities.",""],[1,"323","7","Innovation Identification and Scaling Mechanism","Further, mechanisms would be set up to identify and up-scale innovations that have made visible changes in the lives of beneficiary communities.",""],[1,"323","9","National Policy on the Voluntary Sector","national policy on the voluntary sector to enable and empower an independent, creative and effective voluntary sector in each state, which can contribute to the social, cultural and economic advancement of its people.",""],[1,"323","10","Funding Facilitation for Voluntary Organisations","there is a need to facilitate funding to voluntary organisations in order to enable them to mobilise people as agents of social transformation.",""],[1,"323","11","National Voluntary Organisation Data Bank","A national data bank that lists VOs on the basis of the registration (trust/soci- ety/non-profit company), thematic area of expertise (women's empowerment/health/environment and so on) and nature of work (research/implementation/ evaluation/designing and so on) would be developed to make the present system of engagement more rewarding and efficient.",""],[1,"323","12","Voluntary Organization Accreditation and Certification System","Accreditation and certification of voluntary agencies to enhance their credibility, transparency and accountability, and also ensure their capability in performing certain activities will help improve standards.",""],[1,"324","1","Corporate-VO Partnership Affirmative Action for Equity and Diversity","CSR initiatives and Corporate-VO Partnerships should also include affirmative action to ensure equity, reduce ethnic and social conflicts and make public/private spaces more sensitive to diversity and social justice.",""],[1,"324","1","Voluntary Sector Governance Professional Cadre Development","There is also a need to create a cadre of professionals who can deal with governance issues within the voluntary sector.",""],[1,"324","1","Capacity Building for Voluntary Action Participants","Capacity building of people involved in voluntary action is necessary to improve governance at all levels.",""],[1,"331","7","Stakeholder Consultation Forums","it is bet- ter to create effective forums to identify problems, and for joint teams to be formed to tackle them.",""],[1,"331","10","Institutionalized Consensus-Building Processes","Broad based consensus-building processes, therefore, need to be institutionalised to ensure suc- cessful implementation of plans.",""],[1,"331","10","Strengthening Representation Institutions","Institutions for representation, such as employee unions, employer associations, and civil society organisations (CSOs), must become more",""],[1,"332","3","Stakeholder Consensus-Building Mechanism","We need another mechanism specifically designed to bring people with different perspectives together: to listen to each other, to distil the essence of their shared aspiration for the country and the critical principles they will adhere to in the work they have to do together as partners in progress.",""],[1,"333","6","Dissemination and Use of Multi-Stakeholder Collaboration Tools","A 'movement' to disseminate and use such tools and processes will accelerate implementation and growth.",""]],"136":[[1,"318","7","Local Innovation Promotion under NREGA","The National Rural Employment Guarantee Act (NREGA), the largest flagship programme, promotes local innovation by",""],[1,"321","6","MGNREGA Operational Guidelines for High-SC/ST Blocks","The new Operational Guidelines for MGNREGA, for example, provide that those blocks of the country where either sched- uled castes plus scheduled tribes form ≥30 per cent of the population or the annual MGNREGA",""],[1,"322","1","Cluster Facilitation Teams for MGNREGA","expenditure was more than ₹12 crore in any year since the programme started, will mandatorily have at least three Cluster Facilitation Teams (CFT).",""],[1,"322","3","Bharat Rural Livelihoods Foundation","The Bharat Rural Livelihoods Foundation (BRLF) is proposed to be set up to foster and facilitate civil society action in partnership with government for transforming the livelihoods and lives of people in areas such as the Central Indian Adivasi belt.",""],[1,"322","4","BRLF Collaborative Grassroots Project Support","BRLF will assist its civil society partners in ensuring that the design of their proposals incorporates a collaborative mode of functioning with governments/PRIs/banks.",""],[1,"322","5","BRLF Corpus Financing Mechanism","Beginning with an initial amount of ₹200 crore, the Government of India (GOI) will provide a Corpus of ₹500 crore in three tranches over the next three years to BRLF, against achievement of well- defined milestones.","Government of India (GOI)"],[1,"355","1","Untied BRGF Community Gap Financing","The funds provided under BRGF are untied and can be used to meet any development gap identified by the community in its interaction with the Panchayats and Municipalities.",""],[1,"357","1","KBK Districts Special Development Funding","Accordingly, an annual allocation of ₹120 crore is being made under the Districts Component of the BRGF for the eight KBK districts and the remaining allocation of ₹130 crore is being made through the Special Plan for the KBK districts from 2007-08.",""],[1,"357","1","Development Fund Support for KBK Districts","In addition, this region has been recently receiving development funds under Mahatma Gandhi National Rural Employment Guarantee Scheme (MGNREGS), Pradhan Mantri Gram Sadak Yojana (PMGSY), SSA, NRHM and other development programme.",""],[1,"357","1","Enhanced State Funding for KBK Region","With improvement in the fiscal conditions of the State, flow of the State funds to this region has also improved in recent years.",""],[1,"361","6","Resource Earmarking for Border Areas","It is also important that the Stats ensure earmarking of due share of resourc4s from Centrally Sponsored and State Plan Schemes to the Border Areas.",""],[1,"363","11","Restructured BRGF Coverage Expansion","it is proposed to include the 200 most backward districts and 1,500 most backward sub-districts14 under the restructured BRGF.",""],[1,"363","12","Increased BRGF Financial Allocations","We propose to substantially raise the financial allocations to overcome the criticism that BRGF is inadequately funded and lacks the critical mass to make a significant difference on the ground.",""],[1,"367","6","Eastern Nagaland Special Package","special pack- age for the eastern Nagaland",""]],"137":[[1,"354","11","Local Infrastructure Gap Bridging Initiative","bridge critical gaps in local infrastructure and other development requirements that are not being adequately met through existing inflows;",""],[1,"354","15","District Component of the BRGF and RSVY","The BRGF District Component subsumed the ongoing RSVY.","Ministry of Panchayati Raj (MoPR)"],[1,"354","16","Bank Transfer Mechanism for Local Government Funds","States were requested to adopt the mechanism of bank transfer to local govern- ments through core banking arrangements.","MoPR"],[1,"354","17","Participative Panchayat Infrastructure Planning under BRGF","Under the BRGF, for bridging identified critical gaps in infrastructure, participative plans are required to be prepared by each Panchayat",""],[1,"355","1","District Plan Consolidation Mechanism","These plans, which should take into account all development inflows into the area, including those other than BRGF, are then to be consolidated into district plans by the DPCs.",""],[1,"355","2","District Untied Fund Infrastructure Gap-Filling Works","the untied fund allocated to the districts are generally being used for filling infrastructure gaps in drinking water, health, education, social sectors, electrification, and so on.","Ministry"],[1,"355","3","Rolling Plans and Revolving Funds Mechanism","The Ministry has adopted the principle of 'Rolling Plans and Revolving Funds' under which incomplete works of a year are to be included in Action Plan for the next year.","The Ministry"],[1,"357","11","Integrated Action Plan District Fund and Governance Mechanism","In furtherance of these principles, the funds under the scheme are placed at the disposal of a committee headed by the District Collector and consisting of the Superintendent of Police of the district and the District Forest Officer.",""],[1,"358","1","Integrated Action Plan for District-Level Public Infrastructure and Services","The district-level committee has to draw up a Plan consisting of concrete proposals for public infrastructure and services such as School Buildings, Anganwadi Centres, Primary Health Centres (PHCs), Drinking Water Supply, Village Roads, Electric Lights in public places such as PHCs and schools, and so on.",""],[1,"358","3","Empowered Group for IAP District Scheme Implementation","the GOI has also been focusing on channelising funds from existing programmes, coupled with better monitoring, to ensure the effective implementation of all-India schemes in the IAP districts.","Planning Commission | Ministry of Rural Development | MoPR | Ministry of Tribal Welfare | Ministry of Home affairs"],[1,"358","8","BRGF Fund Release and District Planning Reform","Changes have been made to ensure quick release of fund from State to the local bodies under BRGF.",""],[1,"359","6","Project Implementation Monitoring and Review Mechanism","The progress of implementation is monitored by the Planning Commission and National Rainfed Area Authority (NRAA).",""],[1,"362","4","Participatory Approach to BRGF","The new approach to the BRGF during the Twelfth Plan seeks",""],[1,"362","6","BRGF Capacity Building and Bottom-Up District Planning","The support being provided in the Twelfth Plan through this BRGF window for these districts would be used in building capacities and developing and implementing Plans in a bottom-up and participatory manner.",""],[1,"363","1","District Absorptive Capacity Support and Performance-Based Funding","This would improve the absorptive capacities of these districts for outlays provided under various schemes and also for the use of additional funds to be provided to those districts that are able to move in the direction specified in the next two years of the Plan.",""],[1,"363","1","District Progress Monitoring Against Planning Commission Indicators","This progress would be monitored against the list of indicators developed by the Planning Commission and additional funds provided in the next two years to those districts which show progress against these indicators.",""],[1,"363","3","Restructured BRGF with Multi-Level Territorial Focus","The restructured BRGF, therefore, seeks to make a special emphasis on the sub-district level.",""],[1,"363","4","Restructured Backward Regions Grant Fund","Thus, the restructured BRGF will focus on all three levels: district, sub-district and supra- district.",""],[1,"363","4","Planning Commission Oversight of Special Plans and Supra-district Components","Bihar and West Bengal Special Plans, as also the Supra-district Components of the KBK and Bundelkhand packages, will continue to be overseen by the Planning Commission.","Planning Commission"],[1,"363","5","BRGF Area Selection Based on Census 2011 Data","The Twelfth Plan proposes that we rely only on the relatively unimpeachable data made available through Census, 2011, for selection of",""],[1,"363","14","Restructured BRGF and RGPSA Launch","Even as we restructure the BRGF, a concomi- tant initiative critical for the success of the new BRGF, as shown by all evaluations of the programme, is also being launched by the MoPR in the Twelfth Plan.","MoPR"]],"138":[[1,"356","2","Special Plan for Bihar","The Special Plan has been formulated to bring about improvement in sectors such as power, road connectivity, irrigation, forestry and watershed development.",""],[1,"356","3","Special Plan Funding and Administration","The Planning Commission is administering the Special Plan and funds are being released on a 100 per cent grant basis.",""],[1,"356","4","Continuation of the Special Plan for Bihar","Special Plan for Bihar needs to continue as infrastructure and development gap is still quite high between Bihar and other States, and there is some obligation arising from the preamble to the Bihar Re-organization Act.",""],[1,"359","5","Special Drinking Water Package for UP and MP","additional allocations through an ACA (including ₹100 crore each for UP and MP to provide drink- ing water in the region, approved on 19 May 2011).",""],[1,"359","7","ACA Flow Expansion","There is need to continue this and step up flow of ACA.",""],[1,"359","8","Special Plan for West Bengal","Additional Central Assistance is being provided for the Special Plan for West Bengal to address the development needs of the backward regions of the State through focused projects in the year 2011- 2012 and during the Twelfth Plan period.",""]],"139":[[1,"361","1","Border Area Development Programme","Since then the BADP has been implemented by the GOI together with State Governments as part of a comprehensive approach to border management.","GOI | State Governments"],[1,"361","5","Flexible Scheme Implementation through Local Institutions and Communities","flexibility in implementation has been supported by the revised guidelines, which enable the involvement of local governments, communities, non-governmental organisations (NGOs) and SHGs that do not receiving foreign aid assistance, for executing schemes.","Ministry of Home Affairs"],[1,"361","5","Community Cost-Sharing for Social Infrastructure","State Governments are encouraged to involve the community in sharing of 10 per cent to 15 per cent of the cost of social infra- structure wherever possible.",""],[1,"361","5","BADP Security-Related Works Allocation","Security-related works can also be taken up under BADP to the extent of 10 per cent of the total allocation in a particular year.",""],[1,"361","6","Participatory Border Village and Block Planning","Participatory plans for border vil- lages and blocks should be prepared based on the instructions of the Planning Commission on the for- mulation of district plans.",""],[1,"361","6","Baseline Surveys for Border Block Planning","The preparation of plans will be preceded by baseline surveys in all villages in the border blocks to assess gaps in physical and social infrastructure.",""],[1,"361","6","Convergence of Centrally Sponsored and State Plan Schemes","The district plans would help ensure convergence/dove- tailing of CSS with BADP.",""]],"140":[[1,"358","9","Electrification Projects in IAP Districts","For effective implementation of electrification projects in 60 IAP districts, Empowered Group had approached the Ministry of Power to relax the conditions in the Decentralized Distributed Generation (DDG) guidelines relating to non- availability of grid for implementation of the scheme.","Ministry of Power"],[1,"359","1","Village Solar Charging Stations","Ministry of New and Renewable Energy proposes to provide solar charging station consisting of 50 LED solar lanterns with solar panels, and so on, in villages/habitations of the 60 IAP districts.","Ministry of New and Renewable Energy"],[1,"365","18","Major Hydro Power Projects","Major Hydro project-Lower Subansiri (2,000 MW), Pare, Kameng, Dibang.",""]],"141":[[1,"369","7","Post-Harvest Management and Marketing Infrastructure Development","Post-harvest management and marketing infra- structure required to be attended to by dovetail- ing of programmes/schemes between Central Ministries and the State Governments for filling up gaps in infrastructure.","Central Ministries | State Governments"],[1,"369","8","Employment Opportunity Creation","There has to be continued emphasis on creation of employment opportunities.",""],[1,"369","8","Continuation of Development Momentum with Central Support","This has to continue with all possible support from the Centre.",""],[1,"369","9","Review of the North-East Industrial Policy Incentive Package","there is also a demand for a review of the incentive package under the policy which may be looked into during the Twelfth plan for creating enabling environment for investors and rational use of local resources.","Department of Industrial Policy and Promotion"],[1,"369","9","Industry Association and Bank Consultation Meetings","Meetings between Industry associations and banks would be of helpful in understanding the associated problems related to industries for suitable incorporation in the modified policy.",""],[1,"369","11","Teacher Training and Evaluation Investment","Investment in teachers' training and evaluation.",""],[1,"369","11","Distance Education Infrastructure for the North-East","Use distance education infrastructure for quick completion in the North-East.",""],[1,"369","12","Education Capacity Building and Skills Development","Special emphasis on capac- ity building and skill development with focus on curriculum is needed.",""]],"142":[[1,"370","2","Bangladesh Connectivity Enhancement Measures","Focus on strong relationship with Bangladesh to ensure effective connectivity by different trans- port modes.",""],[1,"370","3","Tri-lateral Highway Connectivity Project","Connecting the NE by road to south-east (SE) Asia through Tri-lateral Highway-Moreh (Manipur)-Mandalay/Bagan (Myanmar)-Mae Sot/Chiang Mai (Thailand).",""],[1,"370","4","Land Customs Stations Development Programme","Focus on development of all Land Customs Stations (LCS) for strengthening border trade and business communication.",""],[1,"370","5","Kaladan River Multimodal Transport Connectivity","Expeditious implementation of multi-modal transport using Kaladan River as alternate con- nectivity to the North-East.",""]],"143":[[1,"370","6","Separate Financial Arrangement for Asset Maintenance","Maintenance of assets, especially roads, is an important aspect and needs a separate financial arrangement.",""]]}});